# Cyrius Field Notes — Compiler Internal Gotchas
# Non-obvious traps an agent will hit when working on cyrius's
# compiler internals: regalloc, layout, alignment, IR ordering,
# heap caps, fn-table conventions, codegen-vs-IR divergence.
#
# Earn their slot when an agent is debugging a cyrius
# regression that doesn't fit a clean pattern. The shape is
# always "this surprised future-me; here's the diagnosis +
# fix + how to recognize it again."
#
# For process methodology see methodology.cyml.
# For design patterns see patterns.cyml.
# For full backstory + chronological context see retros/.
#
# 84 entries (v6.4.46–.48 docs-sync: +2 gotchas, 1 retired-in-place, 4 marked resolved)
# +shift_right_split_gtgt_stays_logical_gtgtgt_is_arithmetic_reverse_of_js_never_flip_gtgt (v6.4.46 — >> MUST stay
#   logical: crypto rotates + ISUNION()=fcount>>63 depend on it; signed-shift goes through the NEW >>> operator)
# +cyrius_auto_calls_main_top_level_var_main_double_runs_and_the_execve_helper_dispatch_pattern (v6.4.47 —
#   auto-main double-run, args_init-first, the cyrsign-efi execve helper-dispatch, argv i64[16] slots-not-bytes)
# ~cx_bytecode_backend_..._cli_exposed: the "cx has ZERO CLI surface" premise RETIRED — cx is CLI-exposed
#   (--target=cx, cxvm installed) since v6.4.17–.22; entry flipped to record the closed gap + evergreen lesson.
# ~4 stale caveats marked RESOLVED: macho socket-family (v6.2.24), callptr Win64-COM frame corruption (v6.0.86),
#   PE capturing closures (v6.4.26), value-form SIMD params/returns on Win64 (v6.4.31).
# 82 entries (v6.4.44, Win64 retptr deep-stack-param homing: +1 gotcha
# win64_retptr_shifted_deep_stack_param_homing_offbyone_prologue_postloop_missing_pp_shift_f32v8_make_lanes_567
# — a value-form-SIMD/struct-returning fn on Win64 PE (hidden retptr in RCX) with >6 params homed its
# DEEP stack params (pidx>=6, the post-loop at parse_fn.cyr ~3404) one local slot too deep and dropped
# the last, because the post-loop used the param index as BOTH source ABI slot AND dest local slot with
# NO retptr shift — while the IN-loop homing (~3321) DOES apply `_pp_shift` and the body reads the
# contiguous -0x30-lidx*8 slots via FINDLOCAL. f32v8_make(a..h) lanes 5,6,7 = garbage on cass, 0-4 OK;
# Linux/SysV fine (no retptr for SIMD register returns, so _pl_shift==0 == old code == byte-id). Fix:
# mirror _pp_shift in the post-loop (pidx=spi+shift src, lidx=spi dest, start spi=6-shift). Pre-existing
# since v6.4.31; latent bc vr01_f32v8_ctor's stale "skips cass" comment masked that CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_
# PARAMS went true on PE at .31. Root-caused by disassembling caller (correct) vs callee prologue (skew).)
# 81 entries (v6.4.44, PE reroute 16-align: +1 gotcha
# pe_kernel32_reroutes_fixed_sub_rsp_only_aligns_on_right_parity_closehandle_faults_on_misalign_wine_cannot_repro
# — 8 PE kernel32 reroutes (ECLOSE_PE/EWAIT/EGETEXIT/ESETHANDLE/ECREATEPIPE/EGETCMDLINE/EGETTICKS/EMMAP)
# used a FIXED `sub rsp,0x28` that only 16-aligns the call when the caller's rsp lands on the right
# parity; CloseHandle faults 0xC0000005 on an aligned SSE access inside kernel32 when misaligned.
# Exposed by the async subprocess resume (deep call stack): 2 sequential async_run_process crashed,
# single+batched worked (parity-dependent). CreateProcessW was fixed this way at v6.0.51 but its comment
# wrongly generalized "≤4-arg reroutes tolerate a misalign". Fix: route through the rbx-anchored
# _pe_call_Narg_aligned / _pe_srw_call_1arg helpers. WINE aligns leniently → returned 42 throughout;
# only real Windows repros. WriteFile-trace of _async_step localized it after ckA/ckR/ckG bisection.)
# 79 entries (v6.4.32, cx SIMD codegen: +1 gotcha
# cx_simd_emitters_are_easy_the_real_bugs_are_local_addressing_eloadlocaladdr_stub_and_regalloc_disp_nonuniformity
# — cx SIMD emitters mirror the f64 loop trivially; the real blockers are 2 pre-existing cx
# local-addressing bugs the SIMD pointer-stash exposes (ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR return-0 stub → &local
# aliases addr 0; scalar load/store disp omitted the regalloc/ret_stash reservation EFLADDR had →
# stash collides with arrays; unify via _cx_ldisp). cxvm >> is LOGICAL (dp8 sign-ext = b−((b>>7)<<8)).
# git-stash-rebuild-vs-baseline proves regression-vs-pre-existing; end cx gates with `return assert_summary()`.)
# 78 entries (v6.4.28, SIMD Phase 5 f32v4 NEON: +1 gotcha
# aarch64_neon_simd_emitter_mirror_f64v_loop_verify_encodings_with_llvm_mc_and_value_form_params_die_on_win64
# — a new aarch64 NEON SIMD emitter (EMIT_F32V_*) mirrors the existing EMIT_F64V_LOOP/DOT (change
# .2d→.4s, lsl#3→lsl#2, +2→+4); verify every hand-encoded word with `llvm-mc --show-encoding`;
# match x86 rounding (fmul+fadd, NOT fused fmla) for fmadd/dot; DON'T make a value-form SIMD test a
# full vr01_ — value-form f32v4 params hard-error on Win64 PE, so gate the flat-array subset instead.)
# 77 entries (v6.4.27, agnos O_RDWR flag-map: +1 gotcha
# agnos_ao_access_mode_bits_are_1to1_with_linux_o_flags_pass_through_dont_fold_verify_under_mirshi
# — agnos AO_RDONLY/WRONLY/RDWR = 0/1/2 == Linux O_*; io.cyr:80 folded (flags&3)!=0 → |0x1 so
# O_RDWR became write-only and reads failed on agnos ONLY; fold passed compile+Linux, only mirshi
# read-after-write caught it; fix `ao |= flags&3`; the sakshi.cyr dup is a fold → fix at source.)
# 76 entries (v6.4.26, Windows PE batch: +1 gotcha
# new_pe_reroute_fn_needs_return0_stubs_in_aarch64_and_cx_backends_for_symbol_resolution
# — a new E*_PE reroute fn (real impl in backend/x86/emit.cyr) called from parse_expr.cyr is
# referenced by ALL forks; aarch64/cx/macho backends need a `fn X_PE(S){return 0;}` stub or
# the cx/aarch64 cycc build fails "undefined function" — invisible to local Linux+wine tests
# (both include the x86 backend); ONLY the cross-OS gate's cycc_cx build on cass catches it.)
# 75 entries (v6.4.25, aarch64 exp2/atan + Payne-Hanek: +1 gotcha
# aarch64_transcendental_polyfill_dispatch_and_dekker_dd_range_reduction
# — aarch64 has no native exp2/atan/trig; EF64_* route to lib/math.cyr _f64_*_polyfill via
# _FINDFN_CSTR; large-|x| trig needs a NO-FMA Dekker double-double reduction, gated so the
# small-angle path stays byte-identical; lib/math.cyr is NOT in cycc so x86 stays byte-id.)
# 74 entries (v6.4.24, P1 struct-field: +1 gotcha
# struct_field_access_mis_parsed_as_enum_variant_when_field_name_shadows_a_global_var
# — X.Y disambiguation loaded a global whenever the FIELD name was a global var, ignoring
# base X; fix = if X is a known var, X.Y is a field access (enum-variant base is a TYPE).)
# 73 entries (v6.4.21, LEXID cap: +1 gotcha
# heap_region_shared_by_all_forks_must_relocate_to_arena_top_because_fork_arenas_diverge
# — growing a fixed-offset heap region a SHARED file touches means relocating to the
# arena top + extending ALL 7 forks' arenas (they map DIFFERENT sizes: x86/win 0x7300000
# with the IR arena, aarch64/macho/cx only 0x5E1D000); lazy mmap makes the extra range free.)
# 72 entries (v6.4.20, cx arc C: +1 gotcha
# cxvm_guest_syscall_is_compiler_renumbered_at_runtime_fix_pointer_slot_and_pe_arity
# — a portable .cyx runs cross-OS via a cxvm-ONLY fix: the compiler already
# renumbers a runtime var syscall number per-host (ESYSXLAT/EMACHO), so the bugs
# were the open pointer-slot (s2 not s3) + the Windows argc-6 arity-bucket miss
# (→ arity-correct dispatch); cycc_cx cross-compiles clean but faults on macOS.)
# 71 entries (v6.4.10, 6.4.x SIMD arc: +2 gotchas from the v6.4.4-.10
# SIMD-compute-arc + fixes work — vex_encoding_vvvv_ones_complement_and_fma_cpuid_leaf
# (the FIRST VEX/AVX the toolchain emits: the VEX vvvv field is the 1's-COMPLEMENT
# of src1 → a wrong-polarity nibble assembles VALID but reads the WRONG register,
# invisible to self-host → disasm-gate every new VEX op; FMA is CPUID leaf 1 ECX.12,
# a DIFFERENT leaf+bit than AVX2's leaf 7 EBX.5) and
# two_step_bootstrap_for_toplevel_array_sizing_and_legit_codegen_diff (a change to
# how cycc sizes its OWN top-level arrays is a TWO-STEP bootstrap, same class as
# W^X-flip; and a real bug fix SHOULD show differential codegen-diff>0 WITH
# status-diff=0 — codegen-diff=0 on a behavior fix means it didn't fire).)
# 68 entries (v6.4.2, 6.4.x: +2 gotchas from the v6.4.0/.1 generics-flip +
# allocator-hardening work — monomorph_default_on_flip_gftp_decouple
# (CYRIUS_MONOMORPH default-on needed GFTP(S,fi)!=0 as the precise per-fn
# signal + decoupling from _INLINE_OK to avoid a +25.6% general-inlining
# regression; generics are now DEFAULT-ON, CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0 opts out) and
# alloc_reset_zero_on_reset_chunk_model_and_lib_fix_not_auto_byte_identical
# (zero-on-reset info-leak fix; _heap_used is cumulative so a naive memset
# over-runs the first chunk; a lib fix is NOT auto cycc-byte-identical when
# cycc includes the lib).)
# 64 entries (v6.3.x closeout, 6.3.44: +4 durable gotchas from the .43/.44
# cross-OS + stdlib correctness sweep — macho_syscall_xlat_source_numbers_arch_specific
# (getpid returned GARBAGE on aarch64-macOS behind a callable-only smoke),
# emit_gvar_inits_parallel_copy_of_parse_struct_init (a field-shape fix must
# land in BOTH the in-fn and global-scope struct-init loops),
# stdlib_mutex_single_owner_sync_cyr (+ module-qualified api-surface snapshot),
# cross_os_self_host_real_hardware_non_negotiable.)
# 60 entries (6.3.10 shipped non-i64 generics — the instantiate-once engine
# RE-INVOKES PARSE_FN_DEF / PARSE_STRUCT_DEF on the base's verbatim tokens with name +
# concrete-binding overrides (full fidelity for free); gotcha = the def-start token
# index is stored +1 because token 0 is valid. See generic_instantiate_once_reinvoke.
# 6.3.9 shipped i64 generic FUNCTIONS — the base fn IS the i64
# monomorphization (body emitted with T->i64); the load-bearing gotcha was the
# `: T` return triggering the retptr/stash ABI in the return-type rough-scan —
# see generic_fn_i64_base_and_ret_stash. 6.3.7 made non-capturing closures `|x| body` actually work — they
# were a scaffolded-but-untested, non-functional feature. FOUR codegen bugs, all
# from the closure being an anonymous fn emitted INLINE in the enclosing fn while
# sharing its per-fn state + local tables: (1) it inherited `_cur_fn_regalloc` →
# params stored past phantom callee-saved slots + read from stale regs; (2) its
# frame size was never EPATCHFRAME'd; (3) it reused the shared local-slot tables
# from slot 0, clobbering the enclosing fn's locals; (4) the enclosing fn's
# regalloc byte-scan post-pass rewrote the inline closure code → SIGILL. See the
# closure_inline_emit_shares_enclosing_fn_state entry. 6.3.8 added lexical capture
# by value — see closure_lexical_capture_by_value_env_object.)
# 56 entries (6.3.5 added the pass-1 fn-signature prescan (CO-01) + monomorph
# token-replay substrate (AR-01): the 3-pass driver structure (register / emit-fn-
# bodies / emit-top-level) means the forward-call ABI bug only bites IN-FN calls;
# pass-1 stops at the first top-level statement so the prescan covers decls-first;
# `_prescan_params` must consume `)` or `: Struct` returns never classify; the
# inline replay emit path was always intact — only the capture was gated off. See
# the forward_call_prescan_and_monomorph_substrate entry.)
# 55 entries (6.2.51 added the modular-resolver CVE-32 + `mod`-keyword trap;
# 6.2.35 added, undefined FUNCTIONS compile to a `ud2`/SIGILL stub +
# a WARNING (exit 0, valid binary) but undefined CONSTANTS/enum-values are a HARD
# error — the function-vs-constant asymmetry. So a stdlib fn call that resolves to
# nothing on a target compiles GREEN and traps only at runtime; a missing enum
# const fails the build loudly. Bit the M6 chain on agnos: lib/sync.cyr had no
# CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS mutex backend, so patra's mutex_new/lock/unlock were
# silent ud2 stubs (patra_init's unconditional mutex_new → runtime SIGILL), while
# the filed "add inert SYS_FUTEX constants" fix was dead. A "does it compile"
# gate PASSES this regression — assert symbols are DEFINED (grep the warning). See
# the undefined_fn_ud2_stub_vs_undefined_const_hard_error entry.)
# 49 entries (6.2.33 added, CYRIUS_ARCH_X86 is predefined on EVERY x86 build —
# AGNOS included — so a stdlib file guarded ONLY on `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` /
# `CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` silently takes the Linux branch on the agnos target and
# calls Linux syscall numbers (sakshi: open#2/close#3/exit#60 = agnos
# getpid/spawn/undefined → silent log corruption, compiles clean). The agnos
# branch must `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` FIRST + wrap the arch branches in
# `#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`. Nested `#ifdef` works (4-deep verified); `#else`
# does not. See the cyrius_arch_x86_predefined_even_on_agnos entry.)
# 48 entries (6.2.0 added, Phase-0 growable pressure tables: the GROW / REHASH /
# RELOCATION paths (fn-table 16-table realloc + fn_name_hash rehash; codebuf
# off-heap relocate) are SELF-HOST-INVISIBLE — cycc's 6486 fns / ~1 MB codebuf
# never reach the 8192 / 3 MB caps, so self-host + check.sh never exercise them;
# only a forced-grow test (shrink _fnt_cap/_codebuf_cap to 64/8192, self-compile,
# cmp vs the no-grow reference) catches relocation/rehash bugs. Also: the 8192
# fn-cap was a magic number scattered across 6 subsystems + colliding TEXTUALLY
# with 5 unrelated 8192-capped tables — the migration needed surgical per-context
# swaps (& 8191 → mask, probe/clear bounds → cap, live[1024] DCE bitmap → [4096]),
# never a global replace. See the two phase0_growable_* entries.)
# 44 entries (6.1.40 added, CVE-24: the four per-fn local tables
# (fn_local_names/depths/types/slice) are indexed by SFLC, which counts stack-frame
# SLOTS not vars — a `stack var buf[N]` registers N/8 fillers — so frames >~2 KB
# silently overran the old 256/576-entry tables; relocated all four to the heap top
# (0x5D9D000/0x5DBD000/0x5DDD000/0x5DFD000) at 16384 slots each + a loud SFLC cap.
# Plus the local-vs-global array convention: local `var a[N]` = N BYTES rounded to
# 8; global = N i64 SLOTS — the footgun behind the daimon report.)
# 43 entries (6.1.18 added: adding a PUBLIC stdlib fn (a top-level `module::fn` in
# lib/) makes check.sh's api-surface gate fail cryptically — `case-FAIL: test3
# addition count not reported as 1` — because docs/api-surface.snapshot is now
# stale; regenerate it: rebuild build/cyrius_api_surface from source then
# `build/cyrius_api_surface --update`. Underscore-private fns + src/backend
# internals are NOT tracked, so only intended public-surface adds show. Also: a
# new lib/<x>_win.cyr should be SELF-CONTAINED (own widen/narrow) — coupling it to
# _args_w2u8/_w_append_cstr forces every Windows consumer of the parent module to
# include args.cyr/process.cyr, FATAL under --strict.)
# 42 entries (6.1.17 added: a NEW PE syscall-reroute emitter `E*_PE` must get an
# aarch64 `return 0` stub in backend/aarch64/emit.cyr — parse_expr.cyr references
# it unconditionally inside the statically-dead `_TARGET_PE==1` block, and a
# missing stub fires "undefined function" which is FATAL under `--strict` on
# aarch64 (cx tolerates the whole cohort un-stubbed, aarch64 does NOT). The build
# passes WITHOUT the stub if you forget `--strict` — the warning is the only tell.)
# 41 entries (6.1.12 added: a large fn-local `var[]` lands in `.bss` static, NOT
# the stack — and a `var[]` in a statically-dead region after a `#ifdef`-gated
# return STILL reserves its `.bss`; diagnose via `objdump -h` section deltas, not
# `sub rsp` scanning. The agnos getenv #PF was `.bss`-image/loader-mapping, not
# stack overflow as the issue assumed.)
# 40 entries (.88-.90 added: x86-Mach-O can't compile byte-array literals — a
# parser miscompile INVISIBLE to self-host since cycc uses no byte arrays (verify
# pre-existing by stashing to the released tag's source on the target); the
# byte-array peephole must hoist &var via the obj-aware _EVRCX, NOT absolute-only
# EVADDR_X1, or obj/PIE byte arrays regress; _emit_fmt must test Mach-O before PE
# to match the old EMITELF ladder for the unguarded CYRIUS_MACHO+CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN
# combo. Plus the zsh-no-word-split test-harness trap.)
# 37 entries (.87 added: getenv on agnos walks the exec-stack envp from
# _agnos_init_rsp (no /proc); the agnos kernel is LOCALLY TESTABLE — rebuild
# with agnos/scripts/build.sh + boot under QEMU via agnsh-smoke.sh/exec-smoke.sh
# and run a ring-3 cyrius binary. Don't claim "no agnos to test on".)
# 36 entries (.86 added: callptr callee-spill frame slot can ALIAS a &-taken
# local — v6.0.70 spilled the indirect-call target to a GFLC frame slot; a COM
# out-param `&local` could land on the same slot → frame/regalloc corruption →
# AV (DXGI GetDesc1 on cass). Fix: PE callptr callee on the STACK + `call rax`,
# no frame slot. Found with cdb on cass (C:\dbg\cdb.exe).
# 35 entries (.85 added: Windows install pillar — no /proc env on Windows (read
# via GetEnvironmentVariableA PE reroute 0xF015); wrapper compile()/run_binary
# are POSIX fork/execve, Windows needs cmd.exe-redirect spawn via CreateProcessW
# (cmd /s /c strips only the outer quote pair); .ps1 must be ASCII (PS 5.1
# mis-decodes UTF-8 em-dashes); the macho-arm/x86 dual-number trap applies to PE
# reroute numbers too. cyrius build now works on Windows.
# 34 entries (.84 added: macOS owns the thread-pointer register — Darwin
# saves/restores TPIDR_EL0 (arm64) / %fs (x86) as thread state and overwrites
# any userspace install on the FIRST preemption, so cyrius can't durably own it
# for thread-local storage (sigil crypto-scratch crash); use a process-global
# on macOS. + socketpair dual-number: arm-macho=199 (aarch64-Linux enum),
# x86-macho=53 — and 53 is fchmodat on the arm enum, so don't reuse it.
# 33 entries (.70 added: callptr/IR_CALL_INDIRECT — compiler-emitted indirect
# calls; the callee-survival crux (frame-slot spill, NOT pop-after-ECALLPOPS
# which the Win64 shadow buries); aarch64 uses x6; top-level/cx = loud error;
# open real-Win64-COM-callee corruption → .71.
# 32 entries (.69 added: PE multi-DLL imports — generalize the single-kernel32
# import descriptor to N DLLs (shell32/dxgi); the flat ftype=4 fixup forces an
# imp_idx→physical-slot remap; design byte-identical at one DLL; x86-only PE
# emit fns need aarch64 no-op stubs (FATAL under --strict).
# .68 added: aarch64-Linux ESYSXLAT gaps — stat/rename/symlink
# literals hit the wrong aarch64 syscall (§D1 deps + build + pulsar); the
# native entry file diverged from the cross (missing auto-call-main); and the
# CI gate tested the cross binary not the shipped native one (placebo).
# .67 added: param_load(reg,idx) asm pseudo + the bootstrap-coupling
# lesson — migrating bootstrap-critical libs to a new compiler feature.
# .66 added: cyrius test on Apple Silicon — qemu mis-route (127)
# + unsigned AMFI SIGKILL (137); exec native + _macho_codesign in run_binary.
# .65 added: portable sleep — no raw nanosleep(35) off Linux;
# poll-as-sleep + Windows Sleep reroute; macho 35 collides with unlinkat.
# .64 added: aarch64 atomic-on-global needs 8-aligned
# var base; aarch64 atomic_cas is not a full barrier — fence the lock.
# .63 added: macho syscall dual-definition 61-vs-217;
# Mach-O output-basename breaks reproducible cmp).

[[entries]]
name = "cx_bytecode_backend_is_cli_exposed_check_backend_dirs_before_declaring_a_feature_absent"
description = "The cyrius-x (cx) bytecode backend is a REAL, self-hosting, CLI-EXPOSED target: `cyrius build --target=cx` emits a portable `.cyx`, `cyrius run` executes it through the installed cxvm, and programs/checks/cx.cyr gates the whole path end-to-end in the green check.sh. Its last-mile CLI exposure landed in the v6.4.17-.22 arc (Release A CLI wiring .17, f64 arith .18-.19, cross-OS `.cyx` .20) with float/SIMD codegen following (.32); cxvm now installs via the `[bins]` loop in scripts/install.sh (`cbt/cyrius.cyr` accepts `--target=cx`, `cbt/core.cyr` routes it). HISTORICAL (through ~v6.4.16): the backend was fully built + gated but had NO CLI verb — no `--target=cx`, cxvm built only ad-hoc inside the gate, `cyrius run` ran native code — so a grep for `wasm`/`--target=cx` returned nothing and it was easy to wrongly conclude 'we have no bytecode/WASM target' (a user premise-check 2026-07-05 caught exactly that). That gap is now CLOSED — the shape mirrors the TS->JS gap before `--emit-js`/`--target=js` wired it in at v6.1.12. LESSON (evergreen): 'grep for the literal string returns nothing' is NOT 'the work doesn't exist' — a backend can be fully built and gated yet invisible because it has no CLI verb; check git history + src/backend/ dirs + the checks/ gates before declaring a feature absent."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "struct_field_access_mis_parsed_as_enum_variant_when_field_name_shadows_a_global_var"
description = "v6.4.24 (P1): `bc.exit_code` silently read the WRONG value (a global, not the struct field) when the field name also happened to be a top-level var. Root cause in parse_expr.cyr's `X.Y` disambiguation (enum-variant vs struct-field): it decided 'enum-variant' by checking whether `FINDVAR(Y) >= 0` — i.e. whether the FIELD NAME Y is a known global var — and if so loaded that global, IGNORING the base X entirely (`Enum.VARIANT` semantics = 'load the global const VARIANT, ignore the enum-type prefix'). So `struct_var.field` where `field` shadowed a top-level var loaded the global instead of the field. SILENT wrong value (no error/crash). LESSON 1 (the fix): the base of an enum-variant access is a bare ENUM TYPE NAME, never a variable — so if X is a known local/global var (FINDLOCAL/FINDVAR), `X.Y` is a FIELD access, full stop; guard the enum-variant branch on `X` not being a var. Genuine `Enum.VARIANT` (base is a type) is preserved. cycc byte-identical (its own source has no field-shadows-global pattern). LESSON 2 (why it fooled everyone): it looked 'size-dependent / only in a large unit' and got FILED as a two-struct field-name/offset collision — but it's neither a two-struct collision (renaming the other struct's field doesn't help) nor a buffer overflow (token/name buffers well under cap). It only bites when the field name ALSO happens to be a top-level var, so small repros pass; a large consumer (stiva's `var exit_code = main()`) has such a var. The consumer's rename-dodge (exit_code→exit_status) worked only because exit_status wasn't also a top-level var. LESSON 3 (method): when a filing's hypothesis is wrong and it won't repro minimally, instrument cycc — a CYRIUS_DUMP_PP env-guarded dump of preprocess_out (S+0x459D000, GBL bytes) + a token-window dump in ERR (tok_types@0x2D7C000 / tok_values@0x357C000 / tok_names@0x60000, walk back from GTI) + a filtered field-load trace pinpointed it in the live unit. Also: reproduce by FULLY reverting the consumer's dodge (a PARTIAL revert — field renamed but accessors not — produced a self-inflicted red-herring 'unexpected ,' from FINDFIELD<0→ERR, wasting a cycle)."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "heap_region_shared_by_all_forks_must_relocate_to_arena_top_because_fork_arenas_diverge"
description = "v6.4.21 (LEXID cap 16384→65536): growing a fixed-offset heap region that lex.cyr/parse*.cyr (SHARED across all 7 main_*.cyr forks) accesses is NOT a one-line bump — two traps. (1) BOXED-IN: `lexid_entries` (16384×8B @ 0x457C900) had `preprocess_out` (8 MB) starting 1792 bytes later, so it can't grow in place; it must RELOCATE. (2) FORK ARENA DIVERGENCE: the 7 forks map DIFFERENT arena sizes — x86-Linux (main.cyr) + Windows (main_win.cyr) map 0x7300000 (they include the IR node/cp arena 0x5E9D000..0x729D000, x86-only under CYRIUS_IR); the aarch64/macho/cx forks (main_aarch64{,_native,_macho}.cyr, main_x86_macho.cyr, main_cx.cyr) map only 0x5E1D000 (no IR arena). A region accessed by a SHARED file must live at ONE fixed offset common to ALL forks — and there is NO free common region above the packed 0..0x5E1D000 (the x86 IR arena occupies 0x5E1D000..0x729D000, which the small forks don't even map). SOLUTION (the ir_nodes v6.3.28 pattern): relocate the region to a fresh offset ABOVE the highest fork's top (0x7300000, past ir_cp) and extend ALL 7 forks' arenas to cover it (mmap size in main.cyr:693/main_win.cyr:351/the two macho forks; SYS_BRK in main.cyr:706/main_aarch64.cyr/main_aarch64_native.cyr/main_cx.cyr). KEY: on the small forks the range 0x5E1D000..0x7300000 (~25 MB) is now mapped-but-unused — that costs NOTHING because anonymous mmap/brk is LAZY (physical pages only on touch). So don't fear the 'waste'. This is a LAYOUT change → TWO-STEP bootstrap; but it's cycc-BYTE-IDENTICAL (the relocated offset + cap are same-width immediates) and differential-diff=0 (it's cycc's RUNTIME heap, not codegen). Verify: cross-OS self-host on ALL of ecb/cass/pi (the arena extension is the exact thing that faults on a fork if you missed one) + a synthetic >old-cap-identifier program that fails on old cycc and compiles on new (proves the relocated region works, not just that it links). The var-family tables (_vars_base etc.) can't hold a per-var FLAG because fixup.cyr SUMS var_size for the global data-section layout (a negative/encoded size corrupts it on all backends) — see the signed-global-sign-ext issue for why signedness needs its own 8th table."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "cxvm_guest_syscall_is_compiler_renumbered_at_runtime_fix_pointer_slot_and_pe_arity"
description = "cx Release C (v6.4.20): making a portable .cyx do I/O on all 4 hosts is a cxvm-ONLY fix — the guest syscall number does NOT need a translation table in cxvm, because the compiler already renumbers it per-host AT RUNTIME. cxvm's 0x70 handler does `syscall(sn, ...)` with a VAR sn (the guest's x86-Linux number). On aarch64 `ESYSXLAT` (src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr) and on macOS `EMACHO_SYSXLAT` emit a runtime `cmp x8,#N / b.ne / movz x8,#M` chain BEFORE svc — so whatever runtime value is in the number register gets renumbered. Empirically: the PRE-fix cxvm cross-compiled to aarch64/macho already ran a write+exit .cyx unchanged. Adding a `_cx_hostsys` renumber table in cxvm (the scoping workflow's proposed design) would DOUBLE-translate (guest 1→64, then 64→garbage). The two REAL bugs were: (1) POINTER-SLOT — the handler translated the guest virtual-offset→real-address on the wrong register for open: open(path,flags,mode) puts path in arg0=s2, but it fixed s3(=flags), so every open() EFAULTed on ALL hosts; read/write buf=s3 were correct. Fix keys on the GUEST number BEFORE renumber, shifts only the pointer reg (offset 0=NULL untouched). aarch64 open→openat's AT_FDCWD arg-shift is emitted by ESYSXLAT and composes automatically. (2) WINDOWS ARITY — cxvm issued a FIXED `syscall(sn,s2,s3,s4,s5,s6)` = argc 6. `EPE_SYSCALL_DYNAMIC` routes var-numbered PE syscalls BY ARITY (argc4→read/write/open/lseek, argc2→close/exit, argc7→mmap) and has NO argc-6 bucket → every guest write/read/open/close/exit hit _pe_dyn_nomatch → -ENOSYS (cass exit=-38, no output; verified defender-clean, NOT a quarantine). Fix: arity-correct dispatch — `if sn∈{0,1,2,8} syscall(sn,s2,s3,s4)` (argc4), `elif sn∈{3,60} syscall(sn,s2)` (argc2), `else` 5-arg passthrough. PE arity buckets now route to the SHIPPED EWRITE_PE/EREAD_PE/EOPEN_PE/ECLOSE_PE/ELSEEK_PE/EEXIT emitters (ZERO new backend code); Linux/macOS ignore extra args so exact-arity is equally correct → one dispatch, four hosts. cycc byte-identical (cxvm is a program, off the self-host/seed chain). SEPARATE catch: the cx COMPILER cycc_cx (src/main_cx.cyr) cross-compiles to Mach-O/PE CLEAN but FAULTS on macOS (signal 12; 2 syscalls ESYSXLAT doesn't route) — so cxvm ships to the tarballs but cycc_cx does NOT (issues/2026-07-07-cycc_cx-cross-native-macho-pe.md). LESSON: cross-compiling clean ≠ running clean — RUN the newly-shipped binary on the host; and when a scoping premise says 'add a translation table', check whether the compiler already translates at runtime before duplicating it."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "undefined_fn_ud2_stub_vs_undefined_const_hard_error"
description = "cyrius treats an unresolved FUNCTION call and an unresolved CONSTANT/enum-value asymmetrically: a call to a function defined nowhere compiles to a `ud2;ud2;nop` (SIGILL) stub at the call site + a `warning: undefined function` and STILL exits 0 with a valid binary (src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr overwrites the `e8` call placeholder with `0F 0B 0F 0B 90` when the fn target is the -1/-2 undef sentinel — deliberate, so it's a loud localisable SIGILL not a jump into low memory); but a reference to an undefined constant/enum value is a HARD compile error (`undefined variable '…'`). CYRIUS_STRICT=1 does NOT promote the fn warning. Consequence: a stdlib fn that resolves to nothing on some target ships GREEN and traps only at runtime. Surfaced v6.2.35 on agnos (lib/sync.cyr had no agnos mutex backend → patra's mutex_* were ud2 stubs → patra_init SIGILL), and explains why the M6-chain issue's 'add inert SYS_FUTEX constants' fix was both unnecessary AND a misdiagnosis (patra ≤1.11.x referenced SYS_FUTEX as a raw CONSTANT → real hard error; 1.12.x uses mutex_lock() a FUNCTION → silent ud2 → no error)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codegen", "fixup", "ud2", "undefined-function", "undefined-constant", "agnos", "cross-target", "silent-failure", "v6.2.35"]
content = """
**UPDATE v6.3.2 (2026-06-28) — the default FLIPPED.** A *reachable* undefined-fn call is now a
**HARD ERROR by default** (`error: refusing to emit binary with N reachable undefined function(s)`;
exit 1, no output). The check in `src/backend/{x86,aarch64}/fixup.cyr` gates on a new `_allow_undef`
global (default 0 = error) instead of `_strict_mode`. `--allow-undef` (CLI) / `--al` downgrades back
to the warn+ud2+exit-0 behavior described below. **Dead-host (DCE-unreachable) refs STAY
warn+ud2+exit-0** (filtered out of the reachable count). So the asymmetry table below now describes
(a) any undef under `--allow-undef`, or (b) a genuinely dead-host ref — NOT the default reachable
case, which is now symmetric with the constant case (both hard-error). The flip's repo-wide blast
radius (forks, cross-arch builds, aspirational tools — not just the tcyr corpus) was cleaned with
ZERO `--allow-undef` via stubs/guards; see project memory `project_v632_undef_hard_error`.

**The asymmetry** (pre-v6.3.2 default; now the `--allow-undef` / dead-host case):

| unresolved symbol | cycc behavior |
|---|---|
| **function call** | `warning: undefined function 'f'` + emit `ud2;ud2;nop` at the call site; **exit 0, valid binary** |
| **constant / enum value** | `error: undefined variable 'X'`; **exit 1, no output** |

So `return f(x)` where `f` is defined nowhere COMPILES (and SIGILLs at
runtime if reached); `return X` where `X` is an undefined enum value does
NOT. `CYRIUS_STRICT=1` does not change the function case.

**Why** (src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr ~170-194): for a function-call fixup
whose target is the undef sentinel (<0), cycc overwrites the 5-byte
`e8 XX XX XX XX` call placeholder with `0F 0B 0F 0B 90` = `ud2; ud2; nop`.
Intentional — a SIGILL at the exact call site beats the disp32 resolving
to an arbitrary low address (the sandhi M2 2026-04-24 jump-into-entry bug).
Runtime confirmation: such a binary exits 132 (128+SIGILL) at the call.

**Where it bit (v6.2.35, the M6 persistence chain on agnos):**
`lib/sync.cyr` defined `mutex_new/lock/unlock` only under
`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_{WIN,MACOS,LINUX}` — no agnos branch. patra includes
`sync.cyr` (not `thread.cyr`/`thread_agnos.cyr`), so on `--agnos` those
calls were undefined → ud2 stubs, compiling GREEN. `patra_init()` calls
`mutex_new()` UNCONDITIONALLY, so patra_init itself SIGILLs at runtime on
agnos. The filed fix ("add inert `SYS_FUTEX`/`FUTEX_*` to the agnos peer")
was a misdiagnosis: those constants are only referenced by stale patra
≤1.11.x's `_patra_lock`, which used `SYS_FUTEX` as a RAW CONSTANT (→ hard
error → that's the error the reporter actually saw). patra 1.12.x moved to
`mutex_lock()` (a FUNCTION → silent ud2), so once 1.12.3 is folded nothing
references the constants. Real fix: an agnos `#ifdef` no-op mutex backend in
sync.cyr (single-core cooperative-iron — no contender, lock is a no-op).

**Recurring class — `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX`-only stdlib helpers / raw
Linux syscall numbers.** This is the same shape, hit THREE times now:
v6.2.33 (io.cyr's `xopen`/`xstat`/… substrate), v6.2.35 (sync.cyr mutex), and
**v6.2.36 (io.cyr's `file_lock`/`file_unlock`/`file_trylock`/`file_lock_shared`/
`file_append_locked` — Linux-only + raw `syscall(73)`, so undefined `ud2` stubs
on agnos+macOS and the wrong number on aarch64; descent's `persist_init` →
libro FileStore → `file_append_locked` trapped at BOOT).** The fix pattern is
always the same: **route through the portable `x*` wrapper** that centralizes
the per-target syscall number (`xflock` → #59 agnos / #92 macOS / #73 x86 / #32
aarch64), and where a target lacks a feature the others have, branch it (agnos
has no kernel `AO_APPEND` yet → `xlseek` SEEK_END under the lock instead of
`O_APPEND`). PREVENTION: periodically grep `lib/` for `#ifdef
CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX` blocks that define `fn` (without sibling agnos/macОS
branches) and for raw `syscall(<small Linux num>)` outside the peer files —
each is a latent off-Linux `ud2`.

**How to recognize / guard it:**
- A consumer "compiles clean for `--agnos`" (or any target) but crashes
  early at runtime → grep the build stderr for `warning: undefined function`.
  Those are live landmines, not noise.
- A regression GATE for this class must NOT just check `exit 0` — it must
  assert the symbols are DEFINED (compile a probe that references them and
  FAIL if stderr contains `undefined function '<name>`). See
  scripts/agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh probe 1f.
- Mirror of the macОS-rot placebo: self-host + check.sh never exercise
  agnos codegen, so undefined-fn stubs ship "found by ports."
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_arch_x86_predefined_even_on_agnos"
description = "The compiler predefines `CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` on EVERY x86-hosted build — the AGNOS ring-3 target included (main.cyr:851, unconditional, before the target predefines). So a stdlib file that selects syscall numbers on `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` / `CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` ALONE silently takes the Linux x86 branch on agnos and emits Linux syscall numbers — which compiles clean and corrupts at runtime (agnos's numbers differ). The fix is a precedence `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` branch; surfaced v6.2.33 by sakshi's logger."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["preprocessor", "agnos", "cross-target", "CYRIUS_ARCH_X86", "CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS", "ifdef", "v6.2.33"]
content = """
**The trap**: `PP_PREDEFINE(S, "CYRIUS_ARCH_X86")` fires unconditionally
near the top of main.cyr (the x86-hosted compiler), BEFORE the per-target
`CYRIUS_TARGET_{AGNOS,WIN,MACOS,LINUX}` predefines. agnos is an x86_64
emit target, so on `env CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS=1 cycc` BOTH `CYRIUS_ARCH_X86`
AND `CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` are defined. A stdlib module that dispatches
syscall numbers purely on the ARCH macro —

    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86
    var SYS_OPEN = 2; var SYS_CLOSE = 3; var SYS_EXIT = 60;   # LINUX numbers
    #endif

— therefore takes the **Linux** branch on agnos. It compiles with zero
errors and then calls the wrong kernel: on agnos #2=getpid, #3=spawn,
#60=undefined, so a file logger silently corrupts (sakshi, v6.2.33).
This is the cross-target sibling of the macOS-rot placebo: nothing in
the self-host or check.sh path exercises agnos codegen, so it ships
"found by ports."

**The fix**: put the agnos branch FIRST and exclude the arch branches
under it —

    #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS
    var SYS_OPEN = 7; var SYS_CLOSE = 6; var SYS_EXIT = 0;     # agnos numbers
    #endif
    #ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86
    ...linux x86...
    #endif
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
    ...linux aarch64...
    #endif
    #endif

cyrius's preprocessor supports **nested `#ifdef`/`#ifndef`** (verified
4-deep) but has **no `#else`** — so a per-target split is always
"agnos/win/macos guards FIRST, then `#ifndef AGNOS { #ifdef ARCH... }`".

**Recognize it again**: any per-target `var SYS_* = <linux number>` or
raw `syscall(<linux-num>,…)` reachable on agnos that is guarded only on
an ARCH macro. cyrlint flags raw `sys_open(<expr>,<literal>,…)` and raw
getdents, but NOT a clean Linux-number `var` taken via the arch branch —
so this class needs a real `env CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS=1 cycc` build +
emit-inspect (the `_agnos_xsys_gate` / agnos-crossbuild gate), never just
self-host. Numbers that ARE carried in every peer (SYS_LSEEK,
SYS_GETRANDOM) are safe to use bare; flock is peer-carried ONLY on agnos,
so io.cyr's `xflock` centralizes its per-target number.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "element_typed_array_slot_spelling_v621"
description = "Bare `var a[N]` is byte-sized in a fn / slot-sized at top level — an accidental divergence that silently corrupts any address-taken local written with the `store64(&a + i*8)` slot idiom (the daimon route-404 class). v6.2.1 adds `var a: T[N]` (N elements of T) as the unambiguous slot/typed spelling; the audit found the class live in cycc itself (PARSE_SWITCH/PARSE_MATCH jump tables) + stdlib + folded libs."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["layout", "var-array", "byte-vs-slot", "0x12A000", "daimon", "v6.2.1"]
content = """
**The trap**: the SAME syntax `var a[N]` reserves different sizes by
scope. In a function (`PARSE_ARRAY`, parse_decl.cyr) it's `(N+7)&~7`
**bytes**; at top level (`PARSE_GVAR_ARR`) it's `N*8` (N i64 **slots**).
Both register into the one var-size table at `0x12A000`. A
function-local slot array written with `store64(&a + i*8, …)` therefore
runs off its byte-sized backing — `var parts[4]` is 8 bytes (1 slot),
so `store64(&parts + 24, …)` corrupts the next static object. Layout-
sensitive, so it stays dormant until an unrelated change shifts the
layout (daimon: deleting dead code moved `parts` 24 B before sandhi's
`" "` literal → every HTTP route 404'd).

**The fix (v6.2.1)**: `var a: T[N]` = N elements of T, sized
`N*sizeof(T)` identically in BOTH scopes. `i64[N]` = N slots (the slot
spelling), `u8[N]` = N bytes, widths i8..i64/u8..u64/u128. Pure
**frontend** change — the element width threads through `scalar_type`
(in-fn, `PARSE_ARRAY(S,noff,ew)`) / `ann_scalar` (top-level,
`PARSE_GVAR_ARR(S,noff,sti,ew)`) into the `0x12A000` byte size, and
every backend (x86/aarch64/macho/pe/cx) inherits it by SUMMING that
table — no backend edits. Bare `var a[N]` keeps its legacy per-scope
meaning (best reserved for byte buffers).

**Two-step bootstrap REQUIRED when migrating cycc's OWN arrays**:
because the OLD build/cycc byte-sizes the new `i64[N]` annotation,
`build/cycc → stage_a` gives stage_a byte-sized internal tables;
`stage_a → stage_b` (stage_a has the feature) gives slot-sized tables.
So `stage_a != stage_b`; the fixpoint is `stage_b == stage_c`. cycc's
own source had no >32-arm switch/match, so the under-sized `ends[256]`
(32 slots) self-hosted by luck for years — the classic latent-until-
layout-shift signature.

**Audit lesson**: run the audit against the lib SOURCE repos, not the
vendored fold — the fold-copy scan missed sigil `snp_report_verify`'s
`parsed[4]` that the source check caught (same `memset(&x,0,32)`-on-an-
8-byte-buffer bug as its sibling `inters[4]`). The ecosystem libs
(sigil/sakshi/agnosys) are the language's own stdlibs: patch each
lib's source, hold for release, then re-fold — never hand-edit the
`# Do not edit` vendored copy.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "large_fn_local_var_array_is_bss_not_stack"
description = "A large function-local `var buf[N]` in cycc is allocated in `.bss` static storage, NOT the stack frame — and a `var[]` sitting in a statically-dead region (e.g. after a `#ifdef`-gated early `return`) STILL reserves its `.bss` even on the path that never reaches it. Diagnose via section deltas, not `sub rsp` scanning."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["layout", "bss", "var-array", "agnos", "stack", "v6.1.12"]
content = """
**The trap**: `lib/io.cyr` `getenv()` had an 8 KB `var buf[8192]`
*after* a `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` early `return`. The agnos
build never touches `buf`, but the agnoshi consumer #PF'd and the
filed issue blamed an "8 KB stack frame reserved past the early
return." Plausible — but WRONG about the mechanism.

**What actually happens**: in cycc a function-local `var[]` of
this size is emitted into **`.bss`** (static storage), not as a
stack-frame allocation. Verified on the real `--agnos` build by
diffing section sizes before/after guarding the buffer behind
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`:

  .bss   0x2f18 -> 0xf08   (-8208 B = buf + align)
  .text  -928 B            (the now-dead environ reader)
  max stack frame: unchanged at 0xd0 — no `sub rsp, 0x2000`
  ever existed.

So scanning the disassembly for a big `sub rsp` finds nothing and
can make you think "the buffer isn't there" — it IS, just in
`.bss`. Always check `llvm-objdump -h` section deltas, not just
prologue `sub rsp`, when chasing a "phantom buffer" on a
constrained target.

**Why it still faulted on agnos** (mechanism the issue got wrong):
an oversized `.bss` grows the PT_LOAD memsz the loader must
zero-map; the agnos #PF is `.bss`-image / loader-mapping related,
NOT init-stack overflow. A "widen the ring-3 init stack" fix
aims a layer off.

**The fix**: guard the whole reader (buffer + parse loop) behind
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` so the dead region contributes
nothing to the agnos image. The broader compiler-side want —
elide storage for a `var[]` that's provably never live on the
taken path — stays a (low-priority) follow-on; the preprocessor
guard fully resolves the case. Related: the v6.0.79 `cyml.cyr`
oversized-`var[]`-on-constrained-target overflow.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "slice_fn_local_layout_flip"
description = "Pre-§8 16-byte fn-local slices put the canonical slot at the higher stack address; `&slice + 8` overflowed past the local frame into saved-rbp territory. §8 layout-flip (v5.8.16) allocates the high-half slot FIRST (higher address) and the canonical slot SECOND (lower address)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["slices", "layout", "stack", "v5.8.16"]
content = """
**The trap**: pre-v5.8.16, `var s: [u8] = 0;` allocated a
single canonical slot (the named slot, where `&s` resolves)
and a sentinel-cleared high-half slot. The canonical slot was
the LOWER-numbered slot (higher stack address per cyrius's
slot-N-at-rbp-(N+1)*8 convention). `slice_set(&s, ptr, len)`
did `store64(s+8, len)`, which on the stack means
`store64(rbp - canon_disp + 8, len)` — overflowing past the
local frame into saved-rbp territory.

This corrupted the caller's saved rbp. Worked anyway:

  1. Round-tripped within the fn — slice_set wrote and slice_len
     read the same (corrupted) location.
  2. `slices_typing.tcyr`'s `return slice_len(&x);` form passed
     because main's rbp = 0 from the C runtime — the read
     returned 0 by coincidence.
  3. §7 indexing tests passed because each test exited via
     `syscall(60, ...)` before any code referenced the corrupted
     rbp.

**Surfaced when**: §8 introduced `s.len` reads via &s+8
(memory I/O through the canonical-slot address) — same
arithmetic as slice_set, but the test reading expected a
deterministic value, not "whatever happened to be at saved rbp."

**Fix**: for `scalar_type==16`, allocate the HIGH-HALF slot
FIRST (slot li, higher address) and the CANONICAL slot SECOND
(slot li+1, lower address). The name + meta live on slot li+1
so FINDLOCAL returns it; EFLADDR(li+1) yields the lower
address; EFLADDR(li+1) + 8 lands on slot li (higher address) —
matching the slice convention's "second 8 bytes."

Init both halves: zero the canonical slot via EFLSTORE(li+1)
and zero the high half via address-based memory store
(EFLADDR_X1 + EADDIMM_X1(8) + ESTOC). Address-based init for
the high half is required because the regalloc post-body
peephole would patch EFLSTORE(li) to `mov rbx, rax` (writing
to a register, not the stack slot). See `slice_high_half_regalloc_pin`.

**Recognize again**: any "fn-local 16-byte type silently fails"
shape. If two adjacent `var x: u128` or `var s: [T]` interact
weirdly, check the layout-flip is still in place.

Full backstory: v5_8.cyml's `slice_fn_local_layout_flip_v5816`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "slice_high_half_regalloc_pin"
description = "Slice's high-half slot has no user-visible name; the regalloc post-body peephole would allocate a callee-saved register for it, leaving the actual stack slot uninitialized. §8 routes high-half I/O through address-based memory access to keep the slot in memory."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["slices", "regalloc", "v5.8.16"]
content = """
**The trap**: the regalloc post-body peephole (parse_fn.cyr
v5.6.20 picker) counts `mov [rbp+disp], rax` /
`mov rax, [rbp+disp]` patterns per slot to decide which user-
locals to pin to callee-saved registers (rbx, r12-r15). Time-
sliced sharing assumes every local interval starts with a STORE —
the register holds the right value from the start of each
interval, no LOAD-from-stack needed.

The slice's high-half slot has no user-visible name (sentinel-
cleared by the §8 layout-flip), so nothing else takes its
address via `&<ident>`. The picker's safety scan bars
`lea rax, [rbp+disp]` (treats `&local` as not-safe). Without
intervention, the high-half slot looks mov-only — picker
allocates rbx for it.

Then `var s: [T] = 0;`'s init `EFLSTORE(li, rax=0)` patches to
`mov rbx, rax` — writes 0 to rbx, leaves the stack slot
uninitialized. Subsequent slice_set memory-writes to the slot
are invisible to the register; `s.len` reads (which go through
the memory access path) see uninit stack data.

**Fix**: init the high half via address-based memory store
(EFLADDR_X1 + EADDIMM_X1(8) + ESTOC) rather than EFLSTORE.
The picker doesn't count memory-via-rcx writes, so the high
slot stays in memory. §8 dot-syntax (.len read/write) and
slice_len helper-fn calls all see the right value.

Same reasoning for §8 dot-syntax `s.len`: lower to address-
based load (EFLADDR_X1 + EADDIMM_X1(8) + ELODC), not direct
EFLLOAD against the slot. `s.ptr` could safely use EFLLOAD
(canonical slot is naturally barred from regalloc by slice_set
taking `&s`), but uses address-based for symmetry.

**Recognize again**: any "no-name auxiliary slot interacts
weirdly with regalloc" shape. The picker's safety scan only
bars slots with `&` taken via a NAMED ident; phantom slots
(high half of a 16-byte type, scratch slots) need explicit
exclusion.

Full backstory: v5_8.cyml's `slice_high_half_regalloc_pin_v5816`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "stale_fixed_cap_drift"
description = "Cap checks drift out of sync with the actual buffer size. The buffer gets expanded; the cap check / comment / docs DON'T. Bug sits latent until a downstream workload crosses the OLD threshold."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["caps", "drift", "diagnostics", "v3.4.20", "recurring"]
content = """
**The trap pattern**: cyrius uses fixed-size heap regions for
nearly every compiler data structure. Each region has an
explicit byte count (e.g., tok_names is 64 KB, preprocess_out
is 2 MB at v5.6.40). Most regions have a "capacity check"
somewhere in the code that silently caps or errors out when
the region fills.

Multiple times now, a capacity check has drifted out of sync
with the actual buffer size. The pattern:

1. Buffer gets expanded in one place (the actual allocation).
2. Cap check / comment / docs DON'T get updated in the other
   places.
3. Bug sits latent for N releases.
4. Some downstream workload crosses the OLD threshold.
5. Confusing error fires that doesn't point at the real
   buffer.
6. Delayed debugging session to figure out the stale cap is
   lying.

**Concrete instances**:

- v3.3.17 (Bug #33): LEXHEX read hex literals from the raw
  input buffer instead of the preprocessed buffer. Masked for
  years.
- v3.4.19: input_buf read loop capped at 131,072 bytes (the
  original 128 KB) silently — mabda's 141 KB bundle triggered
  the latent bug.
- v3.4.20 (lex.cyr:1077): preprocess_out cap checked
  `op > 524288` (512 KB), but the buffer was expanded to 1 MB
  in v3.4.5. Stale for FIVE releases.
- v3.4.20 (cyrius.toml roadmap): documented Input buffer as
  512 KB while actual code was 128 KB then 256 KB. Three
  different numbers across three files.

**The proactive sweep** (do at every minor closeout):

For each fixed-size buffer in cyrius, grep for EVERY
occurrence of the capacity constant (allocation AND cap
check) and confirm they agree. Also check comments, heap map,
docs, roadmap, and Open Limits table — all four need to match.

The Open Limits table in `docs/development/roadmap.md` is
worth special attention — it's the table downstream projects
read to decide how much headroom they have.

**The diagnostic discipline** (every fixed-size buffer):

Every fixed-size buffer should have an EXPLICIT overflow check
with a CLEAR error message pointing at the exact location to
fix:

```cyr
if (op > 1048576) {
    syscall(SYS_WRITE, 2,
        "error: expanded source exceeds 1MB (", 36);
    PRNUM(op);
    syscall(SYS_WRITE, 2,
        " bytes; raise preprocess_out cap in src/main.cyr)\\n", 50);
    syscall(SYS_EXIT, 1);
}
```

Silent truncation is never acceptable — it moves the symptom
arbitrarily far from the cause.

**Recognize again**: any error message reporting a numeric
threshold that doesn't match the comment / heap map / Open
Limits table.

Cross-ref: patterns.cyml's
`heap_cap_growth_compiler_grows_to_fit_language` (the dual —
when to RAISE caps vs when to discover stale caps).

Full backstory: retros/v3.cyml `stale_fixed_cap_pattern` +
`silent_stdin_truncation_saga`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "stdlib_alloc_grow_step_must_cover_request"
description = "Bump-allocator grow logic must cover the requested allocation, not a fixed delta. Fixed step `0x100000` regardless of requested size returned pointers past the brk/mmap for any alloc(>1MB)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["alloc", "stdlib", "v5.6.x"]
content = """
**The trap**: bump allocator (`lib/alloc.cyr`) must grow its
backing region by AT LEAST the requested size, not a fixed
delta. Pre-fix, the grow logic was:

```cyr
if (alloc_used + n > brk_top) {
    sys_brk(brk_top + 0x100000);   # grow by 1 MB
    brk_top = brk_top + 0x100000;
}
return alloc_used; alloc_used = alloc_used + n;
```

For `alloc(1500000)` (1.5 MB request), the grow step adds 1 MB
but the requested size is 1.5 MB → returned pointer is past
the new brk_top. Subsequent stores write into unmapped memory
→ SIGSEGV.

**Fix**: grow by max(requested, fixed_step):

```cyr
if (alloc_used + n > brk_top) {
    var grow = 0x100000;
    if (n > grow) { grow = n + 0x100000; }
    sys_brk(brk_top + grow);
    brk_top = brk_top + grow;
}
```

**Recognize again**: any "alloc(N) for large N silently
SIGSEGVs in subsequent store" shape. Single-store SIGSEGVs
right after an alloc are almost always bump-allocator
boundary issues; print `alloc_used` and `brk_top` at the
crash site to confirm.

Full backstory: v5_6.cyml's
`stdlib_alloc_grow_step_vs_requested_size`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "libssl_needs_fdlopen_not_dynlib_open"
description = "libssl's OPENSSL_init_ssl uses a recursive pthread mutex whose __kind field lives in the glibc TCB. cyrius's dynlib_bootstrap_tls zeroed TCB reads __kind=0 (non-recursive); same-thread re-entry deadlocks on futex. Use lib/fdlopen.cyr (foreign-dlopen) instead."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["dynlib", "tls-loader", "fdlopen", "v5.6.37"]
content = """
**The trap**: cyrius has two ways to load shared libraries at
runtime:

1. `lib/dynlib.cyr` — pure-cyrius ELF .so loader via mmap +
   GNU hash table walk. No libc, no glibc dlopen. Bypasses
   glibc's TLS/TCB setup entirely.

2. `lib/fdlopen.cyr` — foreign-dlopen wrapper. Calls into
   glibc's actual dlopen via the AT_BASE+offset trick, which
   means the loaded library gets glibc's full TLS/TCB setup
   including pthread_*.

For libssl specifically, dynlib_open works for getting symbol
addresses — but calling `OPENSSL_init_ssl()` deadlocks on a
futex inside libcrypto's pthread_mutex_lock. Why: libssl's
init uses `PTHREAD_RECURSIVE_MUTEX_INITIALIZER_NP`. The mutex
struct has a `__kind` field in glibc's TCB. cyrius's
dynlib_bootstrap_tls (the minimal TLS setup that dynlib_open
provides) zeros the TCB. `__kind == 0` means "non-recursive"
in pthread's encoding. Same-thread re-entry to a mutex that
the same thread already holds → futex wait forever (because
non-recursive).

**Rule**: if a library's init function uses pthread mutexes
internally, you NEED fdlopen (real glibc dlopen → real glibc
TLS/TCB), not dynlib (cyrius minimal loader). The list of
libraries that hit this includes:

- libssl (OPENSSL_init_ssl)
- libpthread (everything)
- libgomp (OpenMP runtime)
- libc-internal locking primitives (rare in pure cyrius use,
  but anything that touches TLS-cancellation hooks)

Conversely, dynlib is fine for:

- Pure C libraries with no pthread internals (libz, libcrypto's
  primitive routines if you skip OPENSSL_init_*)
- Libraries you call only for symbol addresses (dlsym then
  call manually)

**Recognize again**: any "dlopen succeeds, dlsym returns valid
pointer, calling that pointer hangs forever" shape on a
shared library that uses pthread mutexes.

Full backstory: v5_6.cyml's `libssl_needs_fdlopen_not_dynlib_open`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "sysv_odd_stack_args_rsp_alignment"
description = "SysV ABI requires rsp 16-aligned at every CALL (so callee sees rsp+8 16-aligned at entry). Cyrius's ECALLPOPS for n>6 args used `add rsp, 48` unconditionally; for odd nextra (N=7,9,11) this left rsp 8-aligned at CALL, propagating misalignment."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["abi", "sysv", "alignment", "v5.6.41"]
content = """
**The trap**: SysV AMD64 ABI requires rsp 16-byte aligned at
every CALL instruction (so the callee sees rsp+8 16-byte
aligned at entry, with the saved rip below it). Cyrius's
codegen for fn calls with >6 args uses ECALLPOPS to clean up
stack arguments after the call:

```cyr
# n > 6 args, n_extra = n - 6
# Push n_extra extra args
# call helper
# add rsp, n_extra * 8  ← cleanup
```

Pre-v5.6.41, the cleanup was `add rsp, n_extra * 8`
unconditionally. For odd `n_extra` (N = 7, 9, 11 args), this
leaves rsp 8-byte-aligned at the next CALL. Misalignment
propagates downstream until something does SSE on a stack-
saved value (libssl/libc prologs commonly do `movdqa xmm,
[rsp+N]`) — SIGSEGV at first instruction.

**Fix**: align rsp to 16 BEFORE pushing extra args:

```cyr
if ((n_extra & 1) == 1) {
    # Pre-pad rsp by 8 to absorb odd n_extra count
    push rax  # any value; we sub rsp, 8 effectively
}
push n_extra args
call helper
add rsp, (n_extra + (n_extra_odd ? 1 : 0)) * 8
```

Win64 path was already correct via framesize-mod-15 alignment
branch; SysV path missed it.

**Recognize again**: any "SIGSEGV on the first SSE instruction
of a libc / libssl entry" shape. Common triggers: 7-arg fn
calls, OpenSSL's EVP_DecryptInit_ex (8 args), recvmmsg
(7 args). Always check rsp alignment via `gdb p $rsp & 0xF`
at the failing instruction.

Full backstory: v5_6.cyml's `sysv_odd_stack_args_rsp_alignment_violation`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "fn_keyword_in_param_position"
description = "`fn` is a reserved keyword and cannot be used as a parameter name even in helper fns. Cyrius surfaces this with 'expected identifier, got reserved keyword fn' at the NEXT fn definition, not the offending one."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "diagnostics", "v5.7.46"]
content = """
**The trap**: writing `fn helper(fn, arg)` declares a
parameter named `fn`. Cyrius's parser treats `fn` as a
reserved keyword (token type 32), so this fails — but the
error message points at the WRONG location.

The parser first sees `fn helper(`, accepts it, then sees the
parameter list. When it tries to read `fn` as a parameter
name, it errors with "expected identifier, got reserved
keyword fn" — but the PARSE_TOKEN cursor is at the NEXT
function definition's `fn` keyword, not the one in the param
list. So the error reports the line of the next fn, not the
broken param.

Diagnosis is "the fn at line X is broken" → user inspects
line X → finds nothing wrong → confused.

**Fix at v5.7.46** (cyrius v5.5.26+ ERR_EXPECT enhancement):
when the expected token is type 2 (identifier) and the got
token is a keyword (IS_KEYWORD_TOK returns 1), the diagnostic
explicitly names the keyword:

```
error: expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'fn'
       (cannot be used as an identifier; rename the variable/
       field/fn)
```

The error fires at the keyword's actual line, not the next
fn. The "rename" hint helps users figure out the fix without
having to know which keywords are reserved.

**The reserved-keyword list** that triggers this: `fn`, `if`,
`else`, `elif`, `while`, `for`, `return`, `var`, `struct`,
`enum`, `union`, `match`, `defer`, `include`, `asm`, `kernel`,
`object`, `secret`, `in`, `shared`, `default`, `as`,
`continue`, `break`. Any of these as a param/local/struct-
field name surfaces the same error.

**Recognize again**: any "compile error 'expected
identifier' on a line that looks fine" shape. Check the
PREVIOUS fn's param list for keyword reuse.

Full backstory: v5_7.cyml's `fn_keyword_in_param_position`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "lsp_globals_before_fn_definitions"
description = "Cyrius requires top-level `var X` to be declared before any fn body that references X. Applies to programs/cyrius-lsp.cyr's symbol-table backing buffers too."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "lsp", "global-init-order", "v5.7.39"]
content = """
**The trap**: cyrius's parser does a single-pass top-down
walk of source. When a fn body references a top-level
`var X`, that `var X` must already be declared at parse time.
Forward fn calls work (the parser pre-scans fn signatures);
forward GLOBAL VAR references DO NOT work.

This is the v5.7.32 cyrlint warning class
(`global-init-order forward-ref`) that mabda surfaced. The
compiler ALSO doesn't catch every shape — the warning is
opt-in via `cyrius lint`.

**The cyrius-lsp specific instance** (v5.7.39): the LSP
extension added cross-file go-to-def + documentSymbol via a
symbol-table backing buffer. The buffer was a top-level `var
symbol_table[N]`. The fns that read it (`SYMBOL_LOOKUP`,
`SYMBOL_INSERT`) were defined before the `var` line in the
source layout.

Pre-fix: the parser saw the fn defs first, registered them,
parsed their bodies → reached `symbol_table` reference →
"undefined variable." Post-fix: moved `var symbol_table[N]`
above all fn defs that reference it.

**Recognize again**: any "undefined variable" error on a top-
level var that VISUALLY appears in the source somewhere.
Check it appears BEFORE the fns that read it.

**Generalize**: any new top-level `var X` declared in
cyrius's own source (or any cyrius project) MUST go above the
fn bodies that reference it. The parser is single-pass for
globals.

Full backstory: v5_7.cyml's `lsp_globals_before_fn_definitions_v5_7_39`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "snapshot_ping_pong_lib_corruption"
description = "lib/*.cyr edits silently revert via install-snapshot copy-back during cyrius deps"
category = "cyrius"
---
Surfaced 2026-05-03 mid-v5.8.23 bite #2 when `lib/tagged.cyr`
edits reverted between Edit calls during the sum-type migration.

== ROOT CAUSE ==

Two-step ping-pong loop:

  1. `version-bump.sh` runs `install.sh --refresh-only` which
     copies `lib/*.cyr` from the repo INTO
     `~/.cyrius/versions/<v>/lib/` and `~/.cyrius/lib`.
  2. Subsequent `cyrius deps` resolution (e.g., during
     `check.sh`) copies the snapshot version BACK into the
     repo.

If you edit a `lib/*.cyr` file AFTER a `version-bump.sh` ran
(snapshot is now stale), the next `check.sh` invocation will
overwrite your edit with the prior snapshot.

== SYMPTOM ==

You make an Edit. You verify the change. You run `check.sh`.
Tests pass. But the next time you read the file, your edit is
GONE. cycc is also gone — the snapshot's version was pulled back
in along with the lib file, AND the snapshot's lib is what's
actually in `~/.cyrius/lib`.

== MITIGATION ==

Documented in CLAUDE.md "Snapshot-ping-pong protection
(cyrius-side `lib/*.cyr` edits)" subsection (added v5.8.26).
3-step recipe:

  1. Edit `lib/<file>.cyr`.
  2. Manually refresh the install snapshot:
     ```sh
     cp lib/<file>.cyr ~/.cyrius/versions/$(cat VERSION)/lib/<file>.cyr
     cp lib/<file>.cyr ~/.cyrius/lib/<file>.cyr
     ```
     OR run `sh scripts/version-bump.sh "$(cat VERSION)"` (same-
     version regenerate path).
  3. Run `sh scripts/check.sh` — file should now stick.

== RELATED ECOSYSTEM RULE ==

CLAUDE.md "Downstream repo setup (ecosystem rule)" forbids
downstream repos (mabda, sigil, sakshi, yukti, etc.) from
symlinking their `lib/` to `~/.cyrius/lib` because writes
through the symlink propagate up the chain into this repo.

Sakshi was found violating this at v5.8.23 (its `lib/` was a
directory-level symlink to `~/.cyrius/lib`); fixed via
`rm sakshi/lib && (cd sakshi && cyrius deps)`.

Audit script (also added to CLAUDE.md v5.8.26):
    find ~/Repos -maxdepth 2 -type l -name lib

Returns nothing now; sakshi was the only directory-level
violation. Other repos have only single-file `lib/<dep>.cyr`
symlinks (legitimate `cyrius deps` artifacts).

[[entries]]
name = "foldin_symlink_vs_regular_file"
description = "When folding a sibling distfile into cyrius's lib/ via the sandhi pattern, the vendored file MUST be a regular file (mode 100644), not the absolute-path symlink that `cyrius deps` creates by default. Symlinks point into the user's local cache (/home/<user>/.cyrius/...) and dangle on any other machine — including CI."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["foldin", "deps", "ci", "sandhi-pattern", "v5.8.65"]
content = """
**The trap**: `cyrius deps` resolves a `[deps.<name>]` entry by
populating `~/.cyrius/deps/<name>/<tag>/` and then creating
`lib/<name>.cyr` as a SYMLINK to that cache path:
```
lib/sakshi.cyr -> /home/<user>/.cyrius/deps/sakshi/2.2.3/dist/sakshi.cyr
```
The symlink target is an ABSOLUTE PATH in the resolving user's
home. On the user's local machine this works because the cache
is populated. On any OTHER machine — including GitHub Actions
CI runners (whose home is `/home/runner/...`) — the symlink
dangles.

When folding a dep into stdlib (sandhi pattern), naive `git
add lib/<name>.cyr` commits the symlink. Git records mode 120000
(symlink). On checkout elsewhere, the symlink resolves against
a non-existent path → file effectively missing.

**Surfaces as**: tcyrs that `include "lib/<name>.cyr"` fail to
compile with "file not found" → cycc exits non-zero → under
`bash -e` (GHA's default shell mode), the test loop terminates
silently mid-loop. No `FAIL:` print, no summary, just exit 1.
Easy to misdiagnose as "test crashed the runner."

**The fix at v5.8.65** (the slot that surfaced this):
1. Convert the symlink to a regular file:
   ```
   cp --remove-destination "$(readlink lib/<name>.cyr)" lib/<name>.cyr
   ```
   `--remove-destination` is essential — `cp` without it follows
   the symlink and writes through to the cache, not the repo.
2. Commit the type change. `git diff` shows mode 120000 → 100644.
3. The cyrius distlib walker (`scripts/lib/audit-walk.sh`) skips
   files matching `^# Generated by: cyrius distlib$` in the first
   5 lines — those byte-snapshot bundles must not be reformatted.
   See also `distlib_marker_styles_v5865`.

**Recognize again**: any time you fold a `[deps.X]` into `lib/`
under the sandhi pattern, audit `git ls-tree HEAD lib/<X>.cyr`
for mode 120000 BEFORE pushing. The v5.7.0 sandhi precedent
(`lib/sandhi.cyr`) is the canonical example — mode 100644,
never a symlink.

**Why it wasn't caught locally**: the user's local machine
HAD the symlink target populated. `check.sh` self-host green,
all 127 tcyrs pass via `cyrius test`. CI is the first place
the absolute path doesn't resolve.

The fix is one shell loop, but the diagnosis took multiple
re-pushes (audit-walk marker fix, then ci.yml inline marker
fan-out, then sakshi-specific bundle.sh marker style). Saved
here so the next foldin slot starts with the de-symlink step
in the playbook.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "distlib_marker_styles_v5865"
description = "Two distlib-bundle marker styles exist in cyrius's vendored lib/: `# Generated by: cyrius distlib` (cyrius distlib output — patra/sigil/vani/yukti/sankoch/agnosys/mabda/sandhi) and `# Bundled distribution of <name>` (sakshi only — its scripts/bundle.sh predates cyrius distlib). audit-walk + ci.yml's stdlib walkers must skip BOTH."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["foldin", "audit-walk", "fmt", "lint", "sakshi", "v5.8.65"]
content = """
**The trap**: `scripts/lib/audit-walk.sh`'s
`_aw_is_distlib_bundle()` and the inline equivalents in
`.github/workflows/ci.yml`'s `Format check (stdlib)` /
`Lint (stdlib)` / `Doc coverage (stdlib)` steps used to match
exactly one marker:
```sh
head -n 5 "$f" 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^# Generated by: cyrius distlib$"
```
When sakshi was folded at v5.8.65, its dist file's header
read:
```
# sakshi.cyr — tracing, error handling, and structured logging
# Bundled distribution of sakshi v2.2.3
```
sakshi builds its dist via its own `scripts/bundle.sh`
predating cyrius's `cyrius distlib` subcommand. Different
header style. The skip-check didn't match → audit-walk ran
`cyrfmt --check` against the vendored bundle, found drift in
some indented `#` comment lines (sakshi-side bundle.sh produces
output whose indent style differs from current cyrfmt's
canonical), and the Format gate failed.

**Wrong fix would be**: apply `cyrfmt --write lib/sakshi.cyr`.
That would make lib/sakshi.cyr DIVERGE from sakshi's
dist/sakshi.cyr — breaking the byte-identity guarantee that's
the whole point of the foldin.

**Right fix**: extend the marker to recognize both styles:
```sh
head -n 5 "$f" 2>/dev/null | \
    grep -qE "^# Generated by: cyrius distlib$|^# Bundled distribution of "
```
Updated in 4 places at v5.8.65: `scripts/lib/audit-walk.sh`'s
`_aw_is_distlib_bundle` + 3 inline call sites in ci.yml
(Format/Lint/Doc-coverage stdlib steps). Mirror comment in
ci.yml notes the audit-walk equivalence so future readers know
they're paired.

**Recognize again**: any new sibling-fold whose dist generator
isn't cyrius distlib needs the marker check audited. The first
few lines of the fold-target's dist file should announce the
generator clearly. If neither `Generated by: cyrius distlib`
nor `Bundled distribution of` appears in the first 5 lines,
add the third marker style at all 4 call sites.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "heap_layout_relocation_option_a_vs_b"
description = "When growing a heap region 8x in-place collides with neighboring scratch state, the choice is Option A (in-place grow + shift downstream — monotonic, ~200+ edits) or Option B (relocate to fresh high block — non-monotonic, ~50 edits). v5.8.46 (tok arrays) + v5.8.59 (str_data) both took Option B; v5.8.61 reverted .59 back to monotonic once the gap was free."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap", "layout", "two-step-bootstrap", "v5.8.46", "v5.8.59", "v5.8.61"]
content = """
**The trap**: cyrius's heap regions have hardcoded offset
constants scattered across many files. Growing any region 8x
to absorb new data collides with whatever sits immediately
above. Two paradigms emerge:

**Option A — in-place grow + shift downstream**:
- Region stays at its original offset; size grows.
- Every region above shifts up by the size delta.
- Layout stays MONOTONIC (small-offset → big-offset reads
  cleanly).
- Cost: every offset literal in the affected band updates.
  v5.8.59 estimated this at ~200+ edits across ~20 files for
  the str_data 256 KB → 2 MB grow. Byte-identity risk grows
  linearly with edit count.

**Option B — relocate to fresh high block**:
- Region moves to a new offset above the highest-used heap.
- Size grows in the new location; old slot becomes empty.
- Layout is NON-MONOTONIC (small-offset reads jump past the
  vacant slot to the high block).
- Cost: only the offset literal changes (~50 edits).
- Precedent: v5.8.46 tok-array relocation moved
  tok_types/values/lines from `0x94A000`/`0xD4A000`/`0xF4A000`
  (tangled with output_buf) to a clean post-TS-region high
  block at `0x368C000`/`0x3E8C000`/`0x468C000`.

**The pattern that emerged**: take Option B during the
expansion slot to get the new capacity shipped fast and
audited cheaply. Pin a follow-up "monotonic reorg" slot for
when the layout-non-monotonicity becomes a maintenance
liability. v5.8.59 took Option B to ship NFKC/NFKD via the
str_data bump (placed at `0x4E8C000`); v5.8.61 reverted to
monotonic once the var_enum_id..preprocess_out gap was sized
right (str_data now at `0x21A000`). Two-step bootstrap
required at both: cycc (old layout) compiles cc5b (new layout);
cc5b compiles cc5b'; cc5b == cc5b' byte-identical at the new
size.

**When to use which**:
- New region or major cap raise → Option B initially.
- Cycle wind-down or pre-major-bump → fold back to Option A
  (monotonic) when the receiving gap is the right size.
- DO NOT skip the two-step bootstrap audit for either path —
  heap layout changes are byte-identity-critical.

**Trade-off note**: Option B's non-monotonicity makes future
heap-map readers do mental math (str_data jumped to where?).
The follow-up monotonic reorg pays this back, but only if the
slot pin holds (don't let the "we'll reorg next minor" become
"we'll reorg next major" — pre-v6.0 hardening pin exists for
exactly this drift, see `_aw_is_distlib_bundle` mention in
the heap-map block of src/main.cyr).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "preprocess_out_vs_str_data_caps"
description = "Two distinct heap caps gate compilation. str_data caps the bytes the COMPILER writes for tracked string literals (raised to 2 MB at v5.8.59). preprocess_out caps the bytes of EXPANDED SOURCE the preprocessor produces from include resolution (still 2 MB since v5.6.40). Hitting one isn't the same as hitting the other, and the workarounds are different."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap", "preprocessor", "str_data", "v5.8.60"]
content = """
**The trap**: at v5.8.60 (NFKC/NFKD), the original design
used 116-char fixed-width compat-decomp records. 3833 records
× 116 chars = ~445 KB hex-encoded source — well under the
v5.8.59-bumped str_data 2 MB cap. Compiled fine in isolation.

But every consumer tcyr that includes lib/unicode/normalize.cyr
(transitively pulling in _normalize_data.cyr's ~445 KB blob)
also includes the rest of stdlib via auto-prepend. The
*expanded source* — the post-preprocessor concatenation of
all includes — overflowed `preprocess_out` (the 2 MB buffer at
S+0x44A000):
```
error: expanded source exceeds 2MB (2114828 bytes)
```
str_data has plenty of room. preprocess_out is the wall.

**These are two DIFFERENT walls**:
- `str_data` (`0x21A000`, 2 MB at v5.8.61): bytes the compiler
  writes for tracked string literals during codegen. Cap
  applies per-translation-unit.
- `preprocess_out` (`0x44A000`, 2 MB since v5.6.40): bytes of
  the source after include resolution. Cap applies BEFORE the
  lexer even runs.

A consumer that auto-prepends 14 stdlib modules + 6 sibling
distlib modules can sit at 1.6 MB expanded source even before
tcyr-specific code. Adding a 445 KB data table easily blows
the cap.

**The fix at v5.8.60**: switch the data-table encoding from
fixed-width 116-char records (445 KB total) to a 2-table
split — sorted IDX (12 chars/record × 3833 = 46 KB) +
variable-width DATA blob (count + cps, ~41 KB). Total ~87 KB,
80% reduction. Same lookup semantics (binary search IDX → data
offset → read count + cps), but consumer-side preprocess_out
stays well under cap.

**Recognize again**: any new fold-target that adds large
data blobs to lib/ should be sized against BOTH caps:
- Single-file size vs 64 KB string-literal cap (token
  encoding's u16 length field).
- Aggregate across ALL auto-prepended stdlib + the consumer's
  own source vs preprocess_out 2 MB cap.

The 2 MB preprocess_out cap is the easier one to forget
because it's not in the compiler's diagnostic surface during
single-file edits — only manifests when consumers compile.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "install_sh_cp_L_same_file_silent_fail"
description = "install.sh's --refresh-only path uses `cp -L lib/*.cyr` to copy stdlib into snapshot. When lib/<dep>.cyr is a `cyrius deps`-resolved symlink AND the snapshot dir has a parallel symlink to the SAME real file, cp -L exits with 'same file' error. `set -e` makes this fatal — install.sh exits BEFORE the symlink-update block at line 249-251. ~/.cyrius/bin / ~/.cyrius/current stay pointing at the previous version. Discovered v5.10.37."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cyrius", "install", "deps", "symlink", "set-e", "v5.10.37", "v5.10.46"]
content = """
WHAT BREAKS

After `sh scripts/version-bump.sh 5.10.X`, expected end state:
- `cat ~/.cyrius/current` → 5.10.X
- `readlink ~/.cyrius/bin` → ~/.cyrius/versions/5.10.X/bin
- `~/.cyrius/bin/cycc --version` → cycc 5.10.X

Observed (multi-bump streak in v5.10.x cycle):
- ~/.cyrius/current → stale (5.10.34 e.g.)
- readlink ~/.cyrius/bin → stale version's bin
- cycc --version → stale 5.10.34

User-visible symptom: `cyrius-prompt-info tool` (which reads
through ~/.cyrius/bin/cycc) shows wrong toolchain version on
the user's starship prompt. Easy to mis-read as "starship.toml
corruption" — actual config bytes are intact, but the cyrius
prompt segments render with stale data.

ROOT CAUSE

scripts/install.sh's --refresh-only fast path includes a
lib-copy loop (around line 224):

```sh
while IFS= read -r f; do
    rel="${f#lib/}"
    dst="$CYRIUS_HOME/versions/$VERSION/lib/$rel"
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$dst")"
    cp -L "$f" "$dst"   # ← FAILS with "same file" for symlink colliders
    _lib_count=$((_lib_count + 1))
done <<EOF_LIB
$(find -L lib -type f -name '*.cyr')
EOF_LIB
```

When `lib/mabda.cyr` is a `cyrius deps`-resolved symlink into
`~/.cyrius/deps/mabda/3.0.0-rc.2/dist/mabda.cyr`, AND the
snapshot at `~/.cyrius/versions/$VERSION/lib/mabda.cyr` is
ALSO a symlink to the same real file (set up by a prior
refresh that ran when the deps target was the same version),
`cp -L` dereferences BOTH and sees source and destination
resolve to the same inode:

```
cp: 'lib/mabda.cyr' and '/home/macro/.cyrius/versions/$VER/lib/mabda.cyr' are the same file
```

install.sh's top-level `set -e` makes this exit code fatal.
The script exits before reaching the symlink-update block:

```sh
# install.sh:249-251 (NEVER RUNS when cp -L fails)
rm -rf "$CYRIUS_HOME/bin" "$CYRIUS_HOME/lib"
ln -sf "$CYRIUS_HOME/versions/$VERSION/bin" "$CYRIUS_HOME/bin"
ln -sf "$CYRIUS_HOME/versions/$VERSION/lib" "$CYRIUS_HOME/lib"
```

So ~/.cyrius/bin / ~/.cyrius/lib / ~/.cyrius/current stay
pointing at whichever version was previously active.

HOW TO DETECT IT MANUALLY

After version-bump.sh, verify:

```sh
test "$(readlink ~/.cyrius/bin | sed 's|.*/versions/||;s|/bin||')" = "$(cat VERSION)" || echo "SYMLINK STALE"
```

If the readlink doesn't match VERSION, install.sh exited
before the symlink dance. Re-run with:

```sh
rm -rf ~/.cyrius/{bin,lib} && \
  ln -sf ~/.cyrius/versions/$(cat VERSION)/bin ~/.cyrius/bin && \
  ln -sf ~/.cyrius/versions/$(cat VERSION)/lib ~/.cyrius/lib && \
  echo "$(cat VERSION)" > ~/.cyrius/current
```

FIX (pinned v5.10.46 closeout)

Two-part fix:
1. **Primary** — change `cyrius deps` to physical-copy
   lib/<dep>.cyr instead of symlinking. Eliminates the
   inode-collision root cause at the architectural layer.
   (Option C from the v5.10.37 user discussion.)
2. **Belt-and-suspenders** — replace `cp -L` with
   `rm -f "$dst" && cp -L` (always nuke dst first) AND add
   a tail-of-refresh-only verification that `readlink
   ~/.cyrius/bin` matches the new version with hard-fail
   if not.

The verification step would have caught this bug in seconds
instead of multiple observations across slots; silent-skip
is the worst kind of failure mode.

LESSON: Install scripts running with `set -e` that perform
multi-step state transitions MUST verify their end-state at
the tail and hard-fail if not consistent. Silent partial-
success is unrecoverable from the user's perspective. The
two version-discovery paths (`cyrius --version` reading
VERSION file directly vs `cyrius-prompt-info tool` reading
through ~/.cyrius/bin/cycc) diverged for multiple version
bumps before anyone noticed.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_deps_global_symlink_wedge_v51117"
description = "cyrius deps stdlib_dir resolved from ~/.cyrius/lib (mutable global symlink) before v5.11.17 — wedge for the snapshot-ping-pong wipe"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "per-repo-isolation", "v5.11.17", "snapshot-ping-pong"]
---
Surfaced 2026-05-11 at v5.11.3 ship and again at v5.11.13.
Sibling agents (agnosys, mabda, phylax) run `cyriusly use
<other-version>` to test against pinned toolchains; this
flips `~/.cyrius/current` + the `~/.cyrius/bin` /
`~/.cyrius/lib` symlinks. Concurrent `cyrius deps` resolution
in this repo read from the stale snapshot and copied OLD
lib/*.cyr files BACK over in-flight edits.

== ROOT CAUSE ==

`cbt/deps.cyr::_dep_find_stdlib_dir()` resolution order
pre-v5.11.17:

  1. `~/.cyrius/lib` (the global symlink — mutable by ANY
     sibling agent on the dev box)
  2. `./lib` dev-mode fallback

For the cyrius source repo, step 1 ALWAYS won — even though
`./lib` was the actual source of truth. Any sibling-agent
version flip mid-edit triggered the copy-back.

== FIX (v5.11.17) ==

Resolution order rewritten:

  (a) `./lib` if `src/main.cyr` exists (cyrius-repo signal —
      dev-mode preferred; closes the cyrius-repo ping-pong
      since the repo reads from its own in-tree files)
  (b) `~/.cyrius/versions/<cyml-cyrius-field>/lib/` if
      cyrius.cyml's `[package].cyrius = "X.Y.Z"` field is
      set AND that version is installed (per-repo isolation
      for downstream consumers)
  (c) `~/.cyrius/lib` legacy fallback for repos without a
      cyrius pin

Plus: pinned-but-not-installed produces a hard error
(`error: cyrius.cyml pins version X.Y.Z but it is not
installed at <path>; run: cyrius install X.Y.Z`) — never
silent slide to `latest`.

== DETECTION ==

If lib/*.cyr edits revert mid-session AFTER v5.11.17 ship,
investigate:

  1. `cat ~/.cyrius/current` — if not matching `cat VERSION`,
     a sibling agent flipped it (recovery: `cyriusly use
     <correct-version>`).
  2. `find ~/Repos -maxdepth 2 -type l -name lib` — finds
     directory-level lib symlinks (the bad pattern; should
     return empty).
  3. `grep '^cyrius =' cyrius.cyml` — confirms which version
     the repo pins for deps resolution.

Pre-v5.11.17 the recovery dance was: copy lib snapshot back,
re-edit, refresh install snapshot, run check.sh. Post-fix:
just recovery for the `current` symlink flip; lib edits
themselves are protected.

== RELATED ==

See `snapshot_ping_pong_lib_corruption` (v5.8.26 surface) for
the full ping-pong shape. v5.11.17 closes one leg of the
ping-pong; v5.11.24 (per-repo isolation Part 2 — CLI version-
resolved dispatcher) and v5.11.25 (Part 3 — `cyriusly use
--global` flag) will close the others.

[[entries]]
name = "parse_nested_call_string_quirk_var_stage_hint_v51128"
description = "expected ')', got string on nested 2-arg fn-calls inside assert — diagnostic now hints at var-stage workaround"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "diagnostic", "fn-args", "var-stage", "v5.11.28", "bote", "v5.11.18"]
---
bote 2.7.1 filed an intermittent `expected ')', got string`
on the shape:

```cyrius
assert(streq(vec_get(caps_v, 0), "fetch") == 1, "msg")
```

i.e. `assert( fn( fn_with_2args(a, b) , LITERAL) == 1,
LITERAL )` — a 2-arg inner call inside another fn-call
inside `assert`. Filing was at cyrius 5.10.34. Trigger was
path-dependent: error position drifted line 187/184/178/173
as preceding lines changed, and after full chunk-by-chunk
re-add the error stopped reproducing — bote couldn't
reliably trigger it even at filing-time. Consumer
workaround: stage the inner call into a `var` first.

== PREMISE-CHECK AT v5.11.28 SLOT ENTRY ==

Reverted bote's `cap0/cap1` var-stage workaround to
recreate the inline-nested shape, compiled at both
v5.11.27 (current) AND v5.10.34 (filing version, intact
at `~/.cyrius/versions/5.10.34/bin/cc5`): clean parse
both times. Synthetic fuzz 0/28 triggers across 9
preceding-line counts (50→240 SSRF-URL asserts before
the trigger) × both versions AND 10 ident counts
(100→10000 filler fns spanning the filing's ~8700
speculation boundary). No reproduction obtainable.

== LIKELY SILENT FIX ==

Commit `f3e98a3e` (first in v5.11.18) doubled the
identifier buffer 131072 → 262144 bytes. This lines up
with the filing's Speculation 2 ("hash collision or
scan-window boundary at ~8700 idents") — capacity
doubling shifts hash-bucket distribution and probe-window
boundaries. Not proof-grade (can't verify without a
repro), but the timing fits the disappearance window.

== WHAT v5.11.28 SHIPS ==

(1) `tests/tcyr/parse_nested_call_assert.tcyr` (13 asserts)
locks the nested-call shape — bote's exact trigger,
3-deep nested variant, and the same shape preceded by a
7-assert SSRF-URL + JSON-literal stress block
approximating bote's ~180-line preceding context. Future
parser refactors that reintroduce the state leak surface
in this file.

(2) `src/common/util.cyr:425-432` adds a hint to
`ERR_EXPECT` when surface is `expected ')'` or
`expected ','` AND got-token is a string literal:

```
error:<source>:N: expected ')', got string
  hint: a string literal where ')' is expected often means
        a nested call inside a fn argument confused the
        parser; stage the inner call into a `var` first.
```

The hint sits between the diagnostic line and the existing
capacity dump, so consumers see "stage to var" before they
start re-balancing quotes or escapes. The filing
explicitly called out the misleading-diagnostic burden
("the `expected ')', got string` symptom sends consumers
down the wrong rabbit hole") as the part worth fixing
even when the underlying bug is intermittent.

== LESSON ==

A misleading diagnostic earns its own slot-share even when
the underlying bug is unreproducible. The bote filing
spent ~45 minutes of bisect before the var-stage rescue
became obvious; one hint string saves that future-time
for every consumer whose parse fails this shape, whether
the original state-leak is still live or not.

[[entries]]
name = "elf64_kernel_fixup_entry_mismatch_v51146"
description = "FIXUP's kmode==1 hard-coded entry=0x100060 (ELF32+mb1 layout) while EMITELF64_KERNEL writes code at 0x1000A8 (ELF64+mb2 layout). 0x48-byte fixup shift produced garbled serial after GRUB-EFI handoff on agnos. Fix: gate FIXUP's kmode==1 entry on _TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "fixup", "elf64", "multiboot2", "agnos", "v5.11.43", "v5.11.46", "cross-emitter-invariant"]
---
v5.11.43 shipped `EMITELF64_KERNEL` (ELF64 + multiboot2
+ EFI64-entry tag) at `src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr:873`,
gated by env var `CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1` flipping
`_TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL`. The new emitter writes code at
file offset `120 + 48 = 168` (ELF64 hdr 64 + PH64 56 +
multiboot2 48), so `e_entry = 0x100000 + 168 = 0x1000A8`.

But `FIXUP()` at the same file's line 95 hardcoded
`entry = 0x100060` for `kmode == 1` (legacy ELF32 +
multiboot1 layout: `0x100000 + 84 + 12`). Every absolute
fixup was computed with `dbase = entry + acp`, so every
baked address was 0x48 = 72 bytes earlier than the
actual symbol location. The classic shape:

```cyrius
mov rcx, 0x1001D8    ; FIXUP baked this address
                     ; ...but string lives at 0x100220
```

A `mov rcx, 0x1001D8` lands inside the multiboot2
header bytes instead of the actual `.rodata` string.
After GRUB-EFI handoff, the kernel reads garbage as
the string pointer → garbled VGA / serial output.

== EMPIRICAL CONFIRMATION (v5.11.46) ==

Compiled `programs/kernel_hello.cyr` with pre-fix
`build/cc5` (v5.11.45) + `CYRIUS_KERNEL=1
CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1`:

- ELF64 `e_entry` (header, offset 24): **`0x1000A8`** ✓
  (EMITELF64_KERNEL writes this correctly)
- `"Hello from Cyrius!"` string at file offset `0x220`
  → true VA `0x100220`
- Baked address inside `.text` at file offset `0x1FE`:
  **`0x1001D8`** = `0x100220 - 0x48` (FIXUP's bug)

The header was self-consistent (EMITELF64_KERNEL did
its own arithmetic correctly); the FIXUP-baked
addresses were 72 B off because FIXUP didn't know
about ELF64-kernel mode.

== FIX SHAPE ==

`src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr:95`:

```cyrius
if (kmode == 1) {
    entry = 0x100000 + 84 + 12;
    if (_TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL == 1) {
        entry = 0x100000 + 120 + 48;
    }
}
```

The expanded form (vs the prior literal `0x100060`)
keeps the arithmetic visibly tied to the same layout
numbers EMITELF_KERNEL + EMITELF64_KERNEL use
internally — future drift between the three sites is
grep-detectable.

== CROSS-EMITTER INVARIANT ==

The general lesson: **every code-emitter that depends
on a header-emitter's layout must consume the same
layout constants, not reinvent them**. cyrius has 5
ELF emitters (x86 user, x86 kernel ELF32+mb1, x86
kernel ELF64+mb2, aarch64 user, aarch64 kernel) and
each must agree on entry-RVA with FIXUP's absolute-
address computation. The v5.11.29-34 section-header
parity sweep was a sibling instance of the same class:
fix one emitter, miss the other 4 silently.

== REGRESSION GATE ==

`_elf64_kernel_entry_gate()` in `programs/check.cyr`
locks the invariant: compiles `kernel_hello.cyr` with
`CYRIUS_KERNEL=1 CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1`, asserts ELF64
magic + `e_entry == 0x1000A8`, locates the
`"Hello from Cyrius!"` string, computes its true VA
from `p_vaddr + file_offset`, asserts the OLD-entry-
shifted pattern (`true_va - 0x48`) is **absent**
anywhere in the binary, asserts the true-VA pattern
is present at least once (positive sanity). Negative-
test verified at slot time: pre-fix cc5 produces the
`FAIL: OLD-entry-shifted address 1049048 at file
offset 510` diagnostic with exact-match numbers.

== FILING CONTEXT — PROCESS PIN ==

This bug existed because an unauthorized cross-repo
edit hit `src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr` from an
agnosticos agent working on AGNOS boot. Patch was
technically correct; **the process violation is the
load-bearing reason for the v5.11.45 revert**, not
the technical content. Cyrius does not accept drive-by
patches from sibling-repo agents even when 100%
correct. Project leader pinned (2026-05-13): "EVEN
IF ITS 100% CORRECT THE VIOLATION IS THE PROBLEM ...
FIRABLE OFFENCE IN REAL LIFE IS MY ISSUE." The filing
form is: file as `docs/development/issues/YYYY-MM-DD-<short>.md`
in cyrius repo, not patch. Cyrius-side investigator
implements through cyrius's own slot lifecycle. See
memory pin `feedback_no_unauthorized_cross_repo_edits`.

[[entries]]
name = "relocs_stripped_vs_ovmf_v51149"
description = "OVMF firmware treats COFF Characteristics RELOCS_STRIPPED (0x0001) as 'load at exact ImageBase or fail'. UEFI Application binaries must NEVER set this bit, even with zero actual fixups. Standard UEFI loaders (rEFInd, systemd-boot, GRUB-EFI) all clear it"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "pe", "uefi", "ovmf", "efi-application", "relocs-stripped", "coff-characteristics", "v5.11.49", "gnoboot", "silent-rejection"]
---
v5.11.48 shipped EFI Application emit (`_TARGET_EFI_APPLICATION
== 1` via `CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1` env var) that produced
a structurally-correct PE32+: Subsystem=0xA, NX_COMPAT,
Data Dirs [1]/[12] zeroed, `.text` with single-byte
`ret` EEXIT. The structural gate `_efi_emit_gate()`
asserted all of those green.

At .49 entry, manual OVMF runtime smoke:

```
BdsDxe: loading Boot0002 "UEFI QEMU HARDDISK QM00001 "
        from PciRoot(0x0)/Pci(0x1F,0x2)/Sata(0x0,0xFFFF,0x0)
BdsDxe: failed to load Boot0002 "UEFI QEMU HARDDISK QM00001 "
        ...: Not Found
BdsDxe: No bootable option or device was found.
```

Firmware found the GPT disk + ESP partition + the
file at `\EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI` (verified via mdir
into the FAT image: content byte-identical to the
compile output). But BdsDxe rejected the load and
reported "Not Found" with no indication of *why*.

== ROOT CAUSE ==

COFF Characteristics field at PE header offset
`pe_off + 4 + 18`:

```python
chars = 0x0023 = 0b0000_0000_0010_0011
                            │      ││
                            │      │└─ EXECUTABLE_IMAGE (0x0002)
                            │      └── RELOCS_STRIPPED (0x0001) ← THIS
                            └──────── LARGE_ADDRESS_AWARE (0x0020)
```

Per the PE/COFF spec, `RELOCS_STRIPPED` declares
"this image's relocations are stripped — the loader
MUST load me at exactly ImageBase or fail." Cyrius
emits with `ImageBase = 0x140000000`. Under OVMF
firmware, the runtime services occupy nearby pages in
firmware's memory map; OVMF's PE loader can't allocate
0x140000000 → BdsDxe silently rejects the image →
reports "Not Found" with no further detail.

The trap: structural correctness of the PE headers
isn't enough. Standard UEFI Application binaries
(rEFInd, systemd-boot, GRUB-EFI, the EDK2 sample
apps) **never** set RELOCS_STRIPPED. Cyrius's PE
backend was setting it by default at
`src/backend/pe/emit.cyr:714` and only clearing it
when `.reloc` had content (`_pe_reloc_vsize != 0`).
A zero-fixup probe-style EFI program (no globals,
no DIR64 entries, no `.reloc`) kept RELOCS_STRIPPED
set → silent rejection on every UEFI firmware that
can't honor ImageBase.

== FIX SHAPE ==

`src/backend/pe/emit.cyr:714` — branch unconditionally
on `_TARGET_EFI_APPLICATION`:

```cyrius
var _chars = 0x0023;
if (_pe_reloc_vsize != 0) { _chars = 0x0022; }
if (_TARGET_EFI_APPLICATION == 1) { _chars = 0x0022; }
```

UEFI mode now clears RELOCS_STRIPPED regardless of
whether `.reloc` is empty. Firmware can place the
image at any free address; the probe doesn't care
(all addressing is runtime register-indirect — no
imm64 references); gnoboot will care correctly once
it has globals (covered by `.reloc` which fires
naturally when DIR64 fixups exist).

Post-fix boot trace:

```
BdsDxe: starting Boot0002 "UEFI QEMU HARDDISK QM00001 " ...
hello, uefi
BdsDxe: loading Boot0000 "BootManagerMenuApp" ...
```

Firmware loaded BOOTX64.EFI, jumped to
AddressOfEntryPoint, our inline-asm ran
`SystemTable→ConOut→OutputString(L"hello, uefi\\r\\n")`,
returned EFI_SUCCESS, firmware unwound back to boot
manager menu. **End-to-end working.**

== REGRESSION GATE ==

`_efi_ovmf_smoke_gate()` in `programs/check.cyr`
runtime-floors this: builds GPT disk + ESP + boots
qemu+OVMF with `-serial stdio` capture + greps for
`"hello, uefi"`. Graceful SKIP if qemu / parted /
mtools / OVMF firmware files absent. Negative-test
verified at slot time: with pre-.49 cc5 (RELOCS_STRIPPED
still set), gate FAILs `(sh rc=1)`; post-fix → PASS.

== LESSON FOR FUTURE EMITTER WORK ==

**Structural correctness is necessary but not
sufficient when firmware silently rejects.** OVMF's
"Not Found" was actively misleading — the firmware
was telling us the *outcome* (couldn't load) without
the *cause* (RELOCS_STRIPPED contract violation). The
only way to catch this class of bug is runtime smoke,
not structural inspection. The v5.11.49 OVMF smoke
gate is the runtime-floor that catches RELOCS_STRIPPED
or any future analogous silent-rejector.

Reference: PE/COFF spec § 5.1 (Characteristics);
UEFI 2.10 spec § 2.3.4.1 (LoadImage behavior on
non-relocatable images).

[[entries]]
name = "pe_entry_prologue_e9_jmp_zero_preserves_gpr_v51148"
description = "cyrius PE entry emits e9 00 00 00 00 (5-byte jmp +0) at AddressOfEntryPoint before user code. The jmp touches no GPRs, so RCX/RDX/R8/R9 (MS x64 first 4 args) survive into the first user instruction. Inline-asm-first is the right shape for capturing UEFI firmware-supplied args"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "pe", "entry-point", "ms-x64-abi", "uefi", "inline-asm", "v5.11.48", "register-preservation"]
---
While scoping `programs/efi_probe.cyr` at v5.11.48 entry,
the question was: where in cyrius's PE entry does user
code start, and do RCX/RDX (firmware-supplied
ImageHandle / SystemTable per the UEFI MS x64 ABI)
survive long enough for user code to capture them?

== EMPIRICAL ANSWER ==

Compiled a probe with a distinctive 4-NOP marker as
the FIRST user inline-asm:

```cyrius
kernel;
asm { 0x90; 0x90; 0x90; 0x90; }   # marker
asm { 0x48; 0x89; 0xC8; }          # mov rax, rcx
var result = 42;
```

Compiled with `CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1` and inspected
`.text` from the AddressOfEntryPoint:

```
.text first 24 bytes: e9 00 00 00 00 90 90 90 90 48 89 c8 b8 2a 00 00 00 48 b9 ...
                      └─ prologue ─┘ └─ 4 NOPs ─┘ └─ mov rax,rcx ─┘ └─ var init ...
```

The 4-NOP marker landed at `.text + 0x05` (after the
5-byte cyrius prologue `e9 00 00 00 00` = `jmp +0`).

== WHY THE PROLOGUE PRESERVES GPRS ==

`jmp rel32` writes only RIP — no flags, no GPRs, no
memory. Even when `disp32 == 0` (a 5-byte effective
no-op), it remains a register-preserving instruction.
So firmware-supplied RCX (ImageHandle), RDX
(SystemTable), R8, R9 (the MS x64 first 4 args, also
the UEFI calling convention) all survive intact into
the first byte of user code.

== WHY FN CALLS DO NOT PRESERVE GPRS ==

Cyrius's normal fn-call emit shape (MS x64 ABI under
`_TARGET_PE == 1`) emits arg-setup before the call:

```
mov rcx, <arg1>    ; clobbers ImageHandle
mov rdx, <arg2>    ; clobbers SystemTable
call <fn>
```

If user code calls *any* fn before capturing RCX/RDX,
the firmware-supplied values are lost. So "capture
RCX/RDX via inline asm before any fn call" is the
required entry shape for UEFI Applications written
in cyrius.

== THE EFI_PROBE PATTERN ==

`programs/efi_probe.cyr` (v5.11.48) uses a single
`asm { ... }` block as the entire program body —
no globals (no fixups → no DIR64 → minimal `.reloc`),
no fn calls (no GPR clobbering), runtime register-
indirect addressing only. The asm walks
`SystemTable → ConOut → OutputString` and embeds the
UTF-16 message bytes inline at the end of the block,
addressed via RIP-relative LEA with a hand-computed
displacement. ~30 bytes of code + 28 bytes of message
+ 1 NOP for 2-byte CHAR16 alignment.

This pattern is the lower-bound shape for any future
cyrius UEFI app that needs to keep firmware args
alive. gnoboot will use a heavier shape (it has
globals, fns, allocator) but the initial argument
capture has to be inline-asm-first.

== ALTERNATIVE NOT TAKEN ==

A "tinified fn prologue" approach (preserve RCX/RDX
via inline asm at entry, then call a regular fn with
the captured values) was considered but rejected:
inline-asm support for emitting a fixup-aware
`mov reg, &global` would require compiler work
(cyrius's `asm { 0xXX; }` byte-emit doesn't have a
fixup-record hook). For the .48 MVP probe, the
single-asm-block shape works because everything is
register-indirect. A future slot may add fixup-aware
inline asm if/when gnoboot needs it.

[[entries]]
name = "dll_characteristics_nx_compat_zero_fixup_efi_v51148"
description = "v5.6.31 set DllCharacteristics = 0x0160 (NX_COMPAT + DYNAMIC_BASE + HIGH_ENTROPY_VA) only when .reloc was present. Zero-fixup EFI binaries (probe-style) had DllChar = 0x0000 with no NX_COMPAT, which UEFI 2.0+ may reject. Fix: EFI mode forces NX_COMPAT bit (0x0100) regardless of .reloc presence"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "pe", "dll-characteristics", "nx-compat", "uefi", "v5.11.48", "efi-application", "memory-attributes-protocol"]
---
The v5.6.31 logic at `src/backend/pe/emit.cyr:776`:

```cyrius
var _dllc = 0;
if (_pe_reloc_vsize != 0) { _dllc = 0x0160; }
```

set DllCharacteristics to `0x0160` (NX_COMPAT 0x0100 +
DYNAMIC_BASE 0x0040 + HIGH_ENTROPY_VA 0x0020) **only**
when `.reloc` was present. For Windows-CUI consumers
with at least one global (almost always true), this
shipped the bits the security audits wanted.

For an EFI Application probe that uses no globals (no
DIR64 entries → no `.reloc` content → `_pe_reloc_vsize
== 0`), DllCharacteristics = `0x0000`. UEFI 2.0+
firmware honors NX on code pages via the Memory
Attributes Protocol; without `NX_COMPAT` (0x0100) set,
some firmware implementations refuse to invoke the
image (mark `.text` as no-execute and trap on entry).

== FIX SHAPE (v5.11.48) ==

```cyrius
var _dllc = 0;
if (_pe_reloc_vsize != 0) { _dllc = 0x0160; }
if (_TARGET_EFI_APPLICATION == 1 && _pe_reloc_vsize == 0) {
    _dllc = 0x0100;   # NX_COMPAT only
}
```

EFI mode + zero fixups now forces NX_COMPAT alone.
DYNAMIC_BASE stays off because the image is
non-relocatable (no `.reloc`); firmware honors
ImageBase = 0x140000000 directly. EFI programs with
at least one global fixup get the full `0x0160` from
the existing v5.6.31 branch (.reloc present →
relocatable → DYNAMIC_BASE meaningful → HIGH_ENTROPY_VA
meaningful too).

== CROSS-REFERENCE ==

This is the **second** silent-rejection class hit by
the v5.11.49 OVMF runtime smoke. The first was
RELOCS_STRIPPED (see `relocs_stripped_vs_ovmf_v51149`
entry above). Both required runtime testing to surface
— structural inspection alone would have missed both.

== HIGH_ENTROPY_VA NOTE ==

The v5.5.35 comment block in `pe/emit.cyr` documents
that HIGH_ENTROPY_VA (0x0020) was empirically observed
to break cc5_win.exe's stdin read on Win11 (5/5 runs).
That failure does NOT apply under UEFI — firmware
doesn't have stdin in the Windows sense; OutputString
+ runtime services are unaffected. The `0x0160` for
EFI mode with `.reloc` present is safe; the Win11
stdin issue is Windows-only.

[[entries]]
name = "build_cc5_silent_contamination_recurrence_v51144"
description = "build/cc5 has been silently overwritten with non-cyrius binaries multiple times across v5.11.x — at v5.11.28/.29 (silently fixed .30) and again at v5.11.43 (a 1.39 MB binary containing mabda/wgpu/compute-pipeline strings). install.sh --refresh-only carries the contamination into ~/.cyrius/bin/cc5. v5.11.45 added a grep gate"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "build", "contamination", "release-process", "v5.11.43", "v5.11.44", "v5.11.45", "mabda", "wgpu", "fail-fast-gate"]
---
v5.11.44 caught a P0 build artifact: the committed
`build/cc5` at v5.11.43 (commit `aad19f6a` EMIT64KERNEL)
was a **1,389,776 B binary containing mabda/wgpu/
compute-pipeline strings — NOT the cyrius compiler**.

`git diff aad19f6a -- src/ lib/` was empty;
current `src/` self-hosted byte-identical to the
canonical 821,712 B at v5.11.43 / 821,984 B at .44.
v5.11.41's cc5 fed current `src/main.cyr` also produced
821,712 B. So the source was clean; only `build/cc5`
was wrong.

== RECURRENCE PATTERN ==

Same shape contamination at v5.11.28/.29, silently
fixed at .30 with no postmortem at the time. Recurred
at .43 → caught at .44.

Most likely cause: a build script during the .43
prep cp'd another binary (a mabda-built binary that
happened to be in the build directory or in a sibling
repo's `build/`) over `build/cc5` immediately before
the commit. `install.sh --refresh-only` then carried
the contamination into `~/.cyrius/bin/cc5` +
`~/.cyrius/versions/5.11.43/bin/cc5` (both 1,389,776 B
mabda carryovers verified at .44 ship).

== WHY IT'S HARD TO DETECT NORMALLY ==

The contamination is invisible to `cyrius test` and
self-host verify if you happen to run them through
the OLD cc5 in `~/.cyrius/bin/`. The new
`build/cc5` is what's broken, but the install-snapshot
shim might re-symlink to a previous-version's cc5
if its mtime check skips the rebuild. Layered
silent-fail until somebody actually pipes
`src/main.cyr` through the committed `build/cc5`.

== FIX (v5.11.45) ==

New `_cc5_contamination_gate()` in
`programs/check.cyr` (v5.11.45 P(-1) hardening bundle).
Greps `build/cc5` for `mabda: gpu` / `wgpuDevice` /
`compute_pipeline` substrings — the v5.11.43-style
slip signature. Fails check.sh fast. Negative test
verified: tainting `build/cc5` makes the gate FAIL;
restoring → PASS.

== PROCESS PIN (CLOSEOUT-CANDIDATE) ==

The CLAUDE.md closeout pass should include a
`strings build/cc5 | grep -E "mabda|wgpu|compute_pipeline"`
check before any version-bump commits. v6.0.0
closeout will be the natural place to lift this from
"gate that catches it after commit" to "pre-commit
hook that refuses to commit a contaminated cc5".

== ADJACENT SLIP: cyrius vet AT v5.11.45 ==

The same v5.11.45 P(-1) hardening bundle caught a
parallel slip in `build/cyrc`: had been silently
replaced with the 12 KB bootstrap binary instead of
the real audit tool. install.sh `_rebuild_stale`
skipped the rebuild because the bad binary's mtime
was newer than source — same shape of silent-fail
as build/cc5's mabda contamination. Fixed by adding
an explicit `syscall(60, r)` exit to
`programs/cyrc.cyr` (vet was running twice — module
init + runtime fall-through), and raising the
scan-buffer cap 16 KB → 256 KB (was truncating reads
on 86 KB source files and falsely reporting "no
dependencies"). cyrc 12,344 B (bootstrap) → 44,672 B
(real audit tool).

== LESSON ==

`build/<binary>` mtime-based rebuild logic in
`install.sh` is a silent-fail surface. Any committed
build artifact should have a content-integrity check
(strings-grep, byte-pattern, ELF-magic — whatever is
cheap and specific) rather than relying on mtime
comparisons against source. v6.0.0 closeout should
generalize the .45 contamination gate to cover every
committed `build/<binary>` artifact, not just cc5.


[[entries]]
name = "gvar_init_order_kmode_static_v51164"
description = "kmode==1 emit order puts gvar-init AFTER PARSE_PROG which never returns. Top-level `var X = INT_LITERAL ;` declarations got their runtime store CODE-EMITTED but never EXECUTED. agnos's XHCI_CMD_TIMEOUT_SPINS = 10000000 read as 0. v5.11.64 fixed via static-init tables (gvar_initval + gvar_byte_off) baking literal values into file image at FIXUP-time."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "kmode", "gvar", "agnos", "kernel", "static-init", "fixup", "v5.11.64"]
content = """
== SYMPTOM ==

agnos kernel main body reads a top-level gvar as 0 even
though source declares `var XHCI_CMD_TIMEOUT_SPINS =
10000000;`. Helper fn `_xhci_wait_command()` consumes the
value as a spin-loop iteration count; gets 0; loops never
execute; xHCI commands time out. 10+ iron-burn debug
attempts before the compiler-side root cause surfaced.

== ROOT CAUSE ==

In `kmode==1` mode (kernel target), cyrius emit order is:

  [boot-shim asm]
  → [rest of PARSE_PROG]
  → [EMIT_GVAR_INITS]   # the runtime stores

The boot-shim asm + PARSE_PROG body produce the kernel
mainline that NEVER RETURNS (kernel main is an infinite
event loop). EMIT_GVAR_INITS emits AFTER the mainline.
The code is in the binary but unreachable.

So the runtime store sequence `mov rax, 10000000; mov
[gvar_X_addr], rax` is generated correctly — but the CPU
never executes it. The gvar reads return the BSS-zero
default instead of the declared literal.

== WHY THIS DIDN'T BITE EARLIER ==

Pre-v5.11.x agnos consumers used compile-time constants
(enum variants, #define macros) for the kernel-mainline
parameters. The first agnos slot to declare a top-level
`var X = N;` for a magic number was the xHCI sub-arc —
which surfaced the bug.

Userland binaries don't hit this: `main()` RETURNS (to
exit syscall stub); EMIT_GVAR_INITS lands BEFORE main
in userland-emit order; gvars get initialized before
main runs.

== FIX (v5.11.64) ==

PARSE_GVAR_REG detects `= INT_LITERAL ;` shape with
positive non-zero RHS:

1. Records value in new `gvar_initval` table at
   0x1EC000 (8192 × 8B; uses freed `fn_ret_sid` slot
   relocated to 0x15A000 at v5.11.19).
2. SKIPS gvar_toks registration so EMIT_GVAR_INITS
   doesn't replay the runtime store.

FIXUP populates per-var byte offsets in stable
`gvar_byte_off` mirror at 0x1B0000 (8192 × 8B prefix-sum
mirror, FIXUP-time written / EMIT-time read).

Each EMIT_* path adds `_EMIT_GVAR_STATIC_INITS(S, O, bss_o)`
after its var-area zero-fill to S64 literal bytes at the
right offsets. Result: value is in the file image at first
read; no runtime store needed.

== CROSS-ARCH COVERAGE ==

Every EMIT_* that writes a var area to file:
- x86 EMITELF_KERNEL + EMITELF64_KERNEL + EMITELF_USER +
  EMITELF_SHARED + EMITELF_OBJ
- aarch64 EMITELF + EMITELF_KERNEL
- MachO x86_64 + ARM64 (ARM64 guarded by has_data == 1)
- PE EMITPE_EXEC (handles both Windows EXE + EFI Application)

cx bytecode opts OUT via `_TARGET_CX != 1` guard in
PARSE_GVAR_REG.

== SHADOW-DECLARATION OPT-OUT ==

When FINDVAR finds an existing match for the new var name
(shadow redecl), fall through to runtime-store. Caught
during bring-up by `math_constants.tcyr`: mabda's `var
F64_TWO = 0` shadowed by math's `var F64_TWO =
0x4000_0000_0000_0000` hit EMIT_GVAR_INITS's FINDVAR-last-
match semantics — mabda's runtime-store-of-zero targeted
math's address, clobbering the new static-init bytes.

== cc5 SIZE DELTA ==

cc5 SHRANK 875,336 → 872,952 B (-2,384 B) from eliminating
runtime stores for cc5's own TS_TOK_* literal gvars in
src/frontend/ts/. Static-init eliminates the per-gvar
`mov rax, imm64; mov [addr], rax` sequence (~11 bytes per
gvar) and replaces with file-image bytes (8 bytes per gvar
in the .data section). Net savings depends on gvar count
in the compiler's own source.

== LESSON ==

Any compiler-emit ordering that places initialization
AFTER an unreachable code path is a silent-fail. The fix
shape "bake value into file image at FIXUP-time" is the
right pattern for kmode-shaped consumers (kernels, EFI
apps, anything where main never returns). The pattern is
also CHEAPER (smaller binary) than runtime stores.

For agents debugging "gvar reads 0 in kernel mode":
1. `objdump -d` the binary; find the gvar's address.
2. Check whether EMIT_GVAR_INITS sequence appears BEFORE
   or AFTER kernel main's entry. If after, static-init
   wasn't applied — confirm `gvar_initval[]` was populated.
3. Common cause: shadow-redecl where FINDVAR returned a
   different gvar's slot. Check both source files for
   the same var name at module scope.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cve_05_tok_names_mangle_magic_budget_v51165"
description = "NPOS_GUARD(S, 256) was a magic-pessimistic budget that source-identifier byte-copy loops could SILENTLY exceed. tok_names region (256 KB @ 0x60000) could overflow into adjacent regions if source identifier + separator + dest identifier > 256 bytes. Worse: 4 of 6 main_*.cyr variants had NO tok_names guard at all on the use-alias mangle path. v5.11.65 replaced magic-256 with compute-source-length-then-NPOS_GUARD across 11 sites."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "tok_names", "mangle-path", "cve-05", "security", "cross-arch", "v5.11.65"]
content = """
== SYMPTOM ==

`docs/audit/2026-04-13-security-audit.md` § CVE-05 flagged
"Heap region overlap — no guard pages" as a P1: write-side
checks for str_data / tok_names / codebuf were the fix
direction. CVE-06 covered str_data at v5.x; codebuf was
covered by EB() on both arches. The remaining gap was
tok_names.

== ROOT CAUSE ==

`NPOS_GUARD(S, need)` in `src/frontend/lex.cyr` checks
`np + need >= 261872` and exits cleanly on overflow. The
`need` argument is supposed to be a pessimistic byte count
for the pending write. 11 mangle-path call sites used
`NPOS_GUARD(S, 256)` as a MAGIC PESSIMISTIC BUDGET:

  fn BUILD_METHOD_NAME(S, si, mnoff): i64 {
      NPOS_GUARD(S, 256);                       // LIES: budget is 256
      var scratch = GNPOS(S);
      var wp = scratch;
      var snoff = L64(S + 0x18E630 + si * 8);
      var ri = 0;
      var rc = load8(S + 0x60000 + snoff + ri);
      while (rc != 0) {                         // unbounded copy
          store8(S + 0x60000 + wp, rc);
          wp = wp + 1; ri = ri + 1;
          rc = load8(S + 0x60000 + snoff + ri);
      }
      // ... separator + method name + null ...
  }

If `struct_name_len + 1 + method_name_len + 1 > 256`, the
write loop exceeds the guard's promise. Source identifiers
can be up to tok_names region cap (262144 bytes), so the
guard's pessimistic budget didn't actually cover the
realistic shape.

In practice the bug rarely bit because typical identifiers
are < 256 bytes. But it's a SILENT overflow — corruption
would land in adjacent regions (struct_*, fn_*, etc.) and
cause downstream miscompile / parse error with no
diagnostic.

== WORSE PRE-EXISTING GAP ==

The 4 non-x86 main_*.cyr variants (aarch64,
aarch64_macho, aarch64_native, cx) had **no prior
tok_names guard at all** on the use-alias path:

  // main_aarch64.cyr — pre-v5.11.65
  S64(S + 0x18FFD0 + uc * 8, ufn);
  var apos = GNPOS(S);                          // NO NPOS_GUARD CALL
  var ri = 0;
  var rc = load8(S + 0x60000 + umod + ri);
  while (rc != 0) { ... }

The parallel x86 main.cyr had the magic-256 NPOS_GUARD
that the cross-arch variants never gained — caught at the
audit-at-slot-entry sweep, not in the original v5.11.18
NPOS_GUARD introduction.

== FIX (v5.11.65) ==

Pre-walk source identifier strlen(s); pass actual sum to
NPOS_GUARD:

  fn BUILD_METHOD_NAME(S, si, mnoff): i64 {
      var snoff = L64(S + 0x18E630 + si * 8);
      var _snlen = 0;
      while (load8(S + 0x60000 + snoff + _snlen) != 0) {
          _snlen = _snlen + 1;
      }
      var _mnlen = 0;
      while (load8(S + 0x60000 + mnoff + _mnlen) != 0) {
          _mnlen = _mnlen + 1;
      }
      NPOS_GUARD(S, _snlen + 1 + _mnlen + 1);   // ACTUAL write size
      // ... rest unchanged ...
  }

NPOS_GUARD's existing region cap check (`np + need >=
261872`) now bounds the ACTUAL write surface, not a lying
magic budget.

== 11 SITES FIXED ==

- parse_decl.cyr — BUILD_METHOD_NAME (struct + method)
- parse_expr.cyr — BUILD_OP_NAME (struct + op_str)
- parse_fn.cyr — PARSE_FN_DEF module-mangle (mmod + noff)
- parse_types.cyr — variant-ctor mangle × 2 (pass 1 + 2)
- main.cyr — use-alias mangle (umod + ufn)
- main_aarch64.cyr — use-alias mangle (added guard from
  scratch)
- main_aarch64_macho.cyr — use-alias mangle (added guard
  from scratch)
- main_aarch64_native.cyr — use-alias mangle (added guard
  from scratch)
- main_cx.cyr — use-alias mangle (added guard from scratch)
- main_win.cyr — use-alias mangle

== STRUCTURAL GATE ==

`programs/check.cyr::_cve05_guard_gate()` greps the 10
affected source files for `NPOS_GUARD(S, 256)` and fails
fast on any reappearance. Negative-tested at slot bring-up
(manual sed-inject of the magic-256 pattern → gate FAIL;
restore → gate PASS). check.sh 75 → 76.

== EXISTING PRECEDENT ==

`parse_expr.cyr::EMIT_OVF_CALL` was already doing the
correct pattern at v5.6.x:

  fn EMIT_OVF_CALL(S, fname_str): i64 {
      var flen = 0;
      while (load8(fname_str + flen) != 0) { flen = flen + 1; }
      NPOS_GUARD(S, flen + 1);                  // compute then guard
      // ... rest ...
  }

The .65 fix propagated this pattern to the 11 other call
sites that hadn't adopted it.

== LESSON ==

Any "pessimistic budget" passed to a bounds-check helper
where the actual write size depends on input data is a
LATENT silent overflow. The fix shape — strlen the input
first, then call the guard with the actual size — adds
~5 LoC per site but eliminates the silent-overflow class.

For agents writing future tok_names-writing code:
1. Walk source identifier(s) with `while load8 != 0`.
2. Sum: `len_a + 1 + len_b + 1 + ...` for each component
   (separator + null terminator counts).
3. Call `NPOS_GUARD(S, actual_sum)`.

DO NOT use `NPOS_GUARD(S, MAGIC_CONSTANT)` for any write
shape where the input length depends on parser state.
The gate at `_cve05_guard_gate()` catches the magic-256
specifically; generalize the principle.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macho_syscall_number_dual_definition_61_vs_217"
description = "SYS_GETDENTS64 is defined as 217 (x86/macos) AND 61 (aarch64-Linux) in different stdlib files; the wrapper binds 61 by include order, and the macho ESYSXLAT translated only 217→344, missing 61→344 — so arm64-macOS dir-walk silently EBADF'd. 'Works in a probe, fails in the wrapper' = a duplicate global bound differently by include order, NOT codegen."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macho", "aarch64", "esysxlat", "syscall", "getdirentries", "include-order", "v6.0.63"]
content = """
**The trap**: `cyrius lib sync` (and any dir-walk — is_dir,
dir_list) returned EBADF on arm64 macOS and reported "snapshot
lib not found" on a directory that demonstrably exists. The .60
fix had already added a macho ESYSXLAT entry translating x86
getdents64 217 → Darwin getdirentries 344, and a standalone
probe calling dir_list WORKED — so it looked fixed and was
mis-filed as an "arm64 arg-corruption codegen bug."

**Why the probe lied**: `SYS_GETDENTS64` is defined in MULTIPLE
files with DIFFERENT values:
  lib/fs.cyr                    : var SYS_GETDENTS64 = 217;  (x86)
  lib/syscalls_macos.cyr        : 217
  lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr: 61   (the aarch64 number)
cyrius is "last definition wins". The wrapper (cbt/cyrius.cyr)
includes fs.cyr (217) THEN syscalls.cyr (→ aarch64 → 61), so it
binds **61**. A small probe with a different include order bound
217. Same source line, opposite value.

**Why 61 failed**: the macho ESYSXLAT chain translated x86
217→344 but had NO 61→344 entry — even though the wrapper/tools
pull the aarch64 stdlib (61). Untranslated 61 ran with a stale
x16 → garbage syscall → EBADF (-9). Instrumenting is_dir in the
wrapper showed `fd=3` (open OK) but `var sg = SYS_GETDENTS64`
loaded **61**, not 217.

**Fix** (src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr, `_TARGET_MACHO==2`
ESYSXLAT block): add `cmp x8,#61; b.ne; movz x16,#344`
(0xF100F51F / 0x54000041 / 0xD2802B10, llvm-mc-verified) beside
the 217→344 entry. Both numbers now translate, robust to
include order.

**Recognize it again**: "works in a minimal probe, fails in the
wrapper/tools" → DON'T reach for a codegen hypothesis. Print the
actual VALUE of any global the failing path reads
(`var x = THE_GLOBAL;` + write it). A duplicate global bound
differently by include order is far more likely than
context-sensitive codegen. The WRAPPER (full include set), not a
probe, is the real test. See retros/v60x.cyml + the .63 CHANGELOG.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macho_output_basename_breaks_reproducible_cmp"
description = "Mach-O embeds the output binary's basename (the ad-hoc code-signature identifier), so compiling the same source to two DIFFERENT names (fib vs fib2) yields binaries that differ by exactly the name bytes — a false 'non-reproducible'. ELF doesn't embed the name, so Linux passes and hides it. Corollary: never codesign-compare; codesign is non-deterministic."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macho", "reproducibility", "codesign", "v6.0.63", "v6.0.44"]
content = """
**The trap**: a reproducible-build check compiled the same
source twice — to `build/fib` and `build/fib2` — then compared
hashes. They differed on macOS but matched on Linux. It looked
like macOS compiler non-determinism.

**Root cause**: the Mach-O embeds the OUTPUT BASENAME (it's the
ad-hoc code-signature identifier / a build id). Two different
output names → the binaries differ by exactly those name bytes.
`cmp -l` showed a SINGLE differing byte, at the offset of the
embedded name (`1` vs `2` in the "…fib1"/"…fib2" string). ELF
does NOT embed the output name, so the same check passed on
Linux and hid the issue for the whole platform.

**Confirm in 30s**: build the same source to the SAME output
name twice → byte-identical. Build to different names → differ
by the name. The compiler is deterministic; the test was wrong.

**Two rules for any Mach-O reproducibility / self-host check**:
1. Rebuild to the SAME output name, never name1/name2.
2. Hash/compare the UNSIGNED output, never the codesigned
   artifact — `codesign` is non-deterministic (the v6.0.44
   lesson: a codesign-compare produced a false "non-reproducible"
   retraction). To RUN a Mach-O on macOS you MUST codesign it
   (AMFI), so `cp` the unsigned bytes aside BEFORE signing and
   compare those. This bit the .63 functional gate AND the macho
   self-host check in the same session (a signed r1 vs unsigned
   r2 differs by ~20 KB of signature).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_atomic_on_global_needs_8_aligned_var_base"
description = "aarch64 ldxr/stxr (what atomic_cas/atomic_fetch_add compile to) MANDATORILY fault with SIGBUS on a non-8-aligned address, even where plain access is permitted. cyrius globals were only 4-byte aligned because the aarch64 ELF var-area base inherited the 4-alignment of the code size — so atomic_cas on a GLOBAL SIGBUS'd. x86 already rounds code to 8; aarch64 rounded to 4. Fix: round code size to 8 in src/backend/aarch64/fixup.cyr."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["aarch64", "atomic", "alignment", "ldxr", "sigbus", "fixup", "var-base", "v6.0.64"]
content = """
**The trap**: `atomic_cas(&someGlobal, ...)` SIGBUSes on
aarch64 (exit 135), while the SAME call on a heap pointer
(alloc returns 8-aligned) or a stack local works fine. The
v6.0.64 thread-safe allocator's `var _alloc_lock = 0;` was
the FIRST-ever atomic op on a global, so it was the first to
trip this — every prior `atomic_cas` user (thread.cyr mutex,
atomics.tcyr) used heap/stack cells.

**Why**: aarch64 `ldxr`/`stxr` (load/store-EXCLUSIVE — what
`atomic_cas` / `atomic_fetch_add` in lib/atomic.cyr compile
to) require natural (8-byte) alignment of the address and
fault on a misaligned one EVEN when SCTLR.A=0 lets ordinary
loads be unaligned. Exclusive accesses are special-cased by
the architecture; there is no unaligned `ldxr`.

**Root cause (the layout, NOT atomic.cyr)**: cyrius globals
live in the ELF var area whose RUNTIME base is
`0x400000 + 120 + cp` (`EMITELF`) / `entry + preamble + acp`
(the fixup `dbase`), where `cp` = code size. aarch64
instructions are 4 bytes, so `cp` is only 4-aligned; when
`cp ≡ 4 (mod 8)` the whole var area is 4-aligned and EVERY
global lands at a 4-aligned address. x86's fixup rounds code
to 8 (`cp = (cp+7)&~7`) precisely so its data base is
8-aligned; aarch64's fixup rounded to 4 (`(cp+3)&~4`).

**The tell**: `&g mod 8 == 4` on aarch64 but `== 0` on x86
for the SAME source. The layout is NOT arch-shared — the
byte OFFSET of a global is identical (shared frontend) but
the BASE alignment differs between backends.

**Fix** (v6.0.64, one line, `src/backend/aarch64/fixup.cyr`):
change the post-emit `cp = (cp + 3) & (0 - 4); SCP(S, cp);`
to `(cp + 7) & (0 - 8)`. `SCP` stores it, so `EMITELF`,
`EMITELF_KERNEL`, `EMITMACHO_ARM64`, and the `dbase` global
fixups all read one consistent size via `GCP` — no
inconsistency between where a global is addressed and where
it's written. ≤4 extra zero pad bytes in `.text` (never
executed). Mach-O is unaffected (its `__DATA` is page-aligned,
independent of `cp`). This makes atomic-on-ANY-global work on
aarch64, matching x86 — not just the allocator lock.

**Recognize it again**: any new aarch64 SIGBUS (exit 135) in
a program that does an atomic on a `var`-declared global, or
a "self-host failed on pi (rc=135)" when a stale
`build/cycc_aarch64` cross-compiler emits the old 4-aligned
base. Rebuild `build/cycc_aarch64` after the fixup change.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_atomic_cas_is_not_a_full_barrier"
description = "lib/atomic.cyr's atomic_cas is documented as 'a full barrier on both arches' — TRUE on x86 (lock cmpxchg) but FALSE on aarch64, where it's plain ldxr/stxr with NO dmb. A spinlock/mutex built on it needs an EXPLICIT post-CAS atomic_fence() for the acquire barrier (thread.cyr mutex_lock has it; the v6.0.64 alloc lock initially forgot it)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["aarch64", "atomic", "memory-ordering", "acquire-barrier", "spinlock", "weak-memory", "v6.0.64"]
content = """
**The trap**: building a lock as `while (atomic_cas(m,0,1)==0){}`
and assuming the CAS gives you the acquire barrier. It does on
x86 (`lock cmpxchg` is a full barrier) but NOT on aarch64:
lib/atomic.cyr's aarch64 `atomic_cas` is plain `ldxr`/`stxr`
with NO `dmb ish` (not the `ldaxr`/`stlxr` acquire/release
forms). So critical-section loads (e.g. `_heap_ptr`) can be
observed BEFORE the lock is actually held → stale read →
corruption that only manifests under real contention on a weak
memory model (invisible on x86, invisible single-threaded).

**The canonical pattern** (lib/thread.cyr `mutex_lock`):
spin-CAS to acquire, THEN `atomic_fence()` — the comment there
notes the fence is "a cheap no-op on x86" (cmpxchg already a
barrier) but REQUIRED on aarch64. Release mirrors `mutex_unlock`:
`atomic_fence(); atomic_store(m, 0);`.

**What bit us** (v6.0.64): `_alloc_lock_acquire` trusted
atomic.cyr's "full barrier on both arches" comment and omitted
the post-CAS fence. The release side was correct (it copied
mutex_unlock). Adversarial review caught the missing acquire
fence before ship; a 4-thread contended run on real aarch64
(pi) validated the fix. atomic.cyr's "full barrier" doc is
aspirational for aarch64 — treat `atomic_cas`/`atomic_fetch_add`
as ATOMIC but UNORDERED there, and fence explicitly.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "portable_sleep_no_raw_nanosleep_off_linux"
description = "Raw syscall(35) nanosleep faults off Linux: Darwin has NO plain nanosleep BSD syscall (and on aarch64 macho, 35 COLLIDES with unlinkat), and PE never routed 35. Portable sleep = poll(NULL,0,ms) on Linux+macOS (poll 7→230 is routed on both Mach-O backends) + a kernel32 Sleep reroute on Windows. WaitForSingleObject on a process/thread pseudo-handle does NOT block as a sleep."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macho", "windows", "syscall", "nanosleep", "poll", "sleep", "esysxlat", "v6.0.65"]
content = """
**The trap**: `syscall(35, &timespec, 0)` (Linux nanosleep) in a
stdlib/consumer sleep helper works on Linux and faults or
misbehaves on every other target:
- **macOS**: XNU has no plain `nanosleep` BSD syscall (libsystem
  builds it over semaphore primitives). Worse, on aarch64-macho
  the number 35 COLLIDES — ESYSXLAT maps aarch64 `35` to
  `unlinkat`→`unlink`, so `syscall(35, ts, 0)` tries to unlink a
  garbage path. (The x86-nanosleep-35 vs aarch64-unlinkat-35
  clash is the same multiply-defined-number swamp as the .63
  getdents 61-vs-217 bug.)
- **Windows (PE)**: 35 is not a routed reroute → fault.

**The portable fix** (v6.0.65, `lib/chrono.cyr` `sleep_ms`):
- Linux + macOS: `poll(NULL, 0, ms)` — `poll` (x86 7 → Darwin 230)
  is ALREADY routed on both Mach-O backends (the .59 socket port),
  and `poll` with 0 fds + a timeout is the portable ms-granular
  sleep. Verified ≈ms-accurate on Linux + ecb + ach (the constant
  ~300ms "overhead" the first time on macOS is one-shot
  codesign/AMFI launch, not poll — baseline-subtract to confirm).
- Windows: a real kernel32 `Sleep(ms)` reroute (0xF00F), 1-arg,
  mirroring the SRWLock reroutes.
- agnos/cx: no-op (no sleep syscall / single-threaded VM).

**Windows dead-end worth knowing**: `WaitForSingleObject(handle,
ms)` on the current-PROCESS (-1) or current-THREAD (-2)
pseudo-handle returns IMMEDIATELY — it does NOT block for the
timeout (verified on cass: 0 ms). It only blocks on a genuinely
non-signaled object. So there's no "sleep via the already-routed
WaitForSingleObject" shortcut; you need a real `Sleep` reroute.

**Measurement gotcha (cmd.exe)**: `cmd /c "echo %TIME% & prog &
echo %TIME%"` prints the SAME time twice — `%TIME%` expands at
parse time (once), the `%errorlevel%` gotcha applied to TIME. Use
`cmd /v:on /c "... !TIME! ..."` or PowerShell `Measure-Command`.

**Recognize again**: any consumer doing raw `syscall(35)` for a
sleep on macOS/Windows; or a new "syscall not routed on Mach-O"
warning naming 35. The fix is always: route through
`lib/chrono.cyr sleep_ms`, never hardcode nanosleep.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_test_apple_silicon_qemu_and_codesign"
description = "cyrius test/run/bench/fuzz/doctest failed on arm64 macOS via two stacked cbt bugs: (1) run_binary took the qemu-aarch64 cross-run branch on a NATIVE Apple-Silicon host (find_tools forces _arch=AARCH64) → no qemu → execve-fail → exit 127; (2) the compiled /tmp binary ran UNsigned → AMFI SIGKILL → exit 137. A compile-and-run command on macho must exec natively (never qemu) AND ad-hoc codesign first."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cbt", "macho", "apple-silicon", "codesign", "qemu", "run_binary", "cyrius-test", "v6.0.66"]
content = """
**The trap**: `cyrius test` (and run/bench/fuzz/doctest) fail on
arm64 macOS with a bare **exit 127** and zero test output — only on
the macOS runner, unchanged by any compiler fix. Two cbt bugs stack:

**Bug 1 (the 127) — qemu mis-route.** `cbt/build.cyr run_binary`
had an `if (_arch == ARCH_AARCH64) { exec /usr/bin/qemu-aarch64 }`
branch — the x86-Linux-host path for cross-running aarch64 test
binaries under qemu-user. But `find_tools()` (cbt/core.cyr) forces
`_arch=AARCH64` on arm64-macOS so current_cc picks the native macho
cycc — which made run_binary ALSO take the qemu branch on a NATIVE
Apple-Silicon host, where there is no `/usr/bin/qemu-aarch64` →
execve fails → `sys_exit(127)`. The binary never runs (zero output;
the literal 127). Fix (v6.0.66): gate the qemu branch
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS` — a native Mach-O host execs directly.

**Bug 2 (the next domino, 137) — unsigned AMFI SIGKILL.** Even with
Bug 1 fixed, the freshly-compiled `/tmp/cyrius_test_bin` is UNsigned,
and arm64 macOS AMFI SIGKILLs unsigned binaries (128+9=137). Only
`cmd_build` ad-hoc-codesigned (v6.0.38); test/run/bench/fuzz/doctest
compiled to /tmp and ran via run_binary without signing. Fix: hoist
the codesign into a shared `_macho_codesign()` and call it INSIDE
run_binary so every run path signs (cmd_build DRY'd to it). No-op off
macOS; harmless on x86-macOS (codesign -s - rejects but unsigned runs
there anyway).

**Why it masked on ecb but not the hosted runner**: ecb (the dev
Apple-Silicon box) had qemu-user installed, and qemu happily runs a
native-arch binary too (AMFI-bypassed) — so run_binary's qemu branch
"worked" there. The hosted macos-15-arm64 runner has neither qemu nor
(post-fix-1) a signature → it exposes both bugs. Classic ecb-vs-runner
split: "run it on the ACTUAL CI target, not just the dev box."

**Recognize again**: any cbt compile-and-run command exiting 127
(qemu/exec-fail, zero output) or 137 (AMFI SIGKILL) on Apple Silicon.
The rule: on macho, exec natively (never qemu) and `_macho_codesign`
the binary before exec.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "callptr_indirect_call_frame_slot_spill_survives_win64_shadow"
description = "v6.0.70: compiler-emitted indirect calls (callptr → IR_CALL_INDIRECT, x86 `call [rbp-disp]` / aarch64 `ldr x6;blr x6`). The crux is callee-survival across ECALLPOPS: the obvious 'push callee, pop after ECALLPOPS' is WRONG on Win64 (ECALLPOPS does `sub rsp,0x20` for the shadow home → buries a stack-pushed callee) and on SysV n>6. The correct general fix is to spill the callee to a FRESH frame local before the args (rbp-relative → unaffected by ECALLPOPS's rsp moves, re-entrant for nested callptr, any arg count). aarch64 must load into x6 (x0-x5 are arg regs, x9 is the >6-arg shuffle scratch). RESOLVED at v6.0.86: on PE the callee is pushed on the stack rather than spilled to a frame slot, so nothing aliases an &-taken out-param; a real Win64 COM callee (dxgi_vram_bytes()=128 MB) verified on real cass hardware — see the next entry, callptr_callee_spill_aliases_addr_taken_local."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["callptr", "indirect-call", "ir_call_indirect", "com", "vtable", "win64", "shadow-space", "frame-slot", "ffi", "v6.0.70"]
content = """
**Compiler-emitted indirect call (callptr/IR_CALL_INDIRECT) — the
callee-survival problem is the whole game.** To call through a computed
pointer (a COM vtable method, a fn-ptr value), the callee value must be
LIVE in a register at the `call`/`blr` — but it has to survive (a) the
arg evaluation (which uses the rax/x0 accumulator) and (b) ECALLPOPS
(which marshals the pushed args into arg regs and, on Win64, `sub rsp,0x20`
for the shadow home).

**The wrong design (the understand-workflow proposed it, it's WRONG for
Win64):** push the callee FIRST (deepest), push args, ECALLPOPS, then POP
the callee into rax and `call rax`. This only works on SysV n<=6. On Win64,
ECALLPOPS does `sub rsp,0x20` AFTER draining the args, so the callee
(pushed before the args) is now BURIED 0x20 below rsp — popping gets the
shadow, not the callee. SysV n>6 also shuffles/drops slots. Verify against
ECALLPOPS (src/backend/x86/emit.cyr) before trusting any callee-survival
scheme — the shadow `sub rsp` is the trap.

**The right design (frame-slot spill):** in the callptr parser arm,
allocate a FRESH frame local (`GFLC` → `EFLSTORE` → `SFLC+1`), spill the
evaluated callee there BEFORE pushing args, then after ECALLPOPS emit
ECALLIND = `call [rbp-disp]` (x86, FF /2 mod=10 rm=101) / `ldr x6,[x29-disp]
; blr x6` (aarch64). rbp/x29-relative → immune to ECALLPOPS's rsp moves;
re-entrant (nested callptr → distinct slots, since each grabs the next GFLC
slot); works for any arg count + ABI. The frame auto-grows (EPATCHFRAME
sizes from GFLC at fn-end). IR_CALL_INDIRECT carries the spill idx (arg1);
its DCE predicates: clobbers_rax (return), def_rcx_any (caller-save),
uses_rax conservatively (matches the call cohort).

**aarch64 register: x6, not x0.** ECALLPOPS loads arg0 into x0 and uses x9
as the >6-arg memory shuffle. x6 is outside the 6 arg regs (x0-x5) and
isn't x9 → it survives ECALLPOPS for any argc. (lib/fnptr.cyr's asm uses
x9, but that's the boilerplate being replaced — don't copy x9.)

**callptr needs a function frame.** The spill is a frame local, so callptr
at TOP LEVEL is a loud compile error (top-level vars are globals → no rbp
frame; `_cur_fn_ix < 0` detects it). cx has no indirect-call bytecode op →
loud error there too. Both fail loud, never silent-wrong.

**Open gotcha (v6.0.71):** callptr to a TRIVIAL cyrius callee works on all
4 hosts (incl. Win64, loaded-callee 2/3-arg). But callptr to a REAL Win64
COM callee (a DXGI vtable method) corrupts the cyrius caller frame on cass
(RC=5 / access-violation) even when the COM call returns S_OK — NOT a slot
error (slot 7 and 12 both fail) and NOT the primitive (verified). A subtle
real-Win64-callee interaction; needs windbg on cass to pin. See
issues/2026-06-05-windows-com-vtable-real-callee-frame-corruption.md.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "pe_multi_dll_imports_byte_identical_refactor_and_cross_arch_stubs"
description = "v6.0.69: generalized the PE backend from ONE hardcoded kernel32 import descriptor to N DLLs (shell32, then dxgi). Three reusable lessons: (1) the flat ftype=4 IAT fixup assumed imp_idx==IAT-slot, so grouping imports by DLL into per-descriptor null-terminated IAT/ILT runs forced an imp_idx→physical-slot remap (_pe_iat_slot); (2) design the generalization to be BYTE-IDENTICAL at one DLL so it's a provable no-op before adding the 2nd; (3) PE-reroute emit fns live in x86/emit.cyr but parse_expr.cyr calls them inside a runtime `if (_TARGET_PE==1)` block that compiles on EVERY backend → aarch64/emit.cyr needs no-op stubs or it's FATAL under --strict."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["pe", "windows", "imports", "iat", "ffi", "shell32", "byte-identical", "cross-arch-stub", "utf16", "v6.0.69"]
content = """
**Generalizing the PE import table from 1 DLL to N (the Windows FFI
foundation).** Pre-v6.0.69 the PE backend hardcoded a single kernel32
import descriptor: `_pe_imp_dir_size = 20*2` (one descriptor + null),
literal `"kernel32.dll"`, and a FLAT IAT/ILT where imp_idx directly
indexed the thunk slot. shell32 (CommandLineToArgvW) + dxgi
(CreateDXGIFactory1) both need a non-kernel32 descriptor.

**(1) The flat ftype=4 fixup is the constraint.** `src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr`
resolves a PE IAT call as `iat_rva = idata + iat_off + imp_idx*8` — imp_idx
IS the physical slot. But a multi-DLL IAT must group each DLL's thunks into
a contiguous, per-descriptor null-terminated run (the loader walks each
descriptor's ILT to a null and fills the matching IAT run). Grouping breaks
imp_idx==slot. Fix: tag each import with a DLL id (`_pe_imp_dll`), in
`_pe_layout` walk DLL ids ascending (kernel32=0 FIRST so ExitProcess stays
at slot 0), assign physical slots per group with a null after each, and
record `_pe_iat_pos[imp_idx]` → consumed by the fixup via `_pe_iat_slot(idx)`.
The fixup FORMAT is unchanged (still a flat imp_idx); only the resolution
gains a lookup.

**(2) Byte-identical-at-N=1 is the safety proof.** Design every size/offset
so one DLL reproduces the old layout exactly: `(dll_count+1)*20` == `20*2`,
grouped order == imp_idx order, `_pe_iat_pos[i]==i`, one trailing null. Then
VERIFY before adding DLL #2: compile the same Windows program with the old
and new compiler → identical bytes (got an identical 2560-byte PE). A PE-
layout refactor that's a proven no-op at N=1 can't have regressed the 25
existing kernel32 imports; only the genuinely-new 2nd-DLL path is untested,
and the cass run covers that. This pattern (prove the generalization is
inert on the existing case, THEN extend) beats trying to eyeball RVA math.

**(3) Cross-arch stubs for x86-only emit fns.** The PE-reroute emitters
(`ECMDTOARGV_PE`, `ELOCALFREE_PE`, `ESLEEP_PE`, …) live in
`src/backend/x86/emit.cyr`. `src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr` calls them inside
a RUNTIME `if (_TARGET_PE == 1)` block (not `#ifdef`), so that code COMPILES
on the aarch64/macho/cx backends too — where the symbols don't exist. Each
needs a `fn EXXX_PE(S) { return 0; }` no-op stub in
`src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr` (the dead `_TARGET_PE` branch never runs
there). WITHOUT the stub it's a warning on a normal build but **FATAL under
--strict** — and cross-os pi surfaces the `undefined function` warning
loud. When you add ANY new PE-reroute fn, add its aarch64 stub in the SAME
slot (cross-arch-propagation-mandatory). cx has no stubs and tolerates the
warning (mirrors ESLEEP_PE) — match whatever the existing cohort does.

**Bonus — Windows argv is UTF-16.** GetCommandLineW / CommandLineToArgvW
return UTF-16LE. The v6.0.54 tokenizer down-converted ASCII-only (drop the
high byte) → non-ASCII paths corrupted silently. `_args_w2u8` (lib/args.cyr,
host-testable) does real UTF-16→UTF-8 incl. surrogate-pair decode (high
0xD800-0xDBFF + low 0xDC00-0xDFFF → one U+10000+ code point → 4-byte UTF-8).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_linux_esysxlat_gaps_native_divergence_placebo_gate"
description = "v6.0.68: the aarch64-Linux ESYSXLAT (src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr, non-macho branch) was missing x86→aarch64 translations for literal syscalls cbt issues — stat 4 (ran as io_getevents → _file_size=-1 → `cyrius deps` include-scan short-circuited → transitive stdlib silently dropped = §D1), rename 82 (=fsync → `cyrius build` never renamed its .tmp output), symlink 88 (pulsar). PLUS: main_aarch64_native.cyr (the SHIPPED native cycc) silently diverged from main_aarch64.cyr — never got the v5.9.37 auto-call-main port → bare `fn main()` builds exited 0. PLUS: the CI gate tested the cross binary, not the shipped native one (placebo)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["aarch64", "esysxlat", "syscall", "deps", "rename", "symlink", "stat", "auto-call-main", "native-vs-cross", "placebo-gate", "found-by-ports", "v6.0.68"]
content = """
**Three bugs, one goal (make the aarch64-native funcgate HARD), all in
the aarch64-Linux NATIVE toolchain. Found because §D1's deps fix let the
soft funcgate finally reach its later steps — a classic mask-chain.**

**(1) ESYSXLAT is x86-numbered everywhere; a literal `syscall(N)` in cbt
needs an aarch64 translation or it hits the WRONG aarch64 syscall.**
The aarch64 backend translates x86 syscall numbers → aarch64 via the
ESYSXLAT cmp/b.ne/movz chain (`src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`, non-macho
branch, after the `_TARGET_MACHO==2` return). It was INCOMPLETE relative
to the macho branch. `cbt` issues several syscalls as bare literals:
- `stat 4`  → aarch64 4 is `io_getevents`. `_file_size` (cbt/deps.cyr)
  → -1 → the deps transitive-include scan does `if (sz<=0) return 0`
  → peers (`syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr`, `alloc_*`, `atomic`) silently
  dropped. THIS is §D1 ("cyrius deps silently fails on aarch64").
- `rename 82` → aarch64 82 is `fsync`. `cyrius build` builds to
  `out.tmp.<pid>` then renames; rename no-op'd → the final binary never
  appeared (rc=0, no output, no error).
- `symlink 88` → aarch64 has no symlink(2). `cyrius pulsar` install
  symlinks failed.
Fix: add `stat 4→fstatat 79`, `rename 82→renameat 38`, `symlink
88→symlinkat 36`, each with the AT_FDCWD arg-shift (mirror the existing
`openat 2→56` reroute). **Ordering gotcha:** put `stat 4→79` AFTER
`getcwd 79→17`, else the `x8=79` it sets is re-caught by `cmp x8,#79`
and double-translated to getcwd. (`fcntl 72` is macho-only F_GETPATH;
aarch64-Linux uses the getcwd `#else` path, so it needs no entry.)
Assembler-verify every hand-rolled encoding (`as` + `objdump -d` on the
target, `cmp` the bytes) — the renameat/symlinkat arg-shifts are easy to
get subtly wrong.

**(2) main_aarch64_native.cyr can silently diverge from
main_aarch64.cyr.** There are TWO aarch64 entry files — the CROSS
(`main_aarch64.cyr`, x86-host) and the NATIVE (`main_aarch64_native.cyr`,
aarch64-host → the SHIPPED `build/cycc-native-aarch64`). They share the
backend but each carries its OWN copy of the entry/epilogue logic,
including the v5.9.37 auto-call-`main` trampoline (walks the fn table for
"main\\0", emits ECALLTO). The cross had it; macho got it v6.0.37, win
v6.0.54 — the NATIVE never did. So `cycc-native-aarch64` never invoked a
bare `fn main()` → every such `cyrius build` on real ARM exited with junk
(0). It still SELF-HOSTS byte-identical because the compiler's own source
`sys_exit`s explicitly (no bare `fn main`). **When you port an
entry-level emit fix, grep ALL `main_*.cyr` — the per-target forks rot
independently, and the bug shows on USER programs, never on self-host.**

**(3) A gate that tests the wrong binary is a placebo.** The CI
`aarch64-native` job built/self-hosted/funcgated `cycc_a64` =
`main_aarch64.cyr` piped through the cross — NOT the shipped
`main_aarch64_native.cyr` binary. So bug (2) stayed green for the gate's
whole life (the cross had auto-call-main). Same disease as the macOS-rot
incident: **the gate must build + run the artifact users INSTALL** (here:
`main_aarch64_native.cyr → cycc_native_a64`, self-hosted from its OWN
source). Test what ships, not a same-arch lookalike.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "param_load_pseudo_and_bootstrap_coupling"
description = "asm-block param_load(reg, idx) (v6.0.67) emits a param load at the prologue-correct disp = -(idx+1+_cur_fn_regalloc)*8 — byte-identical to the hand-rolled [rbp-N]/[x29,#-N] literals (disp8 mov / ldur). BUT: migrating a BOOTSTRAP-CRITICAL lib (atomic.cyr is in cycc via the .64 alloc lock) to a NEW compiler feature couples that lib to the feature — a pre-feature cycc can't compile it (the smoke gate catches a stale install)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["asm", "param_load", "prologue", "bootstrap", "atomic", "regalloc", "v6.0.67"]
content = """
**The feature**: `param_load(reg, idx)` inside an `asm { }` block
emits a load of the idx-th parameter from its ACTUAL prologue slot,
so inline asm stops hardcoding `[rbp-N]` / `[x29,#-N]` byte literals
that silently break when the prologue layout shifts (the recurring
sigil AES-NI / cyrius atomic.cyr coupling bug). Implementation
(src/backend/{x86,aarch64}/emit.cyr): match "param_load" in
ASM_MNEMONIC (first-8 L64 `0x6F6C5F6D61726170` + verify trailing
"ad"), parse `(reg, idx)`, map reg name → number
(`_asm_{x86,aarch64}_reg`), emit at disp = -(idx+1+_cur_fn_regalloc)*8.
x86 → `48 8B (0x45|rl<<3) disp8` (mod=01, rm=rbp); aarch64 →
`0xF8400000 | ((disp&0x1FF)<<12) | (29<<5) | reg` (ldur). Tokens:
LPAREN=10, RPAREN=11, COMMA=31 (NOT 28 — that's LBRACKET).

**Byte-identical check**: param_load with regalloc=0 emits EXACTLY
the old hand-rolled literals (`param_load(rdi,0)` → `48 8B 7D F8`;
`param_load(x0,0)` → `A0 83 5F F8`). Migrating atomic.cyr produced a
byte-identical cycc — the cleanest possible correctness proof (atomic.cyr
is compiled INTO cycc via the v6.0.64 alloc lock).

**The bootstrap-coupling gotcha (the real lesson)**: migrating a
BOOTSTRAP-CRITICAL lib to a NEW compiler feature couples that lib to
the feature. atomic.cyr/thread.cyr are in cycc's own source → after
migration they REQUIRE a param_load-capable (≥6.0.67) cycc. A stale
installed cycc (or a frozen pre-feature stage) can't compile them —
the `cyrius smoke` gate (which uses the INSTALLED cycc on a .smcyr that
includes atomic.cyr) caught exactly this with a 6.0.66 install. Safe
because: consumers get lib+cycc together via `cyrius deps`; the bootstrap
propagates param_load from the CURRENT backend (cybs does NOT compile
main.cyr directly — `cat main.cyr | cybs` fails pre-existing, so cybs is
not the main.cyr compiler); pulsar's self-host chain carries it forward.
Smoke passes once .67 is installed. **Before migrating any bootstrap-
critical lib to a new feature, weigh this coupling** — for a consumer-
only feature you can ship it WITHOUT migrating cyrius's own libs.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macos_owns_thread_pointer_register"
description = "macOS owns TPIDR_EL0 (arm64) / %fs (x86) — Darwin saves+restores it as thread state and overwrites any userspace `msr TPIDR_EL0` / arch_prctl on the FIRST preemption, so cyrius cannot durably own the thread-pointer register for thread-local storage on macOS."
detail = """
v6.0.84. Symptom: sigil's crypto-scratch bank (`cbank()` → `thread_local_get`,
lib/thread_local.cyr) SIGSEGV'd on real ecb (Apple Silicon) AFTER an ECDSA
sign — but every ingredient (sha256, hmac, fl_alloc, the secret vars, the
rfc6979 reject loop) ran clean in isolation. The .76 issue chased it to a
`&_fl_heads` freelist codegen bug via an early lldb; THAT DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG —
the `&global` adrp+add path is provably correct (EVADDR emits a fresh
adrp+add per site, FIXUP_ADRP_ADD masks 0x9F00001F so it can't corrupt the
opcode, var-address resolution is deterministic per index).

Real cause: thread_local.cyr installs a per-thread block via `msr TPIDR_EL0`
(arm64) and reads it back via `mrs TPIDR_EL0`. On Linux the kernel lets EL0
own TPIDR_EL0. On Darwin the kernel uses it for its own thread self-data and
RESTORES it on every context switch. Decisive repro on ecb: install the
block (TPIDR=0x1xxxxxxxx), run a 2-billion-iteration pure-compute loop with
ZERO syscalls, read TPIDR again → 0x200d (a Darwin thread value). Pure
preemption, no syscall, clobbers it. `cbank()` lazy-inits ONCE
(`_crypto_tls_inited` guard), so the first preemption during crypto poisons
every later `thread_local_get`. This is why it looked "context-dependent,
after a preceding svc" — it's preemption, and a long crypto op always gets
preempted.

How to recognize: lldb shows the fault at `ldr xN, [x1, x0, lsl #3]` right
after `mrs x1, TPIDR_EL0`, with x1 a tiny value (~0x2010), NOT a heap addr.

Fix: a macOS process-global slot array (no TPIDR), like the Windows TlsAlloc
path. Safe because cyrius threads don't run on macOS at all — thread.cyr is
clone(2)-only (no Darwin bsdthread path) — so crypto-scratch is
single-threaded. Since preemption (not a syscall) is the trigger, NO
"re-msr before access" scheme is reliable; you must not use the register.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macho_socketpair_dual_number_199_vs_53"
description = "socketpair's syscall number differs by macho arch: arm-macho pulls syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr → 199; x86-macho pulls syscalls_macos.cyr → 53. The ESYSXLAT must map BOTH to Darwin 135 (in their respective backends). And on the arm enum, 53 is fchmodat (already mapped) — reusing 53 there collides."
detail = """
v6.0.84 (cf. the v6.0.63 getdents64 61-vs-217 dual-number trap). There is no
syscalls_aarch64_macos.cyr: lib/syscalls.cyr routes arm-macho to
syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr (aarch64-Linux numbers) and x86-macho to
syscalls_macos.cyr (x86 numbers). So a socket added only as the x86 number is
DEAD on arm-macho. socketpair was the last untranslated one (the .59 socket
surface added socket/connect/accept/bind/listen but not socketpair): the
fork+socketpair TLS e2e (tls12_handshake.tcyr) ran svc with a stale x16 →
garbage fds → TLS_ERR_IO on every read/write. Symptom of a totally
untranslated number on Darwin: SIGSYS (exit 140 = 128+12).

Fix: aarch64-macho ESYSXLAT `cmp x8,#199; movz x16,#135` (NOT 53 — 53 is
fchmodat on the aarch64-Linux enum, already mapped to chmod with an arg-shift,
so a 53 entry both misses real socketpair AND corrupts fchmodat). x86-macho
EMACHO_SYSXLAT `_msx(S, 53, 0x2000087)` (53 is socketpair on the x86 enum,
no collision). Keep `_macho_arm_routes` in lockstep (it whitelists the
unrouted-syscall warning). Encodings: `as -arch arm64` on ecb.

RESOLVED (v6.2.24): the socket FAMILY (socket/connect/bind/listen/setsockopt)
is now mapped in the aarch64-macho ESYSXLAT block too (socket 198→97, connect
203→98, bind 200→104, listen 201→106, setsockopt 208→105 — the "aarch64-Linux
socket numbers → Darwin" branch in src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr). Real-network
sockets on arm-macho are no longer x86-only-mapped; the .84 STILL-LATENT note
is closed.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "windows_install_pillar_env_spawn_exe"
description = "Making `cyrius build` work on Windows took three wrapper layers, each invisible until run on real cass: (1) no /proc env-read on Windows; (2) the wrapper looked for `cycc` not `cycc.exe` and defaulted _home=/root; (3) compile()/run_binary use POSIX fork/dup2/execve which Win32 lacks."
detail = """
v6.0.85. The cross-OS gate had cycc.exe SELF-HOSTING green for a long time, but
`cyrius` (the wrapper) could neither find nor run it on Windows - so a real
install was unusable. Each layer surfaced only by running on cass (do NOT trust
the self-host verdict for the install pillar; see the methodology notes).

1. ENV READ. cbt/core.cyr + lib/io.cyr + _read_env all read /proc/self/environ
   (Linux) or _macho_fill_environ (macOS). Windows has NEITHER, so CYRIUS_HOME /
   USERPROFILE were invisible and _home defaulted to "/root". Fix: a new PE
   reroute syscall(0xF015) -> kernel32!GetEnvironmentVariableA (3 args:
   name->rcx, buf->rdx, size->r8; needs a 3-arg 16-byte-aligned caller
   _pe_call_3arg_aligned). Register the import in pe/emit.cyr
   (_pe_register_kernel32), emit in x86/emit.cyr, dispatch in parse_expr.cyr,
   aarch64 no-op stub (FATAL under --strict otherwise).

2. .EXE SUFFIX. current_cc() (cbt/core.cyr) resolved <home>/bin/cycc. Windows
   binaries are cycc.exe - guard the name with #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN.

3. SPAWN. compile() (cbt/build.cyr) and run_binary() invoke cycc via sys_fork +
   sys_dup2 + sys_execve - all POSIX, absent on Win32. Also SYS_RENAME(82) and
   getpid(39) are NOT PE reroutes, so the atomic tmp+rename block can't run on
   Windows. Fix: a #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN branch that spawns
   `cycc < source > output` via cmd.exe + CreateProcessW (lib/process_win.cyr
   _win_compile_spawn), writing directly to `output`. cmd does the redirect.
   The quoting that works: `cmd /s /c "<inner>"` - /s strips ONLY the outermost
   quote pair, so per-path quotes + the < > operators reach cycc intact:
   cmd /s /c ""C:\...\cycc.exe" < "src.cyr" > "out.exe""

4. .ps1 ASCII. Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads scripts as the system ANSI
   codepage, so a UTF-8 em-dash (or any multi-byte char) breaks the parser
   ("Unexpected token"). Keep install.ps1 / cass-install-gate.ps1 ASCII-only.

5. deps --lock hash: no /bin/sh or sha256sum on Windows; use
   `certutil -hashfile <p> SHA256` and parse line 2 (the 64-hex digest;
   certutil prints a header line first, \r\n endings). cbt/deps.cyr.

The install pillar is gated by scripts/cass-install-gate.{sh,ps1} (cyrius audit):
real install.ps1 -> cyrius build fn main(){return 42} -> assert exit 42 - the
Windows analog of the ecb install gate. The release tarball (build-windows-
tarball.sh, single source of truth) now ships cyrius.exe + install.ps1; it
previously shipped neither, so even a "successful" download had no wrapper.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "callptr_callee_spill_aliases_addr_taken_local"
description = "The callptr indirect-call callee-spill frame slot (v6.0.70) can ALIAS a `&`-taken local. When a function passes `&local` as a COM/FFI out-param and the callptr spills its callee to a GFLC frame slot, the two can land on the SAME rbp offset → the out-param write and the spilled callee fight → frame/regalloc corruption → AV on the NEXT indirect dispatch."
detail = """
v6.0.86. Symptom: lib/dxgi.cyr dxgi_vram_bytes() AV'd (0xC0000005) on real DXGI
hardware (cass) — factory + EnumAdapters1 worked, then the GetDesc1 dispatch
crashed. Looked GPU-specific + windbg-needed; it was neither.

cdb on cass (C:\\dbg\\cdb.exe — the debugger was present; a Program-Files-only +
minimal-ssh-PATH `where` search wrongly reported it absent) gave the fault:
  mov eax,0x50 ; pop rcx ; add rax,rcx ; mov rax,[rax]   with rcx(avtbl)=0
i.e. load64(avtbl + 10*8) [GetDesc1] with avtbl=0. avtbl = load64(pAdapter);
pAdapter pointed at zeroed mem. llvm-objdump of the PE showed the smoking gun:
  0x1843  mov [rbp-0x50], rax    ; EnumAdapters1 callptr callee-spill (cp_idx)
  0x1855  lea rax, [rbp-0x50]    ; &pAdapter (EnumAdapters1's out-param)
SAME slot. The v6.0.70 callptr spills the callee to `cp_idx = GFLC` and calls
`[rbp+disp(cp_idx)]`; here GFLC handed it the slot a `&`-taken local already
owned. The collision corrupted the frame/regalloc enough that a later
regalloc'd var (avtbl in r12) read stale 0.

Why it hid: frame-LAYOUT-dependent (which callptr collides shifts as you add/
remove locals — adding one local moved the AV from GetDesc1 to EnumAdapters1);
real-Win64-callee-only (a cyrius callee writes near rbp, a system DLL writes
near rsp + uses the out-param pointer hard); and INVISIBLE to self-host because
cycc has no callptr (callptr is a consumer/FFI feature) — so the bug shipped
green through every self-host gate. The v6.0.71 ECALLPTR_PE force-16-align was a
SEPARATE, real fix (SSE-spill alignment), not this.

Fix: on PE, don't spill the callee to a frame slot at all — push it on the
STACK (parser pushes it FIRST, below the args), then in ECALLPTR_PE pop the args
into rcx/rdx/r8/r9, `pop rax` (the callee, now on top), align + 32B shadow,
`call rax`. No frame slot → nothing can alias it. The non-PE path keeps the
frame-slot spill + ECALLIND (its ECALLPOPS shadow would bury a stack callee) —
unchanged, byte-identical. Verified: dxgi_vram_bytes()=128MB on cass UHD 600 +
callptr_real_win64 kernel32 regression=42 + 4-host self-host byte-identical.

Lesson echo: don't take a "needs a debugger we don't have / needs special
hardware" verdict as gospel — the debugger was on the box and the integrated GPU
reproduced it fine. See methodology / the hardware-directly field note.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "getenv_on_agnos_and_local_kernel_testability"
description = "getenv() on agnos walks the exec-stack envp from _agnos_init_rsp (agnos has no /proc); and the agnos kernel is locally testable under QEMU — rebuild + boot it and run a ring-3 cyrius binary, don't claim 'no agnos to test on'."
detail = """
v6.0.87. Two linked notes.

(1) getenv() on agnos. agnos has no /proc/self/environ (the Linux source). The
agnos kernel (1.43.2) stages a default envp (HOME=/, PWD=/) on the SysV exec
init stack. cyrius getenv() delegates under CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS to _agnos_getenv
in lib/args_agnos.cyr, which reads the envp from the entry init-rsp captured at
startup (the same _agnos_init_rsp that argc/argv use):
    envp[j] = load64(_agnos_init_rsp + 8 + (argc+1+j)*8)   # NUL-terminated
and matches "KEY=". The delegation lives in args_agnos.cyr (not io.cyr) because
io precedes args in the stdlib include order, so io.cyr calling _agnos_getenv is
a forward FUNCTION ref (REGFN resolves it) — a forward GLOBAL ref to
_agnos_init_rsp from io.cyr would not resolve. Every agnos program already links
args_agnos (the cycc entry emits `call _agnos_capture_rsp` for _TARGET_AGNOS), so
the global is always present.

(2) The agnos kernel is LOCALLY TESTABLE — do not claim "no agnos to run on."
   - rebuild current:  cd agnos && sh scripts/build.sh   (-> build/agnos, 1.43.2)
   - boot + run a ring-3 program under QEMU: agnos/scripts/agnsh-smoke.sh boots a
     production kernel with an ext2 rootfs whose /bin/agnsh it execs in ring 3;
     AGNSH_BIN=<your.agnos> overrides that program. exec-smoke.sh is the
     EXEC_SELFTEST disk-exec path. Both capture the serial output.
   - I verified cyrius getenv on the real kernel this way: built a probe
     (cyrius build --agnos) that prints getenv("HOME"/"PWD"/"NOPE"), ran it as
     AGNSH_BIN -> serial showed HOME=/ PWD=/ NOPE=(null). Requires
     qemu-system-x86_64 + OVMF + gnoboot built at ../gnoboot/build/.
   The kernel needs to be CURRENT (rebuild from source) — an older build/agnos
   predates the 1.43.2 envp staging and getenv would read an empty envp.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "x86_macho_byte_array_literal_self_host_invisible"
description = "The x86-macOS (Mach-O) cycc cannot compile byte-array literals (`var foo[N] = { ... };`) — a parser miscompile that is INVISIBLE to self-host because cycc's own source uses no byte-array literals, so the PARSE_GVAR_ARR path is never exercised when cycc compiles itself. The target self-hosts byte-identical while carrying a latent miscompile of a path it never runs on itself."
detail = """
v6.0.88 (surfaced by the cross-OS lib-test on ach when byte_array_literal.tcyr was
the glob). On ach the native Mach-O cycc aborts compiling the test with
`error:2358: expected ';', got '='` (line is inside the prepended stdlib includes,
not the test's byte-array lines → general parse-state corruption, x86-Mach-O only).
A trivial `syscall(60,42)` compiles+runs fine, so Mach-O execution is healthy — the
break is at parse/compile time on this build.

WHY SELF-HOST NEVER CAUGHT IT: cycc has zero `var x[N] = {...}` literals, so
PARSE_GVAR_ARR is dead during cycc's own compile. The x86-Mach-O cycc self-hosts
byte-identical (green every slot) yet miscompiles a parser path it never self-uses.
Exact "green checkmark over untested codegen" class — cf. the macOS-rot incident
(macho_syscall_number_dual_definition…) and feedback_macos_windows_ci_gate_mandatory.

VERIFY-IT-IS-PRE-EXISTING TRICK: stash the working tree to the released tag's source
on the target and rebuild the native cycc there — the released .87 fails identically,
proving a given slot's emit-layer change didn't cause a *parse* error (a codegen
change cannot). Gated behind the x86-macOS-self-compile HELD decision (Intel/EOL);
filed issues/2026-06-07-x86-macho-byte-array-literal-no-compile.md.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "byte_array_peephole_obj_aware_evrcx_not_evaddr_x1"
description = "When hoisting `&var` once for the byte-array-literal peephole, use the OBJ-AWARE base loader (_EVRCX: rip-relative LEA when _IS_OBJ, else absolute mov), NOT EVADDR_X1 (absolute-mov only). EVADDR_X1 would silently regress byte arrays compiled to relocatable objects (`-c`) / PIE on x86. aarch64/cx alias _EVRCX to their already-correct EVADDR_X1 (aarch64's has the Mach-O adrp/add branch)."
detail = """
v6.0.88. The peephole replaces the ~7-op per-byte EVADDR/EADDRA_IMM/PUSH/MOVI/POPC/
STORE8/XORAA chain with: hoist base into rcx/x1/r1 ONCE + one direct store per byte
(x86 `mov byte [rcx+disp],imm8` C6/0 disp8|disp32; aarch64 `strb w0,[x1,#imm12]`
with a >=4096 legacy fallback; cx `store8 [r1(+disp)],r0`). ~5x emit compression +
one fixup per array.

THE TRAP: the obvious "load &var into rcx" primitive EVADDR_X1 emits `mov rcx,imm64`
unconditionally — fine for a non-PIE executable, WRONG for an object/PIE target that
needs rip-relative + a relocation. The old per-byte code used EVADDR (rax) which HAS
the _IS_OBJ rip-relative branch; the naive peephole port to EVADDR_X1 drops it.
_EVRCX is the rcx-twin of EVADDR's obj-aware logic — already present in x86 emit.cyr,
used by struct-global field stores. cycc has no byte arrays → self-host byte-identical
regardless; the bug only shows in obj-mode CONSUMER codegen.

IR mode (CYRIUS_IR=1|2, opt-in) keeps the legacy IR-aware per-byte sequence so the
IR stream is unchanged; the peephole is the IR-off (default / every real build) path.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "emit_fmt_macho_before_pe_precedence"
description = "When deriving an output-format selector (`_emit_fmt`) from the _TARGET_* flags, test Mach-O BEFORE PE — matching the pre-existing EMITELF ladder's order. _TARGET_MACHO and _TARGET_PE come from INDEPENDENT, unguarded env vars (CYRIUS_MACHO / CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN; main.cyr has no mutual-exclusion guard), so the combined state is reachable. The old code let Mach-O win; flip the order and you silently change the conflicting-combo output (Mach-O → PE)."
detail = """
v6.0.89 (the _TARGET_* consolidation first bite: collapse the format fan-out +
entry-base ladder, duplicated in BOTH x86/fixup.cyr and aarch64/fixup.cyr, into
_emit_fmt(S)→{0=ELF,1=Mach-O-x86,2=Mach-O-ARM,3=PE} + _entry_base(S)).

An adversarial-review workflow caught a DRAFT _emit_fmt that checked `_TARGET_PE==1`
first: for `CYRIUS_MACHO=1 CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN=1` the old EMITELF (macho-first) emitted
Mach-O 8192B, the draft emitted PE 1536B — a real divergence, not a false alarm,
even though no sane build sets both. Fix: `if(_TARGET_MACHO==1)…;if(_TARGET_MACHO==2)
…;if(_TARGET_PE==1)…` so Mach-O wins, byte-identical for that combo too.

VERIFICATION SHAPE (this is the most fixup-fragile code — entry feeds every absolute
fixup): build .PREV-vs-.NEW compilers and diff EMITTED binaries across EVERY mode —
ELF-user, kernel ELF32+ELF64, shared, object, EFI (real source), AGNOS, PE, Mach-O
x86, Mach-O ARM, AND the Mach-O+PE conflict. A single-format self-host run only
exercises ELF; the macho/pe/kernel paths need the explicit matrix + ecb/ach/cass.
TEST-HARNESS TRAP that wasted a cycle: zsh does NOT word-split an unquoted `$var`, so
`env $combo sh -c …` with combo="A=1 B=1" set ONE malformed var and silently tested
the no-flags case — the output's ELF magic (not Mach-O) gave it away. Use literal
`env A=1 B=1 …`, and ALWAYS check the actual output bytes, not just the exit code.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "ts_children_list_reserve_then_fill_corrupts"
description = "TS parser children-list building: NEVER `off = TS_AST_CHILDREN_RESERVE(...)` then parse the elements into `off+i`. RESERVE does not advance the pool cursor, so each element's own sub-lists allocate at the SAME offset and stomp the parent (and vice versa) — a corrupt AST for any nested list. Use the deferred path: `base = TS_CST_TOP()`; parse each element and `TS_CST_PUSH(idx)` AFTER it's fully parsed; then `off = TS_CST_FLUSH(ts_base, base)`. The corruption was invisible for years because --parse-ts never reads lists back. v6.1.10."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["ts-frontend", "parser", "children-list", "ast", "TS_CST_PUSH", "TS_CST_FLUSH", "v6.1.10"]
content = """
**The trap**: every `src/frontend/ts/parse.cyr` list builder used
to do `off = TS_AST_CHILDREN_RESERVE(ts_base, N)` and then parse
its elements, writing each to `off + i`. But RESERVE only *peeks*
the pool cursor — it does NOT advance `used` (only COMMIT does). So
when an element is parsed, that element's own sub-lists allocate at
the SAME `used` (= off) and overwrite the parent's slots, and the
parent's later SETs overwrite the child's. Any nested list (call
args, block stmts, object props, JSX attrs/children, var bindings,
switch cases, ...) came out garbage.

**Why you won't see it in a quick check**: `cycc --parse-ts <f>`
exits 0 — but parse "success" only means no parse ERROR fired. It
never walks the retained AST, so the corruption is invisible until
something reads the lists. For years nothing did.

**The fix / the rule** (v6.1.10): deferred construction. Builders
push fully-parsed element indices onto the `_ts_cst` scratch stack
and flush one contiguous block at the end:

    var base = TS_CST_TOP();
    while (...) { var e = parse_element(); ...; TS_CST_PUSH(e); }
    var off = TS_CST_FLUSH(ts_base, base);   # contiguous, advances cursor
    if (off < 0) { return 0 - 1; }

An element is pushed only AFTER its subtree is parsed, so a run
stays contiguous on the stack even when nested builders push/flush
in between (each saves TS_CST_TOP() and flushes back to it). Two
interleaved lists at once (the template quasi/expr pair) collect
into locals + `TS_AST_CHILDREN_WRITE`. Only VALUE-walked builders
need this; type-only builders can stay on the old path (they can't
corrupt a value list once value FLUSHes advance the cursor past
them). Verify with `cycc --emit-js` (self-validates: non-zero on
any node revisited / out-of-range). Full backstory:
retros/v61x.cyml (v61x_ts_ast_allocator_corruption).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "ts_trailing_comma_generic_params_tsx"
description = "TS_PARSE_TYPE_PARAMS must accept a trailing comma before `>`: the TSX disambiguation form `const f = async <T,>(x) => x` writes `<T,>` (the comma stops the parser reading `<T>` as a JSX open tag). Pre-v6.1.10 the param loop demanded another IDENT after a comma, so every `.tsx` generic arrow failed to parse — including the consumer's real app.tsx. After consuming the comma, re-check for a `>`-family closer and stop. v6.1.10."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["ts-frontend", "parser", "tsx", "generics", "type-params", "v6.1.10"]
content = """
**The trap**: in `.tsx`, a bare `<T>(x) => x` is ambiguous with a
JSX element, so TypeScript's disambiguation is the trailing comma:
`<T,>(x) => x`. Cyrius's `TS_PARSE_TYPE_PARAMS` consumed the comma
and then unconditionally expected another type-param IDENT, so it
errored on the `>` — every generic arrow in a `.tsx` file failed
to parse, which is why the consumer's real `web/app.tsx` wouldn't
parse at all (`async <T,>(...)` twice).

**The fix** (v6.1.10): after `TS_PARSE_EXPECT(COMMA)` in the
param loop, peek for a closing token (`TS_TOK_GT` / `GT_GT` /
`GT_GT_GT` / `GT_EQ` / ...). If found, it's a trailing comma —
set done and stop instead of demanding another param. `<T>`
(no comma) already worked; this just makes `<T,>` work too.

Recognize it: a `.tsx` consumer reports "parses fine in tsc but
cyrius --parse-ts errors near a generic arrow." The bare `.ts`
equivalent (no JSX ambiguity, written `<T>`) parses, so it looks
JSX-specific — it's the trailing comma, not the JSX.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "new_pe_emitter_needs_aarch64_strict_stub"
description = "Any NEW PE syscall-reroute emitter `E*_PE` (added to backend/x86/emit.cyr) must also get a `fn E*_PE(S) { return 0; }` stub in backend/aarch64/emit.cyr. parse_expr.cyr references the emitter unconditionally inside its statically-dead `if (_TARGET_PE == 1)` block, so cycc's symbol resolution needs the symbol to exist when the aarch64 backend builds — a missing stub fires `warning: undefined function 'E*_PE'`, which is FATAL under `--strict`. v6.1.17."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["pe", "aarch64", "stub", "symbol-resolution", "strict", "syscall-reroute", "v6.1.17"]
content = """
**The trap**: v6.1.17 added `ENANOSLEEP_PE` to `backend/x86/emit.cyr`
(routes `nanosleep(35)` → `kernel32!Sleep`) and wired it into
`parse_expr.cyr`'s `_TARGET_PE == 1` block — both the literal
`sc_num == 35` case and the `EPE_SYSCALL_DYNAMIC` argc==3 candidate.
The x86 build + local self-host + cass all passed. But the
**aarch64** cross-build fired `warning: undefined function
'ENANOSLEEP_PE'` (twice). It only *warns* (non-fatal) on a plain
build — so a forgotten stub sails through unless you build `--strict`
(CI / `cyrius build --strict`), where it's a hard error.

**Why**: `parse_expr.cyr` calls every `E*_PE` emitter unconditionally
inside `if (_TARGET_PE == 1)`. On aarch64 `_TARGET_PE` is always 0 so
the branch is statically dead at runtime, but cycc resolves symbols
before that's known, so the fn must exist. `backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`
therefore carries a `return 0` stub for the ENTIRE PE cohort
(EWRITE_PE, EGETTICKS_PE, ECREATEDIR_PE, EPE_SYSCALL_DYNAMIC, …). A
new emitter is the one piece you forget.

**The fix**: add `fn ENANOSLEEP_PE(S): i64 { return 0; }` to the
aarch64 cohort. (The `cx` backend does NOT stub these — it already
emits the undefined-fn warning for the whole cohort and builds anyway,
because it isn't a `--strict` target; matching that means you leave cx
alone, you don't stub only your one new fn there.)

Recognize it: a new `E*_PE` lands, x86 + Windows are green, then a
`--strict` aarch64/macOS build (or CI) breaks on "undefined function."
Grep `backend/aarch64/emit.cyr` for the existing `_PE) { return 0; }`
cohort and add yours next to `EPE_SYSCALL_DYNAMIC`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "per_fn_local_tables_indexed_by_slot_not_var"
description = "The four per-fn local tables (fn_local_names / fn_local_depths / fn_local_types / fn_local_slice) are indexed by SFLC, which counts stack-frame SLOTS, NOT vars — a `stack var buf[N]` registers N/8 filler slots — so any frame larger than ~2 KB silently overran the old 256/576-entry tables (CVE-24). v6.1.40 relocated all four to the heap top at 16384 slots each + added a loud SFLC cap."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "local-tables", "sflc", "heap-cap", "overflow", "cve-24", "v6.1.40"]
content = """
**The trap (CVE-24)**: cyrius's four per-function local tables —
`fn_local_names`, `fn_local_depths`, `fn_local_types`,
`fn_local_slice` — are indexed by `SFLC` (the stack-frame local
count). `SFLC` counts stack-frame **SLOTS**, not user-visible
variables. A `stack var buf[N]` (a byte array on the frame) doesn't
register as one entry — it registers **N/8 filler slots** (one per
8-byte slot the array occupies). So a single `stack var buf[2048]`
burns 256 table entries by itself.

The old tables were sized 256 / 576 entries. Any function whose
frame exceeded ~2 KB of slot-arrays silently ran `SFLC` past the
table end and wrote past the region — a heap overrun with no
diagnostic. Self-host never tripped it (cycc's own fns stay small),
so it was invisible until a consumer with a large stack buffer (the
daimon report) hit it.

**The fix (v6.1.40)**: relocate all four tables to the heap top,
each sized **16384 slots**:

  - fn_local_names   @ 0x5D9D000
  - fn_local_depths  @ 0x5DBD000
  - fn_local_types   @ 0x5DDD000
  - fn_local_slice   @ 0x5DFD000

plus a LOUD `SFLC` cap check (error + exit, not silent truncation)
so a future overrun reports the slot count and the file to raise,
instead of corrupting the heap.

**The footgun behind the report — local vs global array sizing**:
the array-size convention differs by scope, which is exactly what
let the frame grow past expectation:

  - **local** `var a[N]` = **N BYTES**, rounded UP to 8 (so
    `var a[5]` still consumes one 8-byte slot; `var a[2048]`
    consumes 256 slots).
  - **global** `var a[N]` = **N i64 SLOTS** (N*8 bytes).

So a buffer that looks identical in source (`var a[2048]`) is 2 KB
on the stack but 16 KB in `.bss` as a global. Size local arrays by
BYTE count; size globals by slot count. Mixing the two mental
models is how the daimon frame quietly grew past the old cap.

**Recognize again**: any "large stack buffer corrupts compiler
state / heap" shape, or a consumer whose fn has a multi-KB
`stack var buf[N]`. Check `SFLC` against the table cap; the slot
count is `frame_bytes / 8`, not the var count. Related:
`large_fn_local_var_array_is_bss_not_stack` (the dual — big
fn-local arrays land in `.bss`, not the stack frame).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "phase0_growable_grow_path_self_host_invisible"
description = "The v6.2.0 growable pressure tables (fixup_tbl, fn-tables, codebuf) never grow during cycc's own self-compile — cycc is at 6486/8192 fns and ~1 MB of a 3 MB codebuf, so the realloc / rehash / relocation code is dead on the self-host path AND on every check.sh test. A bug in the grow/rehash logic is therefore invisible to byte-identical self-host. The ONLY thing that catches it is a forced-grow test: shrink the initial cap, self-compile, and cmp against the no-grow reference."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["growable", "self-host-invisible", "fn-tables", "codebuf", "fixup", "testing", "v6.2.0"]
content = """
**The trap**: Phase 0 (v6.2.0) made three fixed tables growable —
`fixup_tbl` (`_fixup_grow`), the 16 fn-tables + 2 hashes
(`_fnt_grow`, with a name-hash rehash), and `codebuf`
(`_codebuf_grow`, off-heap relocate). All three GROW paths are
**cold on every path the test suite walks**: cycc self-compiles
with ~6486 functions (the cap starts at 8192) and ~1 MB of
generated code (the codebuf cap starts at 3 MB), so neither
`_fnt_grow` nor `_codebuf_grow` ever fires. A subtle bug — a wrong
copy length, a stale base pointer after relocation, a botched
rehash — produces a **byte-identical self-host and a green
check.sh** while silently corrupting any real consumer that does
cross the cap.

**The only test that exercises it**: artificially shrink the
initial cap so a NORMAL self-compile is forced through many
grows, then prove the output is unchanged:

  # fn-tables: set `_fnt_cap = 64` / `_fnt_hash_mask = 63` in the driver,
  # build cycc_small, then:
  cat src/main.cyr | ./cycc_small > out      # forces ~7 grows + rehashes
  cat src/main.cyr | ./build/cycc > ref      # no-grow reference (cap 8192)
  cmp out ref                                # MUST be identical

  # codebuf: `_codebuf_cap = 8192` → ~7 relocations; same cmp.

Run the forced-grow test on **both** x86 AND aarch64 — the
aarch64 start-hash build + `live[]` bitmap are mirrored code; the
x86 forced-grow does not cover them. The aarch64 variant: shrink
the cap in `main_aarch64.cyr` and run `cross-os-selfhost.sh pi`
(it self-hosts on real ARM, which forces the grow there).

**Recognize again**: ANY migration of a fixed cap/table to
growable, where the compiler's own usage stays under the cap.
"Self-host is byte-identical" proves the RELOCATION-equivalent
(no-grow) path; it says nothing about the grow path. Mandatory
forced-grow + differential-corpus before trusting it. Sibling
trap: `phase0_growable_relocation_and_scattered_cap`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "phase0_growable_relocation_and_scattered_cap"
description = "Two mechanics that bite when migrating cycc's fixed tables to growable: (1) a relocating buffer (codebuf) is only safe if every access re-derives from the base gvar and never holds a captured pointer across an EB — and the disp32 patch sites do an 8-byte read-modify-write that bypasses EB's cap-check, so the grow needs a guard band; (2) the 8192 fn-cap was a magic literal scattered across 6 subsystems and textually colliding with 5 UNRELATED 8192-capped tables, so the swap had to be surgical per-context, never a global replace."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["growable", "codebuf", "fn-tables", "relocation", "magic-number", "v6.2.0"]
content = """
**Relocation safety (codebuf)**: `_codebuf_grow` `alloc()`s a new
buffer and repoints `_codebuf_base`. That is only correct because
EVERY codebuf access re-derives `_codebuf_base + offset` fresh:
- the disp32 patch sites (`var a = _codebuf_base + GCP(S); S64(a, …)`)
  capture-and-store with NO `EB` in between, so no grow can run
  between the capture and the use;
- the DSE/LASE/regalloc peephole passes read+patch already-emitted
  code AFTER all emit, when the buffer is stable.
A pattern that captured `_codebuf_base + cp` and then called `EB`
(which can grow → relocate) before using it would dereference the
freed old buffer. There is none today — keep it that way.

The disp32 stores also do an 8-byte read-modify-write at `cp`
(`S64(a, (L64(a) >> 32 << 32) | disp)`), writing 4 bytes PAST the
4-byte slot, and they bypass `EB`'s cap-check entirely. So
`_codebuf_grow` triggers at `cp >= _codebuf_cap - 64` (a 64-byte
guard band), not at the exact cap, or the last RMW before a grow
would write past the buffer end. The cx bytecode backend has its
OWN codebuf at `0x54A000` (separate region, still fixed 512 KB) —
it is NOT `_codebuf_base`.

**The scattered magic number (fn-tables)**: `8192` (the fn-cap)
was hard-coded in ~6 independent places — `REGFN`'s cap-check,
the `fn_name_hash` + `fn_start_hash` probe/mask/clear bounds, the
`live[1024]` DCE bitmap, and the 85 %-full warning — with NO single
source of truth. Worse, the literal `8192` also appears in 5
UNRELATED capped tables (var table, IR pool, PE reloc, struct
field pool, the TS scratch stack). A blind `8192 → _fnt_cap` would
have corrupted those. The migration was surgical per-context:
`& 8191 → & _fnt_hash_mask` (hash masks only), `probes/sh_clr <
8192 → _fnt_cap` (hash bounds only), `live[1024] → live[4096]`
(the DCE bitmap, sized to the 32768 ceiling), REGFN's check → the
grow call. Everything now reads one `_fnt_cap`.

**Recognize again**: any "raise/remove a fixed cap" task where the
cap value is a bare literal. Grep first; a literal that doubles as
an unrelated table's size will silently break that table.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "agnos_target_stub_predicated_on_obsolete_kernel_surface"
description = "A `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS return 0;` stub written against an EARLIER kernel ABI silently rots once the kernel grows the missing syscall — the stub keeps 'working' (returns a deterministic 0) so nothing fails loud, while every consumer re-rolls a direct-syscall workaround. Re-check the live kernel ABI before trusting a frozen-surface comment."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["agnos", "stub", "syscall", "chrono", "stale", "v6.2.6"]
content = """
**The trap**: `lib/chrono.cyr`'s AGNOS branches for monotonic time
(`clock_now_ns()`) and `sleep_ms()` were fixed-`return 0` / no-op
stubs, with a comment justifying them — "agnos has no monotonic /
sleep syscall in the frozen 0-33 surface (228=clock_gettime,
35=nanosleep are out of range)." True when WRITTEN (v6.0.56). But
the agnos kernel grew `uptime_ms`#40 + `sleep_ms`#41 at 1.43.x (the
cyrius-doom frame clock / pacing primitives). The stubs never
faulted — `clock_now_ms()` just always returned 0 and `sleep_ms()`
busy-spun — so no gate, no smoke, no self-host caught it. It
surfaced only when the v6.2.5 `dig`/`yo` net-tool ports needed real
timing and had to call `syscall(40)`/`syscall(41)` DIRECTLY,
bypassing chrono. Classic "found by ports": the placebo that returns
a plausible value is more dangerous than one that crashes.

**The fix** (v6.2.6): add `sys_uptime_ms`#40 / `sys_sleep_ms`#41
wrappers (new `SysNrAgnosTimer` enum) to
`lib/syscalls_x86_64_agnos.cyr`; point chrono's agnos branches at
them (`clock_now_ns()` = `sys_uptime_ms()*1e6`; `sleep_ms()` agnos
branch calls `sys_sleep_ms(ms)`, guard `ms<=0`). Wall-clock#46 was
already correctly bound at v6.2.3 — this was the monotonic+sleep
counterpart it didn't cover. Extended `agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh`
with a chrono monotonic/sleep probe so a re-stub is now caught.

**Recognize again**: any `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS return 0;`
(or no-op) guarded by a comment naming a kernel version / "frozen
0-N surface." That comment is a timestamp, not an invariant — the
kernel ABI doc (`agnos/docs/development/agnos-userland-abi.md`) is
canonical and grows. Grep the doc for the capability before
trusting the stub; a stub that returns a deterministic 0 will not
fail loud when the assumption expires.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "agnos_server_socket_accept_commits_v6222"
description = "AGNOS gained inbound TCP at kernel 1.45.5/.6 (sock_listen#56 / sock_accept#57); cyrius v6.2.22 wires lib/net.cyr's sock_bind/listen/accept via a listen<->fd adapter. The non-obvious trap: sock_accept(#57) COMMITS the accept kernel-side (the conn is ESTABLISHED + marked accepted, never re-handed-out), so if the LOCAL 8-slot fd table is full you MUST reap the returned conn_id (#50) or it leaks a kernel slot forever. Also: a consumer's 'undefined CLONE_VM on --agnos' is almost always STALE vendored thread.cyr, not a lib bug."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["agnos", "sockets", "net.cyr", "syscall", "v6.2.22", "thread.cyr", "stale-vendored"]
content = """
**AGNOS server sockets (v6.2.22).** The kernel exposes inbound TCP as two
1-arg syscalls: `sock_listen(port)#56` (merges bind+listen -> a listen_id
0..7) and `sock_accept(listen_id)#57` (non-blocking -> next ESTABLISHED
conn_id 0..7, or -1 = WOULD_BLOCK). `lib/net.cyr`'s `sock_bind`/`sock_listen`/
`sock_accept` route onto these on `--agnos` via a **listen<->fd adapter** in
`lib/syscalls_x86_64_agnos.cyr` (parallel to the v6.2.3 conn<->fd adapter):
bind stashes the port, listen issues #56, accept issues #57 + wraps the
conn_id in a fresh tagged fd riding the existing #48/#49/#50 path. Both are
1-arg, so NO codegen change.

**Trap 1 — #57 COMMITS the accept.** Unlike a peek, `tcp_accept` sets the
kernel conn's accepted-bit and never re-hands it out (and `tcp_close` on the
listener skips accepted children). So in `sock_accept`, after #57 returns a
conn_id, if your local fd allocation fails (the cyrius 8-slot table is full:
1 listen + 7 conns), you MUST `sys_sock_close(cid)` (#50) BEFORE returning the
error — otherwise that kernel conn slot leaks forever and, over the server's
life, the kernel's 8-slot table is exhausted. (The client #47 path has no such
window — the fd is allocated BEFORE the kernel call.) Caught by adversarial
review, not the compile-only gate.

**Trap 2 — clear ALL slot tables on close.** A bind-without-listen (or bind
then a FAILED listen) close must zero `listen_port` too, or a recycled fd-slot
inherits a stale port and silently mis-listens. `sys_close` clears conn_tbl +
listen_tbl + listen_port uniformly on every socket-fd close.

**Trap 3 — 'undefined CLONE_VM on --agnos' is NOT a lib bug.** `lib/thread.cyr`
self-guards agnos since v6.2.3 (`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` -> thread_agnos.cyr
no-op mutexes; the `mmap.cyr`/CLONE_VM body is `#ifndef`-gated). A stdlib
consumer hitting `undefined variable 'CLONE_VM'` on `--agnos` is almost always
running a STALE vendored `thread.cyr` (pre-v6.2.3) — the fix is `cyrius deps`
to refresh, NOT a compiler/lib change. (This is why sankoch 2.4.4's lock no-op
is self-sufficiency, not a crash fix — CHANGELOG 6.2.22.)
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macho_sysxlat_imm8_signext_getdents_v6234"
description = "EMACHO_SYSXLAT's _msx helper emits `cmp rax, imm8` (48 83 F8 ib), which SIGN-EXTENDS the byte. Any Linux syscall number 128..255 routed through _msx silently never matches (217=0xD9 -> -39), so the BSD translation is skipped and the raw Linux number reaches the Darwin `syscall` -> SIGSYS. getdents64(217) hit this on x86-macOS; use _msx32 (imm32 cmp) for any number > 127. Self-host-blind (cycc never lists a dir) — found by ports. v6.2.34."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macho", "x86", "EMACHO_SYSXLAT", "syscall", "imm8", "sign-extend", "getdents", "self-host-blind", "v6.2.34"]
content = """
**x86-macOS syscall translation: `_msx` imm8 sign-extension (v6.2.34).**
`EMACHO_SYSXLAT` (src/backend/x86/emit.cyr) rewrites each Linux syscall number
in `rax` to its Darwin BSD number at emit time, one `_msx(lin, classed)` entry
per syscall. `_msx` hand-rolls `cmp rax, lin` as `48 83 F8 ib` — the `83 /7 ib`
form, whose immediate is an **8-bit value sign-extended to 64 bits**. So for any
`lin` in 128..255 the compare is against a NEGATIVE number: `getdents64` 217 =
`0xD9` sign-extends to `-39`, `cmp rax, -39` never matches `rax = 217`, the
`mov rax, 0x2000158` (getdirentries64 344) is skipped, and the **raw Linux 217**
reaches the Darwin `syscall` instruction -> `SIGSYS` (exit 140) on every
directory listing.

**Rule:** route any syscall number > 127 through `_msx32` (`48 3D id` =
`cmp rax, imm32`), exactly as getrandom 318 already did. `_msx` is safe ONLY for
lin <= 127. 217 was the lone offender in the 128..255 gap.

**Why it survived for ~minors:** purely **self-host-blind** — cycc reads its
sources by path and never walks a directory, so the self-host gate (even on
real macOS) cannot exercise getdents64. The aarch64 backend translated 217
correctly all along (wide-immediate `cmp x8,#217`), so it was an x86-only gap.
Caught by a fresh consumer install + a backend imm-width review; PROVEN on real
x86 Darwin (`ach`): pre-fix binary SIGSYS (exit 140), post-fix lists the dir
(exit 3). The textbook "found by ports" / "self-host is a placebo for dir ops"
failure class. Closes live half of 2026-06-02-macos-x86-release-no-compiler.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "mod_reserved_keyword_and_cve32_traversal_replication_v6250"
description = "Two traps from the v6.2.50 Phase C modular resolver: (1) `mod` is a RESERVED cyrius keyword — a var/param named `mod` fails to parse ('expected identifier, got reserved keyword'); use `submod`. (2) A NEW filesystem-write path that takes dep-controlled names MUST replicate the existing path-traversal guard — _dep_pull_submodule added sys_unlink+copy on `lib/<pkg>_<submod>.cyr` but the guard lived only in _dep_copy_file (CVE-04), and the unlink ran BEFORE it -> arbitrary .cyr deletion from a malicious index.cyml (CVE-32, fixed v6.2.51). Validate names at EVERY ingestion point before any fs op."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cyrius.cyml", "deps", "distlib", "modular", "reserved-keyword", "path-traversal", "CVE-32", "security", "v6.2.50", "v6.2.51"]
content = """
**Two traps from the Phase C modular resolver (v6.2.50/.51).**

(1) `mod` is a RESERVED cyrius keyword. Naming a variable or fn parameter `mod`
fails to compile: `expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'mod' (cannot be
used as an identifier)`. Hit while writing `_dep_pull_submodule(dep_src, pkg,
mod, ...)` in cbt/deps.cyr — renamed the param to `submod`. (Same class as
`secret`/`pub`/`shared`.) Grep the keyword set before naming dep/module vars.

(2) **Replicate the traversal guard at EVERY new fs-write ingestion point.**
The .50 `--modular` resolver added a fresh write path: `_dep_pull_submodule`
does `sys_unlink(dst)` + `_dep_copy_file(src, dst)` where
`dst = lib/<pkg>_<submod>.cyr` and `submod` comes from a dep-controlled
`dist/<pkg>/index.cyml` (the sibling-dep list). The project already had the
CVE-04 guard — but it lived INSIDE `_dep_copy_file`, and the new `sys_unlink`
ran BEFORE that call. So a malicious index sibling like `a/../../../x` escaped
lib/ and `sys_unlink`'d an arbitrary `.cyr` during automatic `cyrius deps`
(CVE-32, P1). This is the exact dep-resolver injection class hardened at
v6.1.33 (CVE-14/15/16) that the new path silently failed to replicate.

Fix (v6.2.51): a shared `_dep_reject_unsafe_name(name)` (reject any `/` or `..`)
called at ALL ingestion points — the sub-module name at the TOP of
`_dep_pull_submodule` (before any fs op), each index-returned `lib:<leaf>`, and
the producer-side `pkg_name` in `_distlib_modular_emit`. Regression:
`_deps_modular_traversal_gate`. LESSON: when you add a code path that turns
externally-controlled strings into filesystem paths, the guard goes at the new
path's first op — an existing guard one call deeper does not cover an earlier
unlink/open. Audit "every place dep-controlled data becomes a path," not "the
copy helper."
"""

[[entries]]
name = "forward_call_prescan_and_monomorph_substrate"
description = "v6.3.5 revived two generics prerequisites and exposed three non-obvious facts about cycc's driver structure. (1) The driver is 3-PASS: pass-1 registers structs/enums/globals (and now fn signatures), pass-2 emits fn BODIES (PARSE_FN_DEF), pass-3 (PARSE_PROG) emits top-level statements. So the CO-01 'forward call gets mask 0' bug only bites a call INSIDE a fn body (pass-2) to a fn defined LATER — a top-level call always sees full masks because pass-2 finished all defs before pass-3 runs. A test with a top-level forward call proves nothing. (2) Pass-1 STOPS at the first top-level statement (catch-all `scan = 0` in main.cyr's pass-1 loop), so the prescan — like struct/global registration — only reaches declarations BEFORE the first statement. The standard decls-before-statements (C-style: fns first, `main()`/asserts last) order is fully covered; a fn defined after `alloc_init()` is NOT prescanned. (3) The inline-replay EMIT path (PARSE_FNCALL, the `GFINL != 0` branch) was never removed — only the body-token CAPTURE was gated off (`_INLINE_OK = 0` per backend). Re-enabling capture (v6.3.5's opt-in `_MONOMORPH_OK` / `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=1`) revives value-inlining with no path rewrite."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["prescan", "forward-call", "abi", "3-pass", "fn-table", "monomorphization", "inline-replay", "co-01", "ar-01", "v6.3.5"]
content = """
**CO-01 — forward-call ABI prescan.** A call lexically before its callee's definition used to
auto-register the callee with mask 0 (PARSE_FNCALL's `if (fi < 0) { fi = REGFN }`), silently dropping
`: Str` auto-coercion, SIMD-arg routing, and the struct-return retptr ABI. Fix: a pass-1
`_prescan_fn_sig` (wired into all 7 forks' pass-1 in place of the inline fn-skip) REGFNs every
top-level fn and records its masks/count/variadic/return-type before pass-2 emits any call. Shared
`_classify_param_type` / `_classify_return_type` (extracted from PARSE_FN_DEF, differential-verified
byte-identical) keep prescan and definition in lockstep.

**The cursor-reset trick.** `_prescan_fn_sig` extracts metadata on a THROWAWAY cursor (advancing
through the signature however the scan needs), then `STI(S, start_ti)` and replays the verbatim
pre-CO-01 skip (`_prescan_skip_fn`). So pass-1's NET token-cursor advance is byte-identical to before —
the only new effect is the registration + mask writes (state, not cursor). This is why the prescan
doesn't perturb pass-1's downstream parsing.

**The `)` gotcha (cost hours).** `_prescan_params` originally returned with the cursor still on `)`
(it never consumed the closing paren), so the return-type block in `_prescan_fn_sig` saw `)` instead
of `:` and NEVER classified `: Struct` returns → `SFRS` stayed 0 → a forward call to a struct-returning
fn kept the scalar-return ABI → segfault. Fix: `if (PEEKT(S) == 11) { STI(S, GTI(S) + 1); }` at the end
of `_prescan_params`. Lesson: when a helper is followed by a sibling scan, it must leave the cursor
where the sibling expects it.

**How to debug 'the prescan is a no-op'.** Symptom: forward call still wrong, prescan-on == prescan-off
binary. First check whether the fn was even REACHED by pass-1 (instrument the prescan, or print GFNC at
pass-2 start) — if the callee is defined after a top-level statement, pass-1 stopped before it (fact 2
above). Then check the CALL site resolves the prescanned fi (FINDFN must find it) vs re-REGFNing a
fresh mask-0 slot.

**AR-01 — monomorph substrate.** `_MONOMORPH_OK` (util.cyr) ORs into PARSE_FN_DEF's two capture gates.
**As of v6.4.0 the default is 1 (util.cyr:273) — generics are DEFAULT-ON; `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0` opts
out** (this was `default 0` / `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=1`-opt-in when this entry was first written at v6.3.5).
The default-on flip was NOT a one-liner: the capture arm was DECOUPLED from general inlining by
qualifying it with `GFTP(S, fi) != 0` (only generic fns are captured; see
`monomorph_default_on_flip_gftp_decouple`) so the flip doesn't re-enable value-inlining for every fn.
The per-backend `_INLINE_OK = 0` default is untouched — x86 was disabled for code SIZE at v1.11.3,
aarch64 for a metadata overlap since relocated to S+0x9EA000. When the gate fires the intact replay
emits per-call-site value-substituted code. `_monomorph_substrate_gate` was **repurposed** at the flip:
it originally proved the substrate ACTIVE (compile a fixture ±flag → both exit 42, binaries differ) and
now proves the DECOUPLE holds (non-generic fns stay un-inlined under flag-on). TYPE substitution
(generics) builds on this in v6.3.7 — the substrate substitutes VALUES today, not types yet.

**Filed alongside:** `var p = mk()` (inferred local type from a struct-returning call) segfaults even
NON-forward — a pre-existing var-decl/retptr defect, not CO-01. Use `var p: T = mk()`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "closure_inline_emit_shares_enclosing_fn_state"
description = "A cyrius closure literal `|params| body` is an anonymous fn (`__clNNNNN`) that PARSE_FACTOR emits INLINE inside the enclosing fn's code range (an EJMP0 jumps over it) while sharing the enclosing fn's per-fn codegen globals AND the per-fn local-slot tables. That sharing is the root of four v6.3.7 closure bugs: (1) the closure inherited `_cur_fn_regalloc` from an auto-regalloc'd enclosing fn, so ESTOREPARM shifted its param slots past phantom callee-saved saves and its locals were register-allocated to stale callee regs (`|x| x` returned 0); (2) the closure called ESUBRSP but never EPATCHFRAME, so its `sub rsp,imm32` kept the 0 placeholder — a 0-byte frame whose first body push clobbered param slot 0; (3) the closure reset SFLC(0) and wrote its params/locals into the SHARED name/depth/type/slice tables (S+0x5D9D000 / 0x5DBD000 / 0x5DDD000 / 0x5DFD000) starting at slot 0, overwriting the enclosing fn's locals at those slots (`var c0` shadowed by a later `|x|` became 'undefined variable'); (4) the enclosing fn's regalloc post-pass (the byte-scan at parse_fn.cyr `if (_cur_fn_regalloc > 0)`) scanned the INLINE closure bytes and NOP-patched/reallocated them → SIGILL when the closure was called."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["closure", "lambda", "codegen", "regalloc", "inline-emit", "local-tables", "per-fn-state", "frame-patch", "v6.3.7"]
content = """
**A closure is a nested fn sharing the parent's mutable codegen state.** cyrius
has no general nested fns, so the closure path (`PARSE_FACTOR`, token 35 `|` or
54 `||`) is the only place a fn body is emitted WHILE another fn is mid-emit. It
reuses the parent's per-fn globals and per-fn slot tables, so it must save / reset
/ restore ALL of them — not just `in_fn` / `flc` / `sdep`.

**The four fixes (v6.3.7), all in `parse_expr.cyr`'s closure branch:**

1. **Per-fn codegen state.** Save + zero `_cur_fn_regalloc`, `_cur_fn_ret_stash`,
   `_cur_fn_ret_sid`, `_cur_fn_ret_scalar`, `_cur_fn_naked`, set `_cur_fn_ix` to the
   closure's fi; restore after. A closure takes no `#regalloc` / struct-ret /
   `#naked`. The regalloc leak was the param bug — disasm showed `mov [rbp-0x30],rdi`
   (param shifted by 5×8) and the body loading `a` from an unloaded `rbx`.

2. **Frame patch.** Capture `ESUBRSP`'s patch handle and `EPATCHFRAME(handle,
   (GFLC*8+15)&-16)` after the body, exactly like PARSE_FN_DEF. (The v6.2.41 change
   that dropped ESUBRSP's size arg claimed "patched later via EPATCHFRAME" but the
   closure path never called it — and there were no closure tests to catch it.)

3. **Local-table isolation.** Before SFLC(0), snapshot the enclosing range
   `[0, parent_flc)` of all four slot tables into an alloc'd buffer
   (`_cl_save_locals`); restore it after the closure (`_cl_restore_locals`).

4. **Skip the enclosing fn's regalloc post-pass.** A new global
   `_cur_fn_has_closure` is set in the closure branch and gates the post-pass:
   `if (_cur_fn_regalloc > 0 && _cur_fn_has_closure == 0)`. Skipping it keeps a
   correct, un-reallocated frame (the prologue's callee-saved saves are just
   unused) — the only cost is losing the optimization for that one fn. The clean
   long-term fix is to emit closures OUTSIDE the parent's byte range, but the skip
   is correct and minimal.

**Calling convention.** A closure value is a plain fn pointer (`ELOAD_FN_ADDR`,
same fixup type as `&fn`), so `callptr` / `fncallN` invoke it; `||` is a
zero-param closure at a factor position (binary `a || b` never reaches there as a
leading token). Names are `__clNNNNN` (5 zero-padded digits of GFNC) — the old
`__cl<clid%10>` single digit aliased the 11th closure onto the 1st in REGFN.

**LESSON:** any new "emit a fn body mid-emit" path must isolate the FULL per-fn
state (codegen globals + the 4 slot tables) AND stay clear of the parent's
byte-scan passes. A feature with zero tests (closures had none) hid all four for
many releases. `cycc` itself uses no closures, so self-host + the differential are
byte-identical — the only signal is a dedicated `closures.tcyr`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "closure_lexical_capture_by_value_env_object"
description = "v6.3.8 added lexical capture by value to cyrius closures: a closure body that reads an enclosing local (a free var) captures it by value into a heap env object `[fn_ptr, cap0, cap1, …]`, and the closure VALUE becomes that object (not a bare fn pointer). It works by a PRE-SCAN of the closure body before emitting its prologue (`_cl_prescan_captures` in parse_expr.cyr): a name that FINDLOCAL-fails in the closure's own scope, is not a param/shadow, is not an `IDENT(`-shaped call name, and IS present in the enclosing-fn snapshot is recorded as a capture. If anything is captured, the closure gains a HIDDEN env param at arg-reg 0 / slot 0 and all user params shift to 1+; captured reads in the body become `load64(env + (1+capidx)*8)`. At construction the parent emits `alloc((1+ncap)*8)`, stores the fn addr at obj[0] and each captured value at obj[1+i], and the closure expression evaluates to the object. `callptr` auto-dispatches: if the callee local carries the `CLOSURE_TYID` (0x40000003) var type-tag it loads the fn ptr from `[obj]` and passes obj as the hidden arg1 (see closure semantics)."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["closure", "lambda", "lexical-capture", "by-value", "env-object", "callptr", "alloc", "var-type-tag", "v6.3.8"]
body = """
**Capture is by value into a vtable-shaped heap object.** A capturing closure
is no longer a bare fn pointer — it's `alloc((1+ncap)*8)` laid out as
`[fn_ptr, cap0, cap1, …]`. The closure fn gains a hidden env param (arg-reg 0 /
local slot 0); user params shift up by one (`ESTOREPARM(S, clpc + 1, …)` and the
fn's stored param count `_fnt_params[clfi]` is bumped). A captured read compiles
to `EFLLOAD(env) ; EADDRA_IMM((1+capidx)*8) ; ELOAD64`. The closure VALUE is the
object; a `var f = |x| base + x;` local is tagged `CLOSURE_TYID` so the call site
knows to dispatch it. Non-capturing closures are unchanged (still a bare fn ptr,
no allocation) — only a body that actually reads an enclosing local pays for an
env object.

**`callptr` auto-dispatch.** When the callee is a bare ident whose local type-tag
is `CLOSURE_TYID`, `callptr` spills the object, loads `[obj]` as the fn pointer,
pushes the object as a synthetic arg1, and calls indirect. Call sites are
identical to the non-capturing form — `callptr(f, 2)` works whether `f` captured
or not. This is the "auto-dispatch" calling convention chosen over a separate
`callclosure` builtin.

**THE non-obvious bug — stale name in the construction scratch slot.** The env
construction needs a scratch local slot to hold the freshly-alloc'd object while
it stores fn-addr + captures. That slot is a real entry in the shared local
name/depth/type/slice tables (`S+0x5D9D000` …), and those tables are NOT cleared
between fns — a slot can still hold a name string from a PREVIOUS fn. If the
scratch slot is left named, a later `var f` in this fn whose name happens to
collide trips the duplicate-variable check ("duplicate variable f") even though
the user only declared it once. Two same-name capturing closures in one fn hit
this every time. FIX: anonymize the scratch slot the instant it's claimed —
`S64(S + 0x5D9D000 + slot*8, 0 - 1); SLDEP(S, slot, 0);` (name = -1, depth 0).
The same anonymization is applied to `callptr`'s obj/fn-ptr spill slots.

**Platform.** PE (Windows) capturing closures SHIP as of v6.4.26 — routed
through `ECALLPTR_PE` (the 16-byte-aligned path) with the env/fn-pointer pushed
as the callee (parse_expr.cyr "v6.4.26: capturing closures now supported on PE");
the "capturing closures not yet supported on Windows PE" error string is gone
from src/. Capture is verified byte-correct on x86_64 + aarch64 (real pi + qemu)
and now on real cass.

**LESSON:** when you borrow a shared per-fn table slot for scratch, ANONYMIZE it
— the tables carry stale names across fns, and a live name in a scratch slot is
a landmine for the duplicate-decl check. Tests: `closures_capture.tcyr`
(single / multi / param-mix / by-value / thunk / with-locals / two-closures),
all via `callptr`. `cycc` uses no closures, so the only signal is the dedicated
tcyr — self-host + differential stay byte-identical.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "generic_fn_i64_base_and_ret_stash"
description = "v6.3.9 shipped i64 generic FUNCTIONS (`fn foo<T>(...)`) on the AR-01 token-replay substrate, gated behind _MONOMORPH_OK (default off → cycc byte-identical). The key architectural choice: in an i64-everywhere language the BASE fn is its own i64 instantiation — the body is emitted ONCE with `T -> i64` bound (via _bind_generic_call(S, fi, -8, -8) right before PARSE_PROG in PARSE_FN_DEF), so i64-typed calls are plain normal calls to the base, no instantiate-once machinery needed. `<T>` names are captured at the signature (_capture_tparams, replacing SKIP_GENERICS, gated) into _fnt_tparams (lazy-alloc, 0=non-generic); a scoped T->concrete binding (_tp_n0/_tp_c0/_tp_n1/_tp_c1) is consulted at the 4 type-resolution sites (param-type, local `: T`, slice<T>, sizeof) by _tp_resolve, with canonical token <0=-scalarwidth / >0=struct-sid. THE load-bearing bug: a `: T` generic return type made the return-type ROUGH-SCAN (the pre-param-loop lookahead that sets _cur_fn_ret_stash) default to stash=8 (struct retptr ABI), reserving arg-reg 0 for a hidden retptr and shifting every user param by one — so `id<T>(x:T):T{return x}` stored x into rsi's slot and read 0. Non-i64 instantiation + explicit `foo<i64>(x)` + generic structs are v6.3.10."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["generics", "monomorphization", "type-param", "ret-stash", "retptr-abi", "i64-everywhere", "base-as-instance", "v6.3.9"]
body = """
**The base fn IS the i64 monomorphization.** Cyrius is i64-everywhere (ADR-002),
so v6.3.9 does NOT defer a generic fn's body or build instantiate-once for i64.
PARSE_FN_DEF binds every captured type-param to i64 (`_bind_generic_call(S, fi,
0-8, 0-8)`) immediately before `PARSE_PROG` emits the body, so the body emits as
the i64 instance. An i64-typed call (inferred or `id(42)`) is an ordinary normal
call to that base — no minting, no jump-over, no replay. Dedup is by construction
(one base = one i64 instance). Non-i64 type-args (`foo<i32>`, `foo<Point>`) need a
separate instance and are deferred to v6.3.10, where the closure-style
instantiate-once is shared with generic structs.

**THE gotcha — `: T` return ⇒ phantom retptr/stash ⇒ param shift.** A `: T`
generic return type segfaulted/returned-garbage for ANY body that read a param
(`return x` gave 0; `return a` gave arg1). Root cause: PARSE_FN_DEF runs a
RETURN-TYPE ROUGH-SCAN *before* the param loop (a lookahead past `()` to `: Type`)
that decides `_cur_fn_ret_stash` — and `_cur_fn_ret_stash > 0` makes the param
loop reserve arg-reg 0 (RDI/RCX) for a hidden struct retptr, shifting user param 0
into the SECOND arg reg. For `: T`, `FINDSTRUCT(T)` misses and `T` matches no
scalar name, so the rough-scan fell through to the default `_cur_fn_ret_stash = 8`
— treating the generic return as a big-struct retptr. Disasm of the broken `id`:
`mov rdi,-0x30 ; mov rsi,-0x38 ; … mov rax,-0x38` (TWO param stores for a 1-param
fn, body reads the second = rsi, which `id(42)` never sets → 0). Fix: in the
rough-scan, recognize a captured type-param (`_is_tparam_of(S, fi, _rs_rt_noff)`)
as i64-shaped (`_rs_is_scalar = 1`) so it does NOT set the stash. The *other*
return-type site (the post-param classify) already takes `_rtc = -8` for `: T` via
the same `_is_tparam_of` check — both sites must agree the generic return is
scalar.

**LESSON.** A new "abstract" type spelling (`T`) that isn't a struct and isn't a
known scalar will silently fall into whatever the type machinery's DEFAULT is — and
for return types that default is the retptr/big-struct ABI. Any place that
branches on "struct vs scalar" by `FINDSTRUCT > 0` must get a type-param escape
hatch, or generic code inherits struct ABI. Gate: `_generics_fn_i64_gate`
(check.sh 104→105) compiles `tests/fixtures/monomorph/generic_fn_i64.cyr` under
CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=1 (must exit 42) and asserts it's REJECTED with the flag off (the
`: T` return is gate-only) — so the feature provably can't touch default codegen.
Verified byte-correct on x86_64 + aarch64 (qemu). cycc itself uses no generics →
self-host + the differential vs the pre-arc compiler stay byte-identical; the gate
is the only signal.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "generic_instantiate_once_reinvoke"
description = "v6.3.10 added non-i64 generic instances (foo<i32>, Box<Point>) via an instantiate-once engine that RE-INVOKES the canonical emitter on the base's verbatim tokens rather than replicating it. At a non-i64 generic call/use: mint base$ty into the name pool (_mint_generic_name, '$' separator), FINDFN/FINDSTRUCT is the dedup index (hit → reuse the instance fi/sid). On a miss, _instantiate_generic_fn jumps over the instance (EJMP0), STIs to the base fn's `fn`-keyword token (_fnt_defstart, stored +1 because token 0 is valid), sets _inst_name = mangled + _inst_conc0/1 = the concrete tokens, and calls PARSE_FN_DEF — which re-parses the full signature (params/masks/return) + body and emits the specialized fn with T bound to the concretes (PARSE_FN_DEF honors _inst_name for the fn name + _inst_conc0 for the body binding instead of base-as-i64). Enclosing fn state is saved/restored around the re-invoke + _cur_fn_has_closure=1 makes the enclosing fn skip its regalloc byte-scan (the instance is inline in its code range). Generic STRUCTS are the same idea but simpler (no codegen): _instantiate_generic_struct re-invokes PARSE_STRUCT_DEF, whose field `: T` parse gained a _tp_resolve consult."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["generics", "monomorphization", "instantiate-once", "re-invoke", "PARSE_FN_DEF", "PARSE_STRUCT_DEF", "dedup", "v6.3.10"]
body = """
**Re-invoke, don't replicate.** The clean way to emit a specialized generic
instance is NOT to hand-roll a parallel prologue/param/body emitter — it's to
re-run the CANONICAL emitter (PARSE_FN_DEF / PARSE_STRUCT_DEF) on the base's
verbatim tokens with two overrides: a NAME override (`_inst_name` → the mangled
`foo$ty` / `Box$ty`) and a BINDING override (`_inst_conc0/1` → the concrete type
tokens, vs the default base-as-i64 `T→i64`). That buys full fidelity — params,
ABI masks, struct-typed args, return type, regalloc — for free, and stays in
lockstep with the base by construction.

**Mechanism (fns).** `_instantiate_generic_fn`: mint → `FINDFN` dedup → on miss,
`EJMP0` over the instance, `STI` to `GFDS(base_fi)` (the base's `fn` token), set
the overrides, `PARSE_FN_DEF(S)`, clear overrides, `EPATCH` the jump. The instance
is emitted INLINE in the enclosing fn's code range (jumped over), so the enclosing
fn must skip its regalloc post-pass — set `_cur_fn_has_closure = 1` in the restore
(the same guard closures use, for the same reason). Save/restore the enclosing
`_cur_fn_*` + `GFLC`/`GLVAR`/`rp_vec`/`GTI` + the local-table snapshot
(`_cl_save_locals`) around the re-invoke.

**THE gotcha — def-start +1.** The base's `fn`-keyword token index is stored as
`_defstart_ti + 1` (and read back `- 1`), because **token index 0 is valid** (a
generic fn can be the very first token in a no-include file) and `0` is the
table's "unrecorded" sentinel. Storing the raw index 0 made the instantiate
return the not-recorded error and every instance silently fell back to a clean
compile error / wrong path until the +1 sentinel fix. Same pattern any time a
0-based index shares a table with a 0=absent sentinel.

**Structs.** Generic structs over i64 already worked (the base struct IS the i64
instance: `val: T` registers as an untyped 8-byte field). Non-i64 use
(`Box<Point>`) re-invokes `PARSE_STRUCT_DEF` (no codegen — struct registration
only, so no `EJMP0`/state-save), with the field-type parse gaining a `_tp_resolve`
consult so `: T` resolves to the concrete sid/width via `ADDFIELDTYPED`. Wired at
the `var x: Box<conc>` type-resolution site. STRUCTSZ/FIELDOFF are lazy, so the
re-added concrete-typed fields yield correct sizes/offsets with no cache work — a
field after a `Holder<Point>`'s 16-byte Point field lands at offset 16, proving
the specialized layout.

**LESSON.** When you need to emit a specialized copy of something the compiler
already knows how to emit, re-invoke the canonical path with overrides — far less
code + drift than a parallel emitter. Verified x86 + aarch64; gated `_MONOMORPH_OK`
→ self-host + differential byte-identical; `_generics_full_gate` (check.sh 106).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "async_await_futures_dce_nameread"
description = "v6.3.11 added async/await as first-class FUTURES (deferred-then-forced), NOT stackless CPS coroutines — a premise-check found the cooperative runtime (lib/async.cyr) is run-to-completion (async_run calls fncall1 to completion with blocking epoll yields; no suspend/resume), so true CPS coroutines would need a poll-runtime rework. An `async fn f(p0..pn): T` compiles to a CONSTRUCTOR that allocs a heap Future [ &f$impl, argc, args… ] and returns it (the body becomes a hidden f$impl reached only by pointer; the CALL DOES NOT RUN THE BODY); await fut lowers to future_force(fut) which calls the bundled impl with the bundled args via fncallN. The Future object reuses the closure-env heap construction (alloc + fn-addr fixup + field stores). THE LANDMINE: the pass-2 top-level dispatch reads the fn name at TOKVAL(GTI+1) for its DCE reachability pre-check, assuming the cursor is at `fn` — a leading `async` (token 134) shifted it by one, so it read `fn`(value 0) not the name, mis-marked the async fn unreachable, and stubbed it (call site saw the fn as undefined). Fix: consume `async` INSIDE the fn-dispatch block (cursor → `fn`, so all DCE/match-peek/stub logic stays correct) and arm _pending_async only on the reachable path."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["async", "await", "futures", "future_force", "DCE", "dispatch", "run-to-completion", "v6.3.11"]
body = """
**Futures, not coroutines.** `async fn` / `await` are sugar over the EXISTING
cooperative epoll runtime — and that runtime is run-to-completion (`async_run`
drives each task to completion via `fncall1` with *blocking* epoll/timerfd
yields; it has no suspend/resume). So the roadmap's "CPS state machines" framing
is aspirational for this runtime. The faithful, complete-for-this-runtime model
is first-class **Futures**: calling an `async fn` builds a deferred Future
`[ &f$impl, argc, args… ]` (a heap object — same construction as a closure env);
`await` forces it. True stackless coroutines that suspend/resume *mid-body* across
an `await` are a pinned follow-on needing a poll-based runtime rework. Don't try to
bolt suspend/resume onto the run-to-completion driver.

**Lowering.** In PARSE_FN_DEF, after name-mangling, rename the body to `f$impl`
(`_mint_impl_name`) so the body emits as the hidden impl; at the END (before
`return 0`), `_async_emit_constructor` synthesizes the user-named `f`:
EJMP0 jump-over → EFNPRO → ESTOREPARM the params → ESUBRSP → alloc((2+pc)*8) →
store &f$impl@[0] (ELOAD_FN_ADDR fixup), pc@[8], params@[16+i*8] → return obj →
EFNEPI → EPATCHFRAME → EPATCH. `await` is a factor-level prefix (token 135) in
PARSE_FACTOR: eval operand → rax, EPUSHR, ECALLPOPS(1), ECALLFIX(future_force).
`future_force`/`async_spawn_future` live in lib/async.cyr (shared, pure fncallN).

**The DCE name-read landmine (general).** Any modifier keyword placed BEFORE `fn`
collides with the pass-2 dispatch's DCE reachability pre-check, which reads the fn
name at `TOKVAL(GTI+1)` assuming the cursor sits on `fn`. With `async` in front,
it read the wrong token, the bitmap lookup missed, the fn was deemed unreachable
and STUBBED (`xor eax,eax; ret`), and its call site reported the fn undefined.
The robust fix is to consume the modifier INSIDE the fn-dispatch block so the
cursor lands on `fn` before any name-read / match-peek / stub path runs, and to
arm the per-fn flag (`_pending_async`) only on the reachable branch so a
DCE-stubbed instance can't leak it to the next fn.

**Gating.** Tokens 134/135 are lexed UNCONDITIONALLY (no bare `async`/`await`
identifier exists in src/ or lib/, so cycc's token stream is unchanged →
byte-identical self-host); the FEATURE is gated in pass 2 via a lazy
`_ensure_async_gate` (reads `CYRIUS_ASYNC` on first async/await — no per-fork
driver env-read edit). `_IS_FN_KW` (token 32 OR 134) routes the dispatch in all 7
forks. Verified x86 + aarch64 + native-aarch64; check.sh 107; ecb+cass+pi
SELFHOST_OK; differential + seed→cybs→cycc byte-identical.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_async_reactor_esysxlat_epoll_pwait_collision_and_event_offset"
description = "v6.4.42: the aarch64 epoll async reactor (lib/async.cyr) had NEVER worked — TWO aarch64-only ABI bugs, both invisible to x86 self-host and the async gates (which run only on x86), found only by running the reactor fixtures on REAL pi hardware ('found by ports'). BUG 1 (the blocker): sys_epoll_wait issued the aarch64 epoll_pwait number (22) directly, but 22 is ALSO x86 `pipe`, which ESYSXLAT (src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr) rewrites to pipe2(x0,0) + x1=0 — so every reactor wait ran pipe2(epfd, 0) → -EFAULT, and _async_step's epoll_wait never returned, hanging the whole reactor. The classic ESYSXLAT stdlib/x86 number-collision (cf. the flock 73↔ppoll, socket 41, getdents 217 collisions). BUG 2 (latent behind #1): the epoll_event `data` field was written/read at byte offset 4 unconditionally — correct only for x86_64 (EPOLL_PACKED, 12-byte struct); aarch64 leaves the struct unpacked (16 bytes, u64 aligned to offset 8), so the task-ptr round-trip through epoll_data landed at the wrong offset. Fix: sys_epoll_wait issues x86 232 (epoll_wait) + a new ESYSXLAT arg-extend entry maps 232 → epoll_pwait(22) with NULL sigmask (mirrors poll 7→ppoll 73); the reactor's epoll_data store/load are #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64-guarded to @8. Both verified end-to-end on pi (all 7 async fixtures → exit 42, first time ever)."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["aarch64", "async", "reactor", "epoll", "epoll_pwait", "ESYSXLAT", "syscall-collision", "EPOLL_PACKED", "found-by-ports", "pi", "v6.4.42"]
body = """
**Two aarch64-only ABI bugs, both self-host-blind.** cycc never issues epoll, and
the async `*_gate` fixtures (programs/checks/main.cyr) compile with the x86
build/cycc and run natively on x86 — so the aarch64 reactor path was NEVER
exercised in CI. It surfaced only by cross-compiling the reactor fixtures to
aarch64 and running them on real pi. Same class as the v6.2.10 socket / v6.2.24
getdents64 / v6.0.68 stat gaps: "the compiler self-hosts on aarch64" does NOT mean
"aarch64 stdlib syscalls work" — self-host only exercises the ~40 syscalls cycc
itself makes.

**BUG 1 — the ESYSXLAT number collision (the blocker).** ESYSXLAT emits, inline at
every aarch64 syscall site, a runtime cmp/b.ne/movz chain that translates the
x86-numbered syscalls the compiler + tools issue into aarch64 numbers. It also has
`cmp x8,#22 → pipe2 + x1=0` (v6.2.27: aarch64 has no bare pipe(2); raw
`syscall(22,&fds)` is x86-canonical pipe → pipe2). But the async stdlib's
sys_epoll_wait issued aarch64 epoll_pwait = **22** directly. So x8=22 (meant as
epoll_pwait) hit the pipe cmp → rewritten to pipe2(x0=epfd, x1=0) → wrote 2 fds to
address `epfd` → **-EFAULT** on every wait. Diagnosed by dumping (epfd, ctl, nrA)
from a pi probe: epoll_create1(20)=ok, epoll_ctl(21)=ok (20/21 not in ESYSXLAT),
epoll_pwait(22)=-14. ONLY 22 collided (timerfd 85/86, socket 198, close 57, read
63 etc. all pass through untranslated). THE RULE (already in CLAUDE.md): an aarch64
stdlib syscall number that collides with an x86 number ESYSXLAT translates is
silently mis-remapped — issue the x86 number + add an ESYSXLAT entry instead of the
bare aarch64 number. Fix: sys_epoll_wait issues x86 **232** (4 args); a new ESYSXLAT
entry `cmp x8,#232; b.ne +16; movz x4,#0; movz x5,#0; movz x8,#22` arg-extends it to
epoll_pwait(epfd,events,maxevents,timeout,sigmask=0,sigsetsize=0). Placed AFTER the
pipe(22) cmp so the x8=22 it produces is not re-caught (the same ordering rule the
flock/at-family entries use). Exactly mirrors the poll 7→ppoll 73 arg-extend.

**BUG 2 — epoll_event data offset (latent behind #1).** `struct epoll_event` is
`__attribute__((packed))` ONLY under `#ifdef __x86_64__` (linux/eventpoll.h
EPOLL_PACKED) → x86 layout is {events@0 u32, data@4 u64} sizeof 12. aarch64 leaves
it unpacked → {events@0, 4 pad, data@8} sizeof 16. lib/async.cyr wrote the task ptr
to epoll_data and read it back on wake at a hardcoded byte offset 4 (correct for
x86 only). On aarch64 the kernel reads/writes data at offset 8, so the round-tripped
`wtask` was garbage. Fix: `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` → @8, else @4, at BOTH the
write (_async_wait_events) and the wake read (_async_step); keep the standalone
async_await_readable/async_timeout helpers consistent. cycc byte-identical (the
guard's x86 branch is the pre-existing code).

**Verification.** x86 cycc byte-identical (ESYSXLAT lives in
main_aarch64.cyr's chain, not main.cyr; the wrapper is DCE-dead in cycc). ecb
(macho, addition is after the `_TARGET_MACHO==2` early-return) + cass (x86 PE
backend) byte-identical. pi self-hosts byte-identical AND runs all 7 async reactor
fixtures → 42. Lesson: to verify an aarch64 stdlib syscall, run a program that
CALLS it on real aarch64 — qemu-user is NOT a substitute here (qemu's own
epoll_pwait emulation returned nothing for a ready fd, masking the real cause).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "wx_separate_pt_load_segments"
description = "v6.3.12 split every userland ELF from one RWE PT_LOAD into two — text R E (not writable) + data R W (not executable) — for segment-level W^X (agnos 1.50.6 loader is PF_X-aware). DEFAULT ON; CYRIUS_WX=0 opts out. The split is hard because the code's absolute/PC-relative var+string relocations are patched in FIXUP against dbase = entry + acp (the data base), so EMITELF_USER's 2nd PT_LOAD p_vaddr MUST equal that same dbase or the patched code reads the wrong addresses. Both derive it from a shared _wx_data_vaddr(base + text_foff, cp) that rounds the code-area end up to a fresh 2 MB page (agnos huge-page granularity). Adding a 2nd 56-byte program header pushes .text from file offset 120 to 176, so _entry_base + e_entry must shift to base+176 under W^X. Flipping the default to ON needs a TWO-STEP BOOTSTRAP because the old single-segment cycc cannot emit W^X."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["W^X", "ELF", "PT_LOAD", "cyrld", "FIXUP", "dbase", "two-step-bootstrap", "segments", "v6.3.12"]
body = """
**The lockstep invariant.** In cyrius, FIXUP patches every global-var and string
reference in the emitted code against `dbase = entry + acp` (data base = end of the
aligned code area): a string is at `dbase + totvar + idx`, a gvar at
`dbase + pfx[idx]`. EMITELF_USER then serializes the data at a matching vaddr. To
put data on its own permission-separated PT_LOAD, you MUST change `dbase` in BOTH
places identically — pad `acp` in FIXUP so `dbase` lands on a fresh page, and emit
the 2nd PT_LOAD's `p_vaddr` at the SAME value. The shared helper
`_wx_data_vaddr(S, entry, cp)` (called with `entry = _entry_base` in FIXUP and
`entry = base + text_foff` in EMITELF_USER — they're equal for userland) guarantees
agreement. Get this wrong and every string/gvar load in the program reads garbage.

**Why 2 MB.** The agnos loader maps 2 MB huge pages, so a text and data segment that
share a 2 MB page can't get distinct permissions. Round the data vaddr up to a 2 MB
boundary. The FILE offset only needs the ELF `p_align` (4 KB on x86, 64 KB on
aarch64): since the 2 MB vaddr is a multiple of `p_align`, `p_vaddr ≡ p_offset
(mod p_align)` holds with only a ≤`p_align` file pad — the ≤2 MB gap is *virtual*,
near-zero file bytes (the "p_offset sharing" trick).

**The .text shift.** A 2nd program header (56 bytes) pushes `.text` from file offset
120 (64-byte ELF header + 1 PH) to 176 (+ a 2nd PH). `_entry_base` and the ELF
`e_entry` MUST move to `base + 176` under W^X, or every absolute fn / var / string
fixup (which is `entry + offset`) lands 56 bytes off the real code. Make
`_entry_base` call `_wx_active` (forward-ref resolves via pass-1 fn registration).

**Two-step bootstrap to flip default-on.** The existing single-segment cycc CANNOT
emit W^X (its `_wx_active` doesn't exist / defaults off). So `old-cycc compiles
new-src` → cc1: a single-segment binary whose *code* defaults W^X-on (a transition
intermediate). `cc1 compiles src` → cc2: W^X (cc1 emits W^X). `cc2 compiles src` →
cc3: W^X. `cc2 == cc3` is the stable fixpoint — install that as build/cycc. The
seed chain works the same way: cybs (single-segment) emits gen1 (cycc code,
single-seg), gen1 emits gen2 == build/cycc (W^X). Once build/cycc is cc2,
version-bump's single self-host step reproduces W^X with no further two-step.

**Guard.** W^X is userland-ELF only: `_wx_active` requires `_emit_fmt(S) == 0` (ELF,
not Mach-O 1/2 or PE 3) AND `kmode == 0` (not kernel / shared `.so` / object). So
Mach-O, PE, kernel, `.so`, and `.o` outputs are untouched. x86 and aarch64 are
separate backends, so `_wx_active`/`_wx_data_vaddr` are duplicated in each (aarch64
`p_align` is 0x10000, x86 0x1000). Verified: readelf -lW shows text R E / data RW on
x86, aarch64, and CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS; pi (real aarch64) self-hosts the W^X cycc;
CYRIUS_WX=0 is differential byte-identical to the pre-split release.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macho_syscall_xlat_source_numbers_arch_specific"
description = "The Mach-O syscall-number translation is ARCH-SPECIFIC in its SOURCE numbers, not just its target: x86-macho feeds syscalls_macos.cyr (BSD-numbered — getpid 39, getppid 110) but aarch64-macho feeds syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr (getpid 172, getppid 173) via lib/syscalls.cyr:64-70. So a getpid/getppid/syscall fix that reasons ONLY about the x86 EMACHO_SYSXLAT table (39/110) misses the aarch64-macho stdlib ENTIRELY — the aarch64 numbers (172/173) also need their own ESYSXLAT entries. getpid returned positive GARBAGE on aarch64-macOS (an untranslated 172 ran a raw svc) until v6.3.44, and a callable-only smoke PASSED on it (a garbage-positive value is still 'callable, stable'). Found only by tightening vr01_process_smoke.tcyr to assert a POSITIVE PID and running it on REAL ecb (aarch64-macOS) hardware."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["macho", "syscall", "ESYSXLAT", "aarch64", "getpid", "getppid", "cross-os", "found-by-ports", "v6.3.44"]
body = """
**Two macho stdlibs, two number spaces.** `lib/syscalls.cyr` (lines
~64-70) routes the Mach-O syscall SOURCE numbers by ARCH, not just OS:
  - **x86-macho** includes `syscalls_macos.cyr` — the BSD numbers
    (getpid **39**, getppid **110**).
  - **aarch64-macho** includes `syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr` — the Linux
    aarch64 numbers (getpid **172**, getppid **173**).
The BACKEND translation table (`EMACHO_SYSXLAT` on x86, `ESYSXLAT` on
aarch64) then maps those source numbers to the real Darwin syscall
(getpid → 20, getppid → 39). The trap: these are TWO SEPARATE tables in
TWO SEPARATE backends. A fix that edits only the x86 table (39→20, 110→…)
leaves aarch64-macho (172/173) untranslated — the stdlib picks a totally
different source number there.

**How it shipped garbage.** Pre-v6.3.44 the aarch64-macho ESYSXLAT had no
entry for 172, so `getpid()` on aarch64-macOS emitted a raw `svc` with
x8=172 (a Darwin number that isn't getpid) and returned a positive but
GARBAGE value. getppid (173) returned 0. The x86 getpid (39→20) had
worked since v6.0.02, which is exactly why reasoning "getpid works on
macOS" was wrong — it worked on x86-macho only. Fix: aarch64 ESYSXLAT
`172→20` (getpid) + `173→39` (getppid); x86 `110→39` getppid; and the
`_macho_arm_routes` whitelist gained 172/173.

**Why the old test missed it.** The smoke only checked that getpid was
CALLABLE and returned a stable value — a garbage-positive integer passes
that. Tightening the test to assert a POSITIVE, plausible PID *and running
it on real ecb hardware* is what surfaced it. A green CI check and a
disasm of the x86 table would never have caught it. (Same lesson class as
the v6.3.43 Windows file-I/O std-handle bug.) See
`cross_os_self_host_real_hardware_non_negotiable`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "emit_gvar_inits_parallel_copy_of_parse_struct_init"
description = "EMIT_GVAR_INITS (the pass-2 replay of global var initializers, src/frontend/parse_decl.cyr) is a HAND-DUPLICATED PARALLEL COPY of PARSE_STRUCT_INIT's positional struct-literal loop. A struct field-shape fix that lands in one but not the other makes the global-scope init path silently DIVERGE from the in-fn path. The v6.3.44 bug: EMIT_GVAR_INITS never got the v5.10.7 IS_STR_FIELD single-8-byte-slot handling, so a global `var g = T { .., <str> };` with a Str field flattened the Str into a nested-struct 2-field embedding, PCMPE'd a nonexistent `len` expression, and hit `}` (`unexpected '}'`) — while the same literal inside a fn (which goes through PARSE_STRUCT_INIT, already fixed) compiled fine. There is a documented refactor backlog of ~5 such 'parallel copy' duplications in the struct/gvar-init path; until they're merged, ANY field-shape or width-correctness fix must be applied to BOTH loops."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["EMIT_GVAR_INITS", "PARSE_STRUCT_INIT", "parse_decl", "struct-init", "global-scope", "parallel-copy", "IS_STR_FIELD", "refactor-backlog", "v6.3.44"]
body = """
**Two loops, one shape.** cyrius emits a struct literal's positional
field stores in TWO places:
  - `PARSE_STRUCT_INIT` — the in-fn path (`var g = T { … };` inside a fn).
  - `EMIT_GVAR_INITS` — the pass-2 replay for GLOBAL-scope initializers
    (deferred to after all decls register).
They are hand-duplicated copies of the same positional loop, NOT a shared
helper. Every per-field-shape refinement has had to be applied to both,
and historically hasn't been: the v5.9.36 narrow-width initializer fix and
the v5.10.7 single-slot Str handling both had to be mirrored. When only
one loop gets a fix, the global-scope path silently DIVERGES.

**The v6.3.44 divergence.** A Str field (field-type id > 0) in
`EMIT_GVAR_INITS` fell into the nested-struct FLATTEN branch — which treats
a struct-typed field as a 2-field by-value embedding and `PCMPE`s a second
expression for the embedded struct's `len` field. A Str is a single 8-byte
handle, not a 2-field embed, so the loop consumed a nonexistent expression
and ran into the closing `}` → `unexpected '}'`. Diagnosis was masked by
its being FILED as a `#derive(Serialize)` bug — but it is
`#derive`-INDEPENDENT (a plain global struct literal with a Str field
reproduces it). Fix: mirror the `IS_STR_FIELD` single-8-byte-slot branch
into `EMIT_GVAR_INITS`, exactly as `PARSE_STRUCT_INIT` already had it.

**Recognize it again.** A struct-literal shape that works inside `fn main`
but errors at global scope (or vice-versa) is the fingerprint of a
one-sided fix to this parallel-copy pair. There is a refactor backlog of
~5 such duplications in the struct/gvar-init path — until they collapse
into one shared emitter, patch BOTH loops for any field-shape change.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "stdlib_mutex_single_owner_sync_cyr"
description = "As of v6.3.44 the stdlib mutex has a SINGLE OWNER: lib/sync.cyr. mutex_new/lock/unlock + MUTEX_SIZE used to be defined in BOTH lib/thread.cyr AND lib/sync.cyr (and in each platform sibling: thread_win ⇄ sync_windows SRWLOCK, thread_agnos ⇄ sync agnos) — so any consumer linking both thread and sync (sync arrives transitively via alloc/atomic/patra) saw 3 `duplicate fn 'mutex_*'` warnings and a silent link-order winner if the impls ever drifted. Now sync.cyr owns them, thread.cyr includes sync.cyr once at the top, all thread*.cyr drop their inline defs, and cyrius's include-once dedup collapses the both-included case to one definition. sync.cyr's own #ifdef routing picks the backend (Linux futex / Windows SRWLOCK / macOS spinlock / agnos no-op). SECOND gotcha: the api-surface snapshot is MODULE-QUALIFIED (thread::mutex_lock vs sync::mutex_lock), so RELOCATING a fn from one module to another shows up as REMOVALS in the snapshot diff — regenerate it (build/cyrius_api_surface --update) and verify the removals are relocations, not real deletions."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["mutex", "sync.cyr", "thread.cyr", "single-owner", "include-once", "duplicate-fn", "api-surface", "module-qualified", "v6.3.44"]
body = """
**Single owner.** `mutex_new` / `mutex_lock` / `mutex_unlock` + `MUTEX_SIZE`
now live in exactly one file: `lib/sync.cyr`. Before v6.3.44 they were
ALSO defined in `lib/thread.cyr` (Linux futex) and in each platform sibling
(`thread_win.cyr` ⇄ `sync_windows.cyr` SRWLOCK; `thread_agnos.cyr` ⇄ the
sync agnos branch). Because `sync` arrives transitively (alloc → atomic →
patra all pull it in), any consumer linking `thread` too got THREE
`duplicate fn 'mutex_*'` warnings on every build — with a silent
link-order winner if the two impls ever drifted. Fix: `sync.cyr` is the
single owner; `thread.cyr` includes it once at the top and all three
`thread*.cyr` drop their inline defs. `sync`'s own `#ifdef` routing selects
the backend (Linux futex / Windows SRWLOCK / macOS spinlock / agnos no-op),
and include-once dedup collapses the both-included case. Behavior-preserving
on Linux/Windows/agnos (the bodies were byte-identical) and a NET FIX on
macOS (a working spinlock instead of the never-routed futex).

**The api-surface snapshot is MODULE-QUALIFIED.** Symbols are recorded as
`module::fn` (e.g. `thread::mutex_lock`, `sync::mutex_lock`), so MOVING a
fn between modules registers as REMOVALS (`thread::mutex_*` gone) even
though the consumer-visible unqualified `mutex_*` is unchanged (it stays
via `sync`/`sync_macos`/`sync_windows`). Regenerate the snapshot
(`build/cyrius_api_surface --update`) and confirm the removals are
RELOCATIONS, not deletions — the v6.3.44 fold was 9 relocations / 0 real
additions. (Same regenerate-and-verify discipline as any folded lib that
adds/moves public fns — the check.sh api-surface gate goes RED on a stale
snapshot's exact-count line.)
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cross_os_self_host_real_hardware_non_negotiable"
description = "Cross-OS self-host on REAL hardware — ecb (macOS arm64), cass (Windows PE), pi (aarch64 Linux), one ssh away each — is a STANDING release gate, not a closeout-only or CI-only check. A GREEN CI CHECK IS NOT VERIFICATION. Two v6.3.x bugs shipped SILENTLY behind green smoke and were caught ONLY by running on the hardware: (1) v6.3.43 — Windows sys_write/sys_read on FILE handles (EWRITE_PE always translated the fd → GetStdHandle, so only std handles 0/1/2 ever worked; a CreateFileW handle wrote to garbage) — never caught because no test had ever written to a FILE on Windows. (2) v6.3.44 — getpid returned positive GARBAGE on aarch64-macOS (untranslated syscall 172) — a callable-only smoke passed on it. Hello-world / exit-code / callable-only smoke is a PLACEBO; the actual behavior asserted on the actual hardware IS the test. The macOS COMPILER self-host rotted silently ~9 minors behind a CI job that only ran hello-world."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["cross-os", "self-host", "ecb", "cass", "pi", "release-gate", "found-by-ports", "smoke-is-placebo", "v6.3.43", "v6.3.44"]
body = """
**A checkmark is not the compiler running on the hardware.** After ANY
compiler-backend or stdlib change, cycc must self-host BYTE-IDENTICAL and
the platform tests must PASS with real assertions on real macOS (ecb,
arm64), real Windows (cass, PE), and real aarch64 Linux (pi) — each one
`ssh` away via `~/.ssh/config`. This is a STANDING per-release gate (the
LIBTEST `vr01_` glob + `SELFHOST_OK`/`LIBTEST_OK` on ecb/cass/pi), not a
closeout-only or CI-only step.

**Two silent-rot cases from v6.3.x, both found only by running:**
  - **v6.3.43, Windows file I/O.** `EWRITE_PE` (src/backend/x86/emit.cyr)
    ALWAYS translated the fd into a *standard handle* via GetStdHandle
    (`neg;sub 10`), so `sys_write`/`sys_read` only ever worked for fd
    0/1/2 — a real `CreateFileW` handle (a large value) mistranslated into
    a bogus std-handle index → WriteFile to an invalid handle → the whole
    file round-trip silently failed. Never caught because NO test had ever
    written to a FILE on Windows (tests/win/ was all stdout). Fix: an
    unsigned `cmp rax,2; ja` fd-branch (fd>2 = handle direct), mirroring
    the fix EREAD_PE already had since v6.0.45.
  - **v6.3.44, aarch64-macOS getpid.** getpid returned positive GARBAGE
    (an untranslated syscall 172) — see
    `macho_syscall_xlat_source_numbers_arch_specific`. A callable-only
    smoke passed on the garbage.

**The precedent.** The macOS COMPILER self-host rotted silently for ~9
minors (v5.3.13 → v6.0.32, 400+ patches) behind a CI job named "Mach-O
ARM64 Native ✓" that only ran hello-world / exit-code programs and never
once built or self-hosted cycc. It surfaced only when a human installed on
a Mac and got a broken toolchain. Hello-world / exit-code / callable-only
smoke is a PLACEBO — the compiler self-hosting on the target, and the
actual behavior asserted on the actual hardware, IS the test.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "monomorph_default_on_flip_gftp_decouple"
description = "v6.4.0 flipped CYRIUS_MONOMORPH default-on (generics on by default; CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0 opts out). It was NOT a one-liner: the precise per-fn signal is GFTP(S, fi) != 0 (generic-fn-type-params; non-zero = the fn is generic), because a generic fn's base AND all its token-replayed instances SHARE the generic fi. Two entangled prerequisites: (1) DECOUPLE monomorph from _INLINE_OK — they shared the inline-CAPTURE gate (_INLINE_OK==1 || _MONOMORPH_OK==1), so flipping re-enabled GENERAL inlining (+25.6% x86 size regression, the v1.11.3 class) → gate capture to `_INLINE_OK==1 || (_MONOMORPH_OK==1 && GFTP(S,fi)!=0)` at BOTH SFBS/SFBE sites; (2) narrow the v6.3.29 frame-trim blanket (`if _MONOMORPH_OK!=0 return`) to `if _MONOMORPH_OK==1 && GFTP(S,fi)!=0 return`, passing fi into _ra_frame_trim. cycc has ZERO generic fns → flag-on == flag-off byte-identical (1,024,552 B). DEAD ENDS (the 'why not X' record): _cur_fn_has_closure (incomplete), _ra_picker_ran (over-conserved +12KB), _inst_conc0 (missed nested). GFTP is the only clean signal — a property of the FN, not transient emit state."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["generics", "monomorphization", "GFTP", "default-on-flip", "inline-decouple", "frame-trim", "_INLINE_OK", "_MONOMORPH_OK", "v6.4.0"]
body = """
**The flip needed a precise per-fn signal, not a global toggle.** v6.4.0
made `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH` default-ON (`_MONOMORPH_OK = 1` in
`src/common/util.cyr`); `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0` is the opt-out. The whole
generics feature was already correct behind the flag (through v6.3.39) —
this slot de-risked the DEFAULT flip, and it was NOT a one-liner. Two
prerequisites were entangled, and both are resolved by the SAME signal:
**`GFTP(S, fi) != 0`** — the fn has generic type-params. A generic fn's
base AND every token-replayed instance share the generic fn's `fi`, so
`GFTP != 0` cleanly covers the whole generic family and NOTHING else
(see the comment at parse_fn.cyr:1834).

**Prerequisite 1 — decouple monomorph from `_INLINE_OK`.** The two
shared the inline-CAPTURE gate: `_INLINE_OK == 1 || _MONOMORPH_OK == 1`.
With `_MONOMORPH_OK` now 1 by default, that OR re-enabled GENERAL value
inlining for EVERY fn — a **+25.6 %** x86 size regression (the same
class as the original v1.11.3 inline-off decision; ~1.02 MB → ~1.29 MB
on a flag-on self-host). Fix: qualify the monomorph arm with GFTP at
BOTH capture sites (SFBS body-start + SFBE classify):
`if (_INLINE_OK == 1 || (_MONOMORPH_OK == 1 && GFTP(S, fi) != 0))`
(parse_fn.cyr:3133 / :3178). Non-generic fns are never captured;
generic fns still are.

**Prerequisite 2 — narrow the v6.3.29 frame-trim blanket.** The frame
trim had a blanket `if (_MONOMORPH_OK != 0) return` — under the flag
that disabled the trim for EVERY fn (+16 KB). Narrow it to
`if (_MONOMORPH_OK == 1 && GFTP(S, fi) != 0) return` and thread `fi`
through — `_ra_frame_trim(S, fn_start, fi)` (parse_fn.cyr:1821, guard
at :1838). Only generic fns skip the trim now.

**Why byte-identical.** cycc's own source has ZERO generic fns, so
under the flip every capture/trim GFTP check is 0 → flag-on codegen ==
flag-off codegen → cycc self-hosts byte-identical (1,024,552 B) and the
differential is status-diff=0 / codegen-diff=0 over the whole corpus in
BOTH modes.

**DEAD ENDS (kept as the 'why not X' record).** Three other per-fn
signals were tried and rejected before GFTP:
  - `_cur_fn_has_closure` — incomplete (doesn't cover all generic fns).
  - `_ra_picker_ran` — over-conserved (+12 KB; too broad).
  - `_inst_conc0` — missed NESTED instances.
GFTP won because it is a PROPERTY OF THE FN (its captured type-params),
not transient emit state that a given pass may or may not have set. The
v6.3.5 `_monomorph_substrate_gate` was REPURPOSED here: it now proves
the DECOUPLE holds (monomorph-on leaves non-generic fns un-inlined), the
inverse of what it originally proved.

**Recognize again.** Any 'flip a gated default on' where the gate shared
an OR with another feature's gate: flipping silently re-enables that
other feature for the whole program. The fix is a precise per-fn (or
per-site) qualifier that is a stable PROPERTY, not transient state — and
measure the size delta on a flag-on self-host, because a same-file-size
cycc (0 generic fns) hides a large regression on real consumers.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "alloc_reset_zero_on_reset_chunk_model_and_lib_fix_not_auto_byte_identical"
description = "v6.4.1 made alloc_reset() zero the reclaimed span (CVE-2026-34988-class memory-reuse info-leak — the ONE point the forward-only bump allocator re-hands an address). THE TRAP: the naive `memset(_heap_first_base, 0, _heap_used)` is UNSAFE — _heap_used is CUMULATIVE across chunks, so it over-runs the first chunk (past _heap_first_end) on a multi-chunk heap. Only the FIRST chunk is a reuse channel (spills re-mmap fresh kernel-zeroed chunks), so scrub its written extent: `_heap_ptr - _heap_first_base` when still in the first chunk (common, exact), else the whole first chunk via the `_heap_base != _heap_first_base` spill discriminator; macOS/Windows single-region backends zero exactly [_heap_base, _heap_ptr). A new un-gated `_alloc_zero(base,n)` (store64/store8 loop) keeps the foundational allocator free of a lib/string.cyr memset dependency; fixed in all 4 backends. DURABLE LESSON: cycc INCLUDES lib/alloc.cyr AND alloc_reset is reachable via the allocator vtable's reset_fn (address-taken → NOT DCE'd), so the scrub added +424 B of code to cycc (absorbed into page-align padding → file size unchanged at 1,024,552 B). A 'lib fix' is NOT automatically cycc-byte-identical when cycc includes the lib."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["alloc", "alloc_reset", "info-leak", "CVE-2026-34988", "chunk-chain", "_heap_used", "_alloc_zero", "lib-fix-byte-identical", "vtable-reachable", "v6.4.1"]
body = """
**The leak.** The bump allocator is forward-only — every allocation
moves onto never-touched, kernel-zeroed pages — EXCEPT `alloc_reset()`,
the one point it rewinds the bump pointer and hands the same address to
a new owner. Pre-v6.4.1 it rewound without zeroing, so a post-reset
allocation read the prior occupant's bytes (CVE-2026-34988 /
CVE-2022-39393 class; sandhi's per-batch reset is the live call site).

**THE TRAP — `_heap_used` is cumulative across chunks.** The obvious
fix `memset(_heap_first_base, 0, _heap_used)` is UNSAFE: on a chunk-chain
allocator (`lib/alloc.cyr` Linux, `alloc_agnos.cyr`) `_heap_used` counts
bytes across ALL chunks, so it over-runs the first chunk — writing past
`_heap_first_end` into whatever follows. Correct model: only the FIRST
chunk is a reuse channel (a spill past it re-`mmap`s a fresh, already
kernel-zeroed chunk that alloc_reset drops entirely), so scrub just the
first chunk's WRITTEN extent (`lib/alloc.cyr:243-257`):

    var span = _heap_ptr - _heap_first_base;                       # still in chunk 1 (common, exact)
    if (_heap_base != _heap_first_base) {                          # a spill happened
        span = _heap_first_end - _heap_first_base;                 # → whole first chunk
    }
    if (span > 0) { _alloc_zero(_heap_first_base, span); }

The `_heap_base != _heap_first_base` test IS the multi-chunk
discriminator. macOS (`alloc_macos.cyr`) / Windows (`alloc_windows.cyr`)
are single contiguous regions → zero exactly `[_heap_base, _heap_ptr)`.

**Why a local `_alloc_zero`.** `alloc.cyr` is the foundational
allocator; making it call `memset` (lib/string.cyr) would give every
platform backend a string.cyr dependency. New un-gated
`_alloc_zero(base, n)` (alloc.cyr:105) is a plain `store64`/`store8`
loop, visible to every included backend (the `arena_*` precedent proves
un-gated alloc.cyr fns reach the backends). Fixed in ALL FOUR backends.

**THE DURABLE LESSON — a lib fix is not auto cycc-byte-identical when
cycc includes the lib.** cycc INCLUDES `lib/alloc.cyr`, AND `alloc_reset`
is reachable via the allocator vtable's `reset_fn` (address-taken → NOT
DCE-eliminated). So the scrub added **+424 B of code to cycc** — here it
happened to be absorbed into page-align padding (file size unchanged at
1,024,552 B), but that is luck, not a guarantee. Do NOT assume 'it's
just a lib change → cycc is byte-identical'; run the self-host fixpoint
(and, for any allocator/heap change, the two-step + seed-derive) for a
lib touched by code cycc includes.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "vex_encoding_vvvv_ones_complement_and_fma_cpuid_leaf"
description = "The v6.4.8/.9 SIMD arc emitted the FIRST VEX/AVX instructions the toolchain has ever produced (f32v8 256-bit: 2-byte C5 VEX for vaddps/vsubps/vmulps at v6.4.8; 3-byte C4 VEX for vfmadd231ps + vextractf128 at v6.4.9). Two traps bit here that hand-rolled hex-literal encoders on the SSE side never had. (1) THE VEX vvvv FIELD IS THE 1's-COMPLEMENT OF THE src1 REGISTER — the non-destructive 3-operand form encodes src1 in the inverted vvvv nibble, so a wrong-polarity vvvv assembles to a VALID instruction that silently reads the WRONG register (no crash, no decode error — just wrong math). It is INVISIBLE to a size/self-host check and can even pass a coarse value test if the mis-read register happens to alias. The only defense is to round-trip EVERY new VEX op through a real disassembler (llvm-mc / llvm-objdump) and diff the mnemonic+operands against intent — a disasm gate, not an eyeball. (2) FMA NEEDS A DIFFERENT CPUID LEAF/BIT THAN AVX2 — the runtime feature probe for vfmadd231ps is CPUID leaf 1, ECX bit 12 (simd_has_fma), NOT the AVX2 probe of leaf 7, EBX bit 5 (simd_has_avx2). They are genuinely separate capabilities (a CPU can have AVX2 without FMA in principle), so a wrapper that gates FMA on the AVX2 bit is wrong; each 256-bit op checks the RIGHT bit and falls back (2×SSE, or scalar for FMA). AVX2/FMA are NOT x86-64 baseline — the CPUID probe + fallback is mandatory, not optional. Corollary from the same arc: decode.cyr's VEX length-decode was DROPPED because it collided with a pre-existing SYSCALL/CPUID mis-decode of no-ModRM 0F opcodes (filed P3) — the disassembler gate uses external llvm tooling, not the in-tree decoder."
category = "cyrius"
---
The v6.4.4–.9 SIMD compute arc took x86 codegen from SSE-only into
**AVX/VEX** territory for the first time: v6.4.8 emits 2-byte (C5) VEX
for the f32v8 elementwise ops (vaddps/vsubps/vmulps on ymm), v6.4.9 adds
3-byte (C4) VEX for `vfmadd231ps` + the 8-lane `vextractf128` reduce.
Two traps here have NO analogue in the older hand-rolled SSE hex-literal
emitters (EMIT_F32V_LOOP etc. in src/backend/x86/float.cyr).

**TRAP 1 — the VEX `vvvv` field is the 1's-COMPLEMENT of src1.** The
non-destructive 3-operand VEX form encodes the first source register in
the `vvvv` nibble **inverted** (`~reg & 0xF`). Get the polarity wrong
and you STILL assemble a valid instruction — it just reads a DIFFERENT
register. No SIGILL, no decode failure, no assembler complaint: silently
wrong math. It is invisible to a size/self-host check (the bytes are a
legal encoding) and can even sneak past a coarse value test if the
mis-read register happens to hold a compatible value. **The only defense
is a disassembler gate:** round-trip every new VEX op through real
external tooling (llvm-mc / llvm-objdump), diff the decoded
mnemonic+operands against intent. Eyeballing the hex is not enough — the
whole point of the trap is that the wrong bytes look plausible.

**TRAP 2 — FMA and AVX2 are DIFFERENT CPUID capabilities.** The runtime
probe for `vfmadd231ps` is **CPUID leaf 1, ECX bit 12**
(`simd_has_fma`), which is a DIFFERENT leaf AND bit from the AVX2 probe
**CPUID leaf 7 (ECX=0), EBX bit 5** (`simd_has_avx2`). They are not
interchangeable — a part can advertise AVX2 without FMA — so gating the
FMA path on the AVX2 bit is a real bug. AVX2/FMA are NOT x86-64 baseline;
each 256-bit wrapper MUST probe the correct bit and branch to a fallback
(2×SSE for elementwise, scalar for FMA) when absent. This is mandatory,
not a nicety — a plain `-march=x86-64` box will `#UD` on an unguarded
`vaddps ymm`.

**Corollary — the in-tree decoder was NOT extended for VEX.** During the
arc, `decode.cyr`'s VEX length-decode was DROPPED because it collided
with a PRE-EXISTING mis-decode of no-ModRM 0F opcodes (SYSCALL/CPUID),
filed P3 (issues/2026-07-05-decode-len-mislengths-no-modrm-0f-opcodes.md).
So the disasm gate deliberately uses EXTERNAL llvm tooling, not the
in-tree `decode.cyr` — don't reach for the in-tree decoder to validate
VEX bytes; it can't, and fixing it is a separate tracked item.

**Recognize again.** Any first-of-a-new-encoding-family codegen
(VEX→EVEX, a new prefix, a new 3-operand form) where the encoding packs
an operand in a non-obvious way (inverted, split across bytes): assume a
plausible-but-wrong encoding will assemble cleanly and pass self-host,
and add a disasm round-trip gate BEFORE trusting value tests. And any new
ISA-extension op: find its EXACT CPUID leaf+bit (they are not grouped by
"AVX-ness") and wire a runtime fallback.

[[entries]]
name = "two_step_bootstrap_for_toplevel_array_sizing_and_legit_codegen_diff"
description = "Two paired differential-testing / bootstrap gotchas from the v6.4.x SIMD-and-fixes minor. (1) A LAYOUT change to how cycc sizes its OWN data needs a TWO-STEP BOOTSTRAP, same as a heap-map or W^X change. v6.4.10 fixed a P1 kernel-blocker: a bare top-level `var X[N]` was silently 8× UNDER-sized (N bytes, the fn-local default) instead of N×8 (the PARSE_GVAR_ARR slot size) when the array was declared AFTER the first bare top-level statement — because pass-2 PARSE_ARRAY fell through to the fn-local N-byte path. Fixed in parse_decl.cyr (~61): a bare NON-fn-local array gets N×8. Because cycc's OWN top-level arrays were mis-sized under the old compiler, flipping the sizing needs the two-step (old cycc → cc5b, then cc5b compiles itself; cycc==cc5b) — a plain one-step fixpoint can bake in the old layout. This joins W^X-default-flip and array-convention as the recurring 'cycc's own storage layout changed → two-step' class. (2) A BUG FIX legitimately shows differential codegen-diff>0 WITH status-diff=0 — the opposite of a pure refactor (which must be codegen-diff=0). When a fix CHANGES emitted bytes for the affected inputs (e.g. correct array sizing, a sign-extension fix, a correct register), the differential corpus SHOULD show codegen-diff>0 on exactly the affected programs while status-diff stays 0 (nothing that passed now fails); a codegen-diff=0 on a supposed behavior fix means the fix didn't actually fire. Read the two axes separately: status-diff guards regressions, codegen-diff measures blast radius."
category = "cyrius"
---
Two paired gotchas about READING differential/bootstrap results, from the
v6.4.x minor's fixes (not the SIMD codegen itself).

**(1) A change to how cycc sizes its OWN top-level data needs a TWO-STEP
BOOTSTRAP.** v6.4.10 fixed a P1 kernel-blocker: a bare top-level
`var X[N]` was silently **8× under-sized** — it got N BYTES (the
fn-local array default) instead of N×8 (the `PARSE_GVAR_ARR` slot size)
**whenever it was declared AFTER the first bare top-level statement**,
because at that point pass-2 `PARSE_ARRAY` fell through to the fn-local
N-byte path instead of the global slot path. Fix (parse_decl.cyr ~61): a
bare **non-fn-local** array is sized N×8. The catch: **cycc's own
top-level arrays were affected**, so the sizing flip cannot be validated
by a one-step fixpoint alone — the old (mis-sizing) cycc could bake the
old layout into the new binary. Run the **two-step**: old cycc → `cc5b`,
then `cc5b` compiles itself, assert `cycc == cc5b` (+ seed-derive). This
is the SAME class as the W^X default-on flip and the local-vs-global
array-convention gotcha: **whenever a change alters cycc's own storage
layout / data sizing, it's a two-step, not a one-step.**

**(2) A real behavior fix SHOULD show differential codegen-diff > 0 with
status-diff = 0** — and that is CORRECT, not a red flag. The differential
harness reports two independent axes over the corpus:
  - **status-diff** — did any program's pass/fail status change? For a
    fix this MUST stay 0 (nothing that compiled+ran now breaks).
  - **codegen-diff** — did the emitted BYTES change, and for which
    programs? For a fix that changes what's emitted (array sizing, a
    sign-extension, a corrected register) this SHOULD be > 0, on exactly
    the affected programs.

The trap is conflating the two. A pure REFACTOR must be
codegen-diff = 0 (identical bytes). A BUG FIX is the opposite: if a
supposed behavior fix shows codegen-diff = 0, the fix **did not fire** —
you changed a path nothing exercises, or guarded it wrong. So read the
axes separately: status-diff is the regression guard; codegen-diff is the
blast-radius measurement. `codegen-diff > 0 && status-diff = 0` on the
programs you expected is exactly the signature of a landed, contained fix.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_transcendental_polyfill_dispatch_and_dekker_dd_range_reduction"
category = "cyrius"
description = """
aarch64 has no native transcendentals — x86 gets them from x87 (fsin/fcos,
f2xm1/fscale for exp/exp2, fyl2x for ln/log2, fpatan for atan). Every f64
transcendental on aarch64 is therefore a lib/math.cyr POLYFILL that the aarch64
backend dispatches to. The dispatch pattern (v5.7.31 exp/ln → v6.4.16 sin/cos →
v6.4.25 exp2/atan) is identical: EF64_<OP>(S) in src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr does
`var pf = _FINDFN_CSTR(S, "_f64_<op>_polyfill", <len>); if (pf<0) ERR_MSG(...requires
include lib/math.cyr...); EPUSHR(S); ECALLPOPS(S,1); ECALLFIX(S,pf);` — i.e. it emits
a 1-arg call into the stdlib fn resolved by name. The x86 EF64_<OP> in
src/backend/x86/emit.cyr keeps emitting native x87 bytes and is NOT touched. THREE
consequences that trip you up:

(1) x86 cycc stays BYTE-IDENTICAL across a pure-aarch64 transcendental change. The
x86 fork (src/main.cyr) includes ONLY backend/x86/emit.cyr — not the aarch64 emit —
and cycc does NOT include lib/math.cyr (it's a stdlib, not compiler source). So
adding a polyfill + rerouting the aarch64 EF64_* changes ONLY the three aarch64 forks
(main_aarch64{,_macho,_native}.cyr) and lib/math.cyr. The x86 self-host fixpoint and
the differential (343 inputs, codegen-diff=0) are trivially green — that is CORRECT,
not a missing test. The real verification is the aarch64 self-host fixpoint (build the
x86-hosted aarch64-emitting cross-compiler `main_aarch64.cyr | cycc`, then `| itself`,
cmp — byte-identical under qemu) PLUS running the actual polyfill on real hardware
(pi + ecb) via a vr01_ tcyr. A green x86 run proves nothing about the aarch64 path.

(2) The polyfill must NOT recurse into the builtin it backs. _f64_exp2_polyfill uses
round/sub/mul/add/to (NOT f64_exp2); _f64_atan_polyfill uses gt/div/sub/add/mul/neg +
bit ops (NOT f64_atan). Each polyfill compiles on x86 too (arch-independent body) but
is only INVOKED on aarch64 — x86 keeps the x87 path.

(3) Large-argument trig range reduction on a machine with only f64 add/sub/mul (no
scalar FMA builtin) requires a Dekker double-double. The single-constant reduction
`r = x - round(x*2/pi)*pi_2` catastrophically cancels for large |x| (rel err ~5e-2 at
1e15). The fix is a NO-FMA Dekker TwoProduct: split via (2^27+1), form x*(2/pi) and
frac*(pi/2) as hi+lo words with the constants pre-split into _SH/_SL halves so the
product is exact without fma. Validated numerically (Python float64 == IEEE754) BEFORE
touching the compiler — double-double is exact to ~1e-16 through |x|≈1e15; triple-double
is NOT needed. GATE it (|x| >= 8192) so the cheap small-angle path is untouched and
stays BYTE-IDENTICAL — the issue's "small path unchanged" is a byte-identity
requirement, not a vibe. The dd quadrant k must come from the dd sum (round(ph+plo)),
NOT a naive round(x*2/pi) which is off-by-one for large x — so the dd block writes k_f
locally, and (no multi-return in this compiler) the ~14-line block is inlined verbatim
in both sin and cos, same as their small-path reduction already is. Keep each emitted
Horner/dd line under 120 chars up front (cyrlint gate) by accumulating into a stepwise
var, not one deep f64_add(f64_add(...)) nest — cyrfmt won't split it for you.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "new_pe_reroute_fn_needs_return0_stubs_in_aarch64_and_cx_backends_for_symbol_resolution"
category = "cyrius"
description = """
Adding a new kernel32/PE reroute emitter (an `E<NAME>_PE(S)` fn — TerminateProcess,
GetCurrentProcessId, ProcessPrng, etc.) is THREE edits, not one, and the third is
easy to forget because local testing never exercises it:

(1) the REAL impl in src/backend/x86/emit.cyr (emits the call bytes; often just
    `_pe_ensure_<x>(S); return _pe_call_Narg_aligned(S, _pe_<x>_get());`),
(2) the kernel32 IAT import registration in src/backend/pe/emit.cyr
    (`var _pe_<x>_ready/_idx; fn _pe_ensure_<x>(S){ _pe_register_kernel32(S,&_idx,"<Name>"); }`),
(3) the parse_expr.cyr dispatch (`if (sc_num == 0xF0NN) { if (argc==K) { E<NAME>_PE(S); ... } }`)
    AND the routed-range warning string.

The TRAP: parse_expr.cyr is compiled into EVERY fork (main.cyr, main_win.cyr,
main_x86_macho.cyr, main_aarch64{,_macho,_native}.cyr, main_cx.cyr). The dispatch's
CALL to E<NAME>_PE is emitted on every fork (the `if (_TARGET_PE==1)` is a runtime
constant, so the call is still COMPILED, just dead). cycc requires the symbol to
RESOLVE at compile time. But the real impl lives ONLY in backend/x86/emit.cyr,
which is included by exactly THREE forks (x86-Linux, PE, x86-macho). The aarch64
forks include backend/aarch64/emit.cyr and the cx fork includes backend/cx/emit.cyr
— NEITHER pulls in the x86 backend. So those forks need a `return-0` STUB:
`fn E<NAME>_PE(S){ return 0; }` in backend/aarch64/emit.cyr AND
`fn E<NAME>_PE(S): i64 { return 0; }` in backend/cx/emit.cyr (note the aarch64
stubs omit the `: i64`, the cx stubs keep it — match each file's local convention).
There is already a big block of these stubs (EWAIT_PE, EGETEXIT_PE, ESETHANDLE_PE,
EGETCURPID_PE, EPROCPRNG_PE, …) — add the new one next to them.

WHY IT BITES SILENTLY: local Linux self-host + `cat prog | CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN=1 cc_win`
+ wine all use forks that include backend/x86/emit.cyr, so they compile fine and pass.
The stub gap is invisible until a fork WITHOUT the x86 backend is built — which the
cross-OS gate does when it builds cycc_cx (the cx compiler) on cass. v6.4.26 shipped
ETERMINATE_PE with the x86 impl + pe import + parse dispatch, all green locally +
wine (terminate test 3/3, self-host byte-identical), then cass SELFHOST_FAILED with
`error: refusing to emit binary with 1 reachable undefined function(s): ETERMINATE_PE`
— the cycc_cx build. Adding the two stubs fixed it. LESSON: after adding an E*_PE
reroute, immediately build the aarch64 AND cx cross-compilers locally
(`cat src/main_aarch64.cyr | build/cycc >/dev/null` and `cat src/main_cx.cyr |
build/cycc >/dev/null`) — an undefined-function error there is the missing stub, and
it's far cheaper to catch than a cass round-trip. This is the same class as the
`_macho_arm_routes` stub-for-symbol-resolution note already in backend/x86/emit.cyr.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "agnos_ao_access_mode_bits_are_1to1_with_linux_o_flags_pass_through_dont_fold_verify_under_mirshi"
category = "cyrius"
description = """
The agnos file ABI reuses Linux's low-2-bit access-mode encoding EXACTLY:
AO_RDONLY=0x0, AO_WRONLY=0x1, AO_RDWR=0x2 — identical to O_RDONLY/WRONLY/RDWR
(lib/syscalls_x86_64_agnos.cyr). So in `file_open`'s `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`
flag-translation, the access mode PASSES THROUGH: `ao = ao | (flags & 3);`. Do NOT
write `if ((flags & 3) != 0) { ao = ao | 0x1; }` — that folds BOTH WRONLY(1) and
RDWR(2) to WRONLY(1), so an O_RDWR open becomes write-only on agnos and every later
read on that fd returns -1 (v6.4.27 P1: `io.cyr:80`; patra "cannot read header"
reopening its B-tree O_RDWR under sit/mirshi). The higher bits are NOT 1:1 and DO
need per-bit mapping (O_CREAT 64→AO_CREAT 0x100, O_TRUNC 512→0x200, O_APPEND
1024→0x400) — only the access-mode nibble is a straight pass-through.

TWO meta-lessons: (1) an agnos flag/ABI-translation bug is INVISIBLE to Linux runs
and to a bare `--agnos` COMPILE — both pass. The only thing that catches it is
running the agnos binary under **mirshi** (~/Repos/mirshi/build/mirshi <bin>, the
agnos→Linux ptrace supervisor) and asserting real I/O behavior (here: create+write,
close, reopen O_RDWR, read → exit 42; the buggy fold exits 4 on the read). Build a
minimal exit-code program (NOT the assert framework — it uses raw syscall(1,…) writes
that aren't agnos-portable) and check $? under mirshi. (2) When the same helper is
COPIED into a vendored stdlib (here lib/sakshi.cyr:88 had the identical fold), fix the
cyrius-native site in a cyrius release but fix the fold at its SOURCE repo
(~/Repos/sakshi) + patch-bump + re-vendor — never patch lib/sakshi.cyr directly (it's
materialized; the edit evaporates on the next `cyrius deps`).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_neon_simd_emitter_mirror_f64v_loop_verify_encodings_with_llvm_mc_and_value_form_params_die_on_win64"
category = "cyrius"
description = """
Implementing an aarch64 NEON SIMD emitter (SIMD Phase 5 — EMIT_F32V_LOOP/FMADD/DOT,
and the pending EMIT_IVEC_*/EMIT_F32V8_*) is far easier than it looks because the
loop + addressing + reduce infrastructure already exists for f64: mirror
`EMIT_F64V_LOOP` / `EMIT_F64V_DOT` in src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr. The pattern:
`mov x10,x0`(n) `movz x11,#0`(index); loop `cmp x11,x10`/`b.ge done`; per operand
`EFLLOAD(slot)` → `mov x12,x0` → `add x12,x12,x11,lsl #K` → `ldr q, [x12]`; the NEON
op; `str q, [x12]`; `add x11,x11,#LANES`; `EJMP`/`EPATCH`. To go from f64v (2 lanes)
to f32v4 (4 lanes) change exactly three things:
  - element stride: `lsl #3` (×8) → `lsl #2` (×4)   [encoding 0x8B0B0D8C → 0x8B0B098C]
  - the NEON op:    `.2d` → `.4s`  (fadd .4s=0x4E21D400, fsub=0x4EA1D400, fmul=0x6E21DC00)
  - counter:        `add x11,#2` → `add x11,#4`      [0x9100096B → 0x9100116B]
The 128-bit `ldr q`/`str q` are identical (width-agnostic). Horizontal reduce: f64
needs ONE `faddp d0,v2.2d`; f32v4 needs TWO `faddp v2.4s` (4→2→1) then `fmov w0,s2`.

THREE traps:
(1) VERIFY EVERY hand-encoded word with `llvm-mc --arch=aarch64 --show-encoding`
    (`printf 'fadd v0.4s,v0.4s,v1.4s\\n...' | llvm-mc ...`). Do NOT hand-compute A64
    encodings from memory — the imm6 shift field + the sz bit are easy to get wrong.
(2) MATCH x86 ROUNDING for fmadd/dot: x86 SSE has no FMA at f32v4 width, so its
    EMIT_F32V_FMADD/DOT do mulps THEN addps (two roundings). Use `fmul`+`fadd`, NOT
    the fused `fmla`, so the two arches agree bit-for-bit on the test values. (The
    pre-existing f64 dot DOES use fmla and already diverges cross-arch — don't copy
    that for new f32 work.)
(3) HISTORICAL (fixed v6.4.31): value-form f32v4/f64v2/f64v4 PARAMS/RETURNS once
    hard-errored on Win64 PE ("value-form params not yet supported on Win64 PE —
    use pointer-form"). As of v6.4.31 they are address-passed (by hidden pointer) on
    Win64 — parse_decl.cyr "v6.4.31: value-form SIMD arg → address-passed on Win64",
    and main_win.cyr PP_PREDEFINEs CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS — so a value-form SIMD
    test now runs on cass and CAN be a full `vr01_` cross-OS gate. (When first landing
    an aarch64 SIMD emitter the strict-XFAIL machinery in ci.yml still auto-forces XFAIL
    removal: the corpus gate is `test $xpass -eq 0`, so the test flips RED (XPASS) the
    moment your emitter lands — that RED is the cue to remove it from XFAIL, not a
    regression.)
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cx_simd_emitters_are_easy_the_real_bugs_are_local_addressing_eloadlocaladdr_stub_and_regalloc_disp_nonuniformity"
description = "cx SIMD (f64v_*/f32v_*/iv_*) emitters mirror the f64 loop trivially; the real blockers are 2 pre-existing cx local-addressing bugs the SIMD pointer-stash exposes: ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR was a return-0 stub (&local aliases to addr 0) and the scalar load/store disp path omitted the regalloc/ret_stash reservation EFLADDR already had. Plus cxvm >> is LOGICAL."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cx", "cxvm", "simd", "codegen", "regalloc", "frame-layout", "v6.4.32"]
content = """
Adding SIMD to the cx bytecode backend (`src/backend/cx/emit.cyr`) is the same
"mirror the f64 loop" job as the aarch64 NEON one — a single `_CX_VLOOP_BIN` driver
covers f64v/f32v/iv add/sub/mul/div, and the special verbs (dot/scale/axpy/fmadd/
sqrt/dp8) are short variants. cx is a scalar register VM, so a "SIMD" op is just a
per-lane loop; it's a byte-exact **correctness oracle**, not hardware SIMD. Three
cx-specific encoding facts:

- **n arrives in r0, NOT a frame slot.** The intrinsic dispatch leaves the count in
  r0 (like x86 rax after the arg stash); every emitter's first instruction is
  `mov r8, r0` to save it before r0 is reused for values. A `nslot` param on the
  loop driver is a trap — there is no such slot.
- **f32 = compute-in-f64, round per lane.** Widen both operands (new cxvm opcode
  `f32widen` 0x66 = cvtss2sd), run the scalar op in f64, narrow the result
  (`f32narrow` 0x67 = cvtsd2ss). One packed op → one rounding, bit-exact vs `addps`/
  `mulps`. `fmadd` narrows twice to match `mulps`+`addps` (two roundings). `f64v_sqrt`
  compiles to the new `fsqrt` 0x68.
- **cxvm `>>` (op 0x24) is LOGICAL, not arithmetic.** So the usual sign-extend trick
  `(x << 56) >> 56` does NOT work for the i8 lane in `EMIT_IVEC_DP8`. Use
  `b − ((b >> 7) << 8)` instead (subtract 256 iff the sign bit is set); `a` stays
  zero-extended, accumulate in i64.

**But the emitters are the easy part — SIMD is a tripwire for two PRE-EXISTING cx
local-addressing bugs** that never bit before because nothing else stashed pointers
in frame slots next to stack arrays:

1. **`ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR` was a `return 0` stub.** `&local` emitted nothing and left
   r0 = 0, so `&a` and `&b` for two different locals BOTH evaluated to address 0 — a
   silent aliasing bug for *any* address-of-a-stack-variable, not just SIMD. It only
   surfaced as "f64v_add(&R,&A,&B,n) writes garbage" because SIMD is the first common
   caller of `&local`. Fix: emit `r0 = fp − adisp`.
2. **cx local-slot disp non-uniformity.** `EFLADDR` (address-of) folded the
   `_cur_fn_regalloc` + `_cur_fn_ret_stash` reservation into its slot disp, but the
   SCALAR load/store path (`EFLLOAD`/`EFLSTORE`, the f64v2 load/store pairs,
   `ESTOREPARM`, and the SIMD stash loader `_cx_ld_local`) did NOT. A fn using both
   stack arrays (addressed via EFLADDR) and the SIMD pointer stash (stored via
   EFLSTORE) had the stash slots land on top of regalloc-shifted array storage. Fix:
   route EVERY local-disp site through one `_cx_ldisp(idx)` helper that always adds
   the reservation. Lesson: when one addressing path folds a frame reservation, ALL
   of them must — an address-of and a load of the same slot index must resolve to the
   same byte.

**Debugging method that separated regression from pre-existing (reusable):**
- Isolate SIMD correctness with synthetic **manual exit-code** probes (`if (x != y)
  syscall(60, code)`), NOT assert_eq — library code drags in its own frame/global
  behavior and muddies the signal.
- To prove regression-vs-pre-existing, `git stash` the `src/` change and rebuild the
  cx toolchain (`build/cycc < src/main_cx.cyr > cycc_cx`; `build/cycc < programs/
  cxvm.cyr > cxvm`) — `build/cycc` is the committed prior-release x86 binary,
  unchanged by your edit, so it reproduces the exact prior-release cx behavior. Run
  the SAME repro against it. Here it proved the frame fix has NO general `var x =
  <call>` regression with large (4-array) frames, and split off a genuinely
  pre-existing cx bug: `var x = assert_summary()` mis-captures after a prior
  `assert_eq` mutated the assert globals (fails on the prior release, zero SIMD/
  arrays). Consequence: the standard tcyr ending `var r = assert_summary();` is
  unreliable on cx — end cx gates with `return assert_summary();` directly.

**Verifying cross-OS:** the cx round-trip fixture in `scripts/cross-os-selfhost.sh` is
built by the LOCAL cycc_cx (shipped `.cyx`) AND recompiled by each host's NATIVE
cycc_cx, then run on the native cxvm. Upgrade it from a bare write to run a real SIMD
op (`f64v_add`+`f64v_sqrt` over local arrays → exit 42) so a green ecb/pi/cass leg
proves the cx SIMD emitters + frame fix + new opcodes execute on real hardware — not
the placebo "a write halts" that CLAUDE.md warns about.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "pe_kernel32_reroutes_fixed_sub_rsp_only_aligns_on_right_parity_closehandle_faults_on_misalign_wine_cannot_repro"
description = "Windows PE kernel32 reroutes must DYNAMICALLY 16-align rsp before the call — a fixed `sub rsp, 0x28` is a latent bug. Win64 requires rsp = 0 (mod 16) immediately before a `call`; a fixed sub-rsp achieves that only when the caller's rsp lands on the exact parity the offset assumes, which depends on the function's frame size + expr-stack depth + call chain — so it silently works or faults by context. CloseHandle (and any kernel32 fn with an internal aligned SSE access, e.g. movaps) faults 0xC0000005 on an 8-misaligned stack; GetExitCodeProcess/WaitForSingleObject happen to tolerate it, which is why the crash localizes to CloseHandle though its siblings share the bug. EXPOSED at v6.4.44 by the async subprocess resume (lib/async_win.cyr _async_process_task): main -> task_join -> _async_pump -> _async_step -> fncall1 -> resume -> CloseHandle is a deep stack, and TWO sequential async_run_process calls crashed on real cass while a SINGLE call and the BATCHED (spawn-all-then-join) form worked — pure alignment-parity luck. CreateProcessW's ECREATEPROC_PE was already rbx-anchor-aligned at v6.0.51, but its own comment asserted 'the <=4-arg reroutes tolerate a misalign this 10-arg call does not' — that generalization was FALSE. FIX: route each of the 8 fixed-offset reroutes (ECLOSE_PE/EWAIT_PE/EGETEXIT_PE/ESETHANDLE_PE/ECREATEPIPE_PE/EGETCMDLINE_PE/EGETTICKS_PE/EMMAP_PE) through the existing rbx-anchored helpers (_pe_call_0/2/3/4arg_aligned, _pe_srw_call_1arg: push rbx; mov rbx,rsp; and rsp,-16; sub rsp,0x20; call; mov rsp,rbx; pop rbx). cycc SHRANK ~4 KB (inline reroutes -> shared helpers). HOW TO RECOGNIZE: a real-Windows-ONLY 0xC0000005 that WINE cannot reproduce (WINE aligns leniently -> returned 42 throughout) AND flips crash<->pass with call-depth / caller-frame changes -> suspect a fixed-`sub rsp` reroute, not the callee. Localize with a WriteFile (syscall(1,1,...)) trace bracketing each op; bisect step-0 vs resume vs the post-fncall1 loop code with manual GQCS + fncall1 replays. Self-host + seed-derive stay byte-identical (PE-emit-only; cycc's Linux codegen never calls these). See CHANGELOG [6.4.44]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "win64_retptr_shifted_deep_stack_param_homing_offbyone_prologue_postloop_missing_pp_shift_f32v8_make_lanes_567"
description = "On Win64 PE a by-value SIMD/struct return uses a HIDDEN RETPTR in RCX, so every user arg shifts down one ABI slot (a,b,c -> rdx/r8/r9; d,e,f,g,h -> stack). The callee prologue homes params in TWO places and they must agree on that retptr shift: the IN-loop homing (parse_fn.cyr ~3321) computes `_pp_shift = (_cur_fn_ret_stash>0 && !aarch64) ? 1 : 0` and calls ESTOREPARM(int_pc+_pp_shift, li) -- pidx shifted for the source Win64 slot, lidx contiguous for the dest local slot. But the POST-loop that homes the DEEP stack params (pidx>=6, parse_fn.cyr ~3404) did `ESTOREPARM(spi, spi, pc)` -- using spi as BOTH pidx AND lidx with NO shift. Correct only with no retptr. With one, it homed each deep stack param ONE LOCAL SLOT TOO DEEP (dest lidx = true+1) and stopped one param short (spi<pc never reached the last source idx), so the first excluded stack param was never homed, the rest got the previous param's value, and the last param's incoming bytes were ignored. The body reads each param at the contiguous -0x30-lidx*8 slot via FINDLOCAL, so it diverged. SYMPTOM: f32v8_make(a..h) returned lanes 0-4 correct but lanes 5,6,7 = garbage/shifted on real cass; Linux/SysV all 8 lanes correct (SIMD returns via the xmm PAIR there, no retptr, so _pl_shift==0 and the emit is textually the old code -> self-host byte-identical). FIX: mirror _pp_shift in the post-loop -- `pidx = spi + _pl_shift` (shifted source), `lidx = spi` (contiguous dest), start `spi = 6 - _pl_shift`, guard `pc + _pl_shift > 6` (also catches the exact-6-param retptr case pc>6 dropped). PRE-EXISTING since v6.4.31 (Win64 value-form SIMD params/returns); latent for many minors because vr01_f32v8_ctor.tcyr carried a stale comment saying it 'skips cass', but v6.4.31 made CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS true on PE so the native cass cycc actually RAN the f32v8 assertions -- surfaced only when the v6.4.44 permanent Windows async gate ran the full vr01_ corpus on cass. HOW TO RECOGNIZE: a value-form-SIMD/struct-returning fn with >6 params whose DEEP args (3rd+ stack arg) are wrong on Win64 but correct on Linux -> suspect the retptr-shift being applied inconsistently between the two prologue-homing sites. Confirm by disassembling BOTH the prologue homing (`mov rax,[rbp+src]; mov [rbp-dst],rax`) and the body read (FINDLOCAL disp = -((lidx+1)*8)-regalloc*8-ret_stash) -- they must land on the same local slot. The caller (ECALLPOPS / parse_decl retptr push) was CORRECT; the bug is callee-side. Same '_cur_fn_ret_stash disp<->idx off-by-one' class as project_v6431_win64_valueform_simd, here the one remaining site. See CHANGELOG [6.4.44]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "shift_right_split_gtgt_stays_logical_gtgtgt_is_arithmetic_reverse_of_js_never_flip_gtgt"
description = "v6.4.46 added `>>>` as the ARITHMETIC (sign-preserving) right shift and KEPT `>>` LOGICAL (zero-fill) — the REVERSE of JS/Java (there `>>` is arithmetic, `>>>` logical). The split is NOT arbitrary: `>>` MUST stay logical because two load-bearing sites depend on it. (1) CRYPTO ROTATES: keccak/sha/chacha rotate via `(x << n) | (x >> (64-n))` — logical `>>` is required or the high bits sign-extend and the rotate is wrong; flipping `>>` to arithmetic silently miscompiles SHA-3, cascading into TLS (tls_native_alpn went red). (2) THE COMPILER'S OWN UNION FLAG: `ISUNION()` is `fcount >> 63` — logical `>>` gives 1 for a tagged (high-bit-set) fcount, arithmetic `>>` gives -1 and the union sizeof/layout miscompiles (structs went red). BOTH were caught ONLY by check.sh's corpus — the self-host fixpoint AND seed-derive stayed GREEN because cycc's own source didn't exercise the exact miscompiled path until the corpus ran (a reminder that green self-host is necessary, not sufficient). LESSON: do NOT 'fix' `>>` to arithmetic to match JS/Java — it is a deliberate, load-bearing choice; route signed-shift needs through the NEW `>>>` (IR_ASHR=26; x86 `sar rax,cl` = 48 D3 F8; aarch64 `asrv` = 0x9AC12800; cx op 37). The drishti bug that motivated it was a signed value silently zero-filled by `>>`; the fix is `>>>`, never a `>>` semantics flip."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["shift", "logical-shift", "arithmetic-shift", "operator", "crypto", "rotate", "union", "isunion", "sign-extension", "green-selfhost-not-sufficient", "v6.4.46"]

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_auto_calls_main_top_level_var_main_double_runs_and_the_execve_helper_dispatch_pattern"
description = "Three program-shape gotchas surfaced building the `cyrsign-efi` UEFI helper (v6.4.47). (1) DOUBLE-RUN: cyrius AUTO-CALLS `fn main` at program end, so a file that ALSO has a top-level `var z = main();` runs main TWICE (the crypto helper printed its output twice). Fix: end such a program with `var _rc = main(); syscall(60, _rc);` — the explicit exit syscall fires BEFORE the auto-main call, so main runs exactly once and the process exits with its rc. (2) args_init FIRST: `argc()`/`argv()` read a buffer `args_init()` fills from /proc/self/cmdline (lib/args.cyr, under `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX`) — call `args_init()` before any argc/argv or you get argc 0 / garbage argv. (3) HELPER-DISPATCH: `cyrius sign-efi` does NOT link sigil's ~14 MB of crypto into the CLI — it `sys_execve`s a SEPARATE `cyrsign-efi` binary (located via find_tools/_tools_dir), keeping the CLI delta +144 B. The `[bins]` manifest in cyrius.cyml drives install.sh building the helper (`_build_tool sign-efi programs/cyrsign-efi.cyr`). ARGV-ARRAY SIZE trap: `var _se_argv: i64[16]` = 16 SLOTS (correct); `var _se_argv[16]` = 16 BYTES (2 slots) and overflows the execve argv. Windows `sys_execve` is a stub returning -1, so the dispatcher is Linux/macOS only."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["main", "auto-call", "double-run", "args_init", "argv", "execve", "helper-dispatch", "cyrsign-efi", "sign-efi", "var-array-bytes-vs-slots", "v6.4.47"]

[[entries]]
name = "codebuf_is_growable_soft_warning_grow_not_exercised_by_selfhost_force_it_with_a_low_cap_build"
description = "The compiler's code buffer (generated machine code) has been GROWABLE since v6.2.0 — do NOT panic at a 'code buffer at N%' warning or treat the initial cap as a hard wall (a consumer, thoth, hit this: '91% of the ~3.14 MB cap' was a SOFT warning against the small initial allocation; the build succeeds and relocates transparently). v6.4.49 tuned it: initial cap 3 MiB → 8 MiB, and the grow went from ×2 doubling to +8 MiB LINEAR steps (8→16→…→64, dividing evenly into the 64 MiB ceiling — no doubling overshoot). THREE traps for anyone touching it: (1) the INITIAL cap can't just be bumped in-heap — the old 3 MiB region at S+0x41A000 is boxed in by file_map at 0x71A000, so the 8 MiB initial is an off-heap alloc(8388608) (the fixed region is now vestigial; alloc is proven at that init point — _vsgn_base already allocs 8 MiB there, so it's cybs/seed-safe). (2) the cx bytecode backend keeps a SEPARATE fixed 512 KiB region at 0x54A000 (cx/emit.cyr checks cp >= 524288, never _codebuf_base/_codebuf_grow) — cx's _codebuf_base/_codebuf_cap are vestigial; leave them. (3) — the big one — the self-host fixpoint AND check.sh do NOT exercise the grow: cycc emits ~1.09 MB, far under the 8 MiB initial, so no grow ever fires during self-compile or the test corpus. A change to _codebuf_grow (util.cyr) will pass all normal gates while being broken. To actually test the grow: build a throwaway cycc with a tiny initial cap (sed _codebuf_cap = 65536 + _codebuf_base = alloc(65536) into main.cyr, build, RESTORE main.cyr), have it compile the full cycc source (forces a grow), and cmp its output to a normal build — byte-identical proves the grow emits correct code. The warning lives only in main.cyr + main_win.cyr (the x86/Win drivers with an end-of-compile summary); aarch64/macho drivers have no summary block, so no codebuf warning there (pre-existing)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codebuf", "grow", "growable", "off-heap", "alloc", "self-host-doesnt-cover", "force-grow-test", "cx-separate-region", "warning", "v6.4.49"]

[[entries]]
name = "capacity_warnings_were_driver_fork_duplicated_now_one_shared_capacity_warnings_helper_all_7_drivers"
description = "v6.4.50 closed the gap the v6.4.49 note ends on: the end-of-compile soft capacity warnings (`warning: fn_table/identifier buffer/var_table/fixup_table/string_data at 85%`, `code buffer at 75%`) lived INLINE and byte-identically DUPLICATED in only 2 of the 7 compiler drivers — main.cyr (x86-Linux) + main_win.cyr (Win64). The other 5 — main_aarch64{,_native,_macho}.cyr, main_x86_macho.cyr, main_cx.cyr — emitted ZERO capacity warnings, which is why a consumer's codebuf warning (thoth) only ever showed on x86 and never on the macOS/aarch64 toolchains. FIX: extract the block into ONE shared `fn _capacity_warnings(S, codebuf_cap, codebuf_growable)` in common/util.cyr (next to _codebuf_grow), called from every driver's end-of-compile path. KEY DESIGN POINTS for anyone touching it: (1) it reads CYRIUS_STATS itself via `_read_env` — which has TWO definitions, the real one in backend/common/runtime.cyr (native forks) and a `return 0` STUB in backend/cx/emit.cyr (cx fork excludes runtime.cyr), so the call resolves in ALL forks (a util.cyr FORWARD call to _read_env is fine — direct calls resolve via the fixup table regardless of include order; only _FINDFN_CSTR runtime lookups need pre-registration). (2) FORK-AGNOSTIC: every metric goes through its accessor (GFNC/GNPOS/GVCNT/GFCNT/GSPOS/GCP) — no hardcoded S+offset — and the fixed table caps (8192/262144/8192/1048576/2097152) are identical across all forks. (3) The ONLY fork-varying line is the code-buffer warning: the 6 NATIVE forks pass `(67108864, 1)` — the 64 MiB growable off-heap codebuf, message '…% of 64 MiB ceiling (N bytes, +8 MiB/grow)' (byte-identical to the old inline text); cx passes `(524288, 0)` — its code lives in the SEPARATE fixed 512 KiB region at 0x54A000 (the shared _codebuf_base/_codebuf_cap it sets to 3 MiB are VESTIGIAL for cx), message '…% of the 512 KiB cx region (N bytes)' with no grow advice. cx had NO overflow guard on that region, so this is its first heads-up. (4) INSERTION POINT matters: call it AFTER FIXUP(S) (GCP is final) and BEFORE EMITELF/output-write; on cx the warnings go to stderr (fd 2) and the .cyx to stdout (fd 1), so they don't corrupt output. VERIFICATION (this is a logic-preserving DRIVER code-move, NOT a codegen change): cycc self-host fixpoint byte-identical, seed-derive gen2==gen3==build/cycc (cybs compiled the ~30-call helper cleanly — watch for the cybs per-fn call-reference limit, but it held), differential corpus 0/0 in BOTH default and DCE modes, dead-code floor unchanged (64/24633), x86 behavioral: the var_table warning fires with byte-identical text old-vs-new AND the compiled binary is byte-identical. Cross-OS pi+ecb+cass all SELFHOST_OK + LIBTEST_OK. NOTE cx's var_table warning (85% of 8192) is UNREACHABLE — cx hard-errors at 4096 initialized globals first — so the reachable cx warning is the code-buffer one. See CHANGELOG [6.4.50], roadmap Pin 3."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["capacity-warning", "driver-fork", "_capacity_warnings", "util.cyr", "read_env-dual-definition", "cx-separate-region", "fork-agnostic", "logic-preserving", "differential-corpus", "v6.4.50"]

[[entries]]
name = "output_buf_1gib_alloc_caps_on_macos_windows_linux_overcommits_platform_adaptive_output_cap_fallback"
description = "v6.4.51 raised output_buf 16 MiB→1 GiB by moving it OFF-HEAP (`_output_base = alloc(1073741824)`, routed through ~19 emit/fixup/write sites via a `_output_base` seam in common/util.cyr, replacing the fixed S+0x4D9D000 region). It works on Linux but cycc SIGSEGV'd on real macOS (ecb) + failed on Windows (cass) — because the macOS and Windows ALLOCATORS CANNOT PRODUCE A >256 MiB SINGLE REGION. macOS (lib/alloc_macos.cyr) caps at its 256 MiB initial reservation: growth uses a HINTED 1 MiB mmap at _heap_end, macOS does NOT honor mmap address hints, so the first grow past the reservation returns a different base and the contiguity guard fails → alloc returns 0. Windows (lib/alloc_windows.cyr) has a FIXED reservation with NO grow (`if (new_ptr > _heap_end) return 0`). Only Linux (lib/alloc.cyr) handles it — `_linux_new_chunk` mmaps a fresh chunk SIZED TO THE REQUEST and Linux overcommits anon mmap. When alloc(1 GiB) returns 0, `_output_base` was 0 → cycc emits its output image to address 0 → SIGSEGV. FIX (ship-Linux-now, .52-for-the-rest): PLATFORM-ADAPTIVE fallback — each of the 6 native drivers does `_output_base = alloc(1073741824); if (_output_base == 0) { _output_base = S+0x4D9D000; _output_cap = 16777216; } else { _output_cap = 1073741824; }`, and ALL the output-size caps (`_check_output_cap` in runtime.cyr + the x86/fixup.cyr ELF-exe `final_sz`/`.so`/`.o` caps — there are FOUR, not one; a >16 MiB binary trips whichever fires first) key on the `_output_cap` GLOBAL, never a hardcoded literal. So Linux gets 1 GiB, macOS/Windows keep the pre-.51 16 MiB (no crash, no regression). The dedicated macOS/Windows large-alloc path (a direct unhinted mmap on macOS / dedicated VirtualAlloc on Windows, mirroring _linux_new_chunk) is v6.4.52 — see issue 2026-07-11-macos-windows-large-single-allocation-path.md. LESSON: raising ALLOC_MAX (the size guard) does NOT make a large alloc succeed if the underlying per-OS allocator physically can't produce the region — the guard and the mechanism are different things. TWO test-harness traps hit while verifying this: (1) `cyrius build` uses the INSTALLED toolchain cycc (~/.cyrius/versions/<v>/bins/cycc), NOT ./build/cycc — so a COMPILER change (caps, codegen) must be tested with raw `cat prog.cyr | build/cycc` (or reinstall); `cyrius build` is only valid for LIB changes. A >16 MiB test 'failing' with the old cap was just the stale installed cycc. (2) `cyrius build` takes output as a POSITIONAL arg (`cyrius build <src> <out>`), NOT `-o` — `cyrius build src -o out` silently creates a file literally named `-o` and ignores `out`. See CHANGELOG [6.4.51]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["output_buf", "alloc", "macos", "windows", "overcommit", "mmap-hint", "platform-adaptive", "_output_cap", "cyrius-build-uses-installed-cycc", "cyrius-build-positional-output", "multiple-output-caps", "v6.4.51"]

[[entries]]
name = "signal_ignore_sigpipe_macho_esysxlat_rt_sigaction_to_bsd_sigaction_46_socketpair_test_per_arch_nums"
description = "v6.4.51 added `signal_ignore(signum)` (lib/syscalls.cyr) so a server on `sock_send` survives a peer disconnect (SIGPIPE) instead of being killed — the sandhi macOS DoS gap. ONE fn, target-gated by #ifdef (exactly one branch survives per compile, so the #ifdef-blind LSP wrongly warns 'duplicate fn' — ignore it; grep confirms one def): Linux rt_sigaction (x86 #13 / aarch64 #134, 4-arg incl. sigsetsize=8), macOS BSD sigaction (3-arg), Win/agnos no-op. The 32-byte {sa_handler=SIG_IGN,0,0,0} struct is valid for BOTH the Linux and BSD sigaction ABIs. MACOS is the whole point (Linux had sandhi's raw-syscall workaround; macOS had none): it needed the Mach-O ESYSXLAT to translate rt_sigaction → BSD sigaction 46 — aarch64 `cmp x8,#134`(0xF102191F)/`movz x16,#46`(0xD28005D0, llvm-mc-verified) + THE PARALLEL `_macho_arm_routes` MIRROR LIST (add 134 there too or parse_expr's macho whitelist warning drifts), and x86 `_msx(S, 13, 0x200002E)`. cycc doesn't include syscalls.cyr so the lib part is self-host-neutral; the ESYSXLAT entries are macho-backend-only (x86-Linux differential 0). VERIFIED ON REAL ecb via tests/tcyr/vr01_signal_ignore.tcyr: socketpair → close one end → write → assert EPIPE (not SIGPIPE-kill). TEST-WRITING TRAP that cost a cross-OS round-trip: a vr01 test that raw-`syscall(53/3/1,...)`s HARDCODES x86 numbers and SIGSEGVs on aarch64/macOS (53 = fchmodat on the aarch64 enum) — use the PER-ARCH `SYS_SOCKETPAIR`/`SYS_CLOSE`/`SYS_WRITE` constants (x86 53/3/1, aarch64 199/57/64, macOS via ESYSXLAT) so the numbers are right on every target; qemu-aarch64 catches the aarch64 case locally before the pi leg. Also: a .tcyr must end `var ec = main(); syscall(60, ec);` (explicit exit; cyrius auto-main otherwise returns a GARBAGE exit code) AND include assert+alloc+string+fmt+syscalls (assert alone → 'undefined main'). See CHANGELOG [6.4.51], issue 2026-07-11-sandhi-signal-ignore-stdlib-gap.md."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["signal_ignore", "sigpipe", "sigaction", "esysxlat", "macho", "_macho_arm_routes", "rt_sigaction", "per-arch-syscall-nums", "vr01-test", "tcyr-explicit-exit", "duplicate-fn-lsp-false-positive", "v6.4.51"]

[[entries]]
name = "macos_windows_large_alloc_path_dedicated_unhinted_mmap_and_virtualalloc_output_buf_1gib_all_platforms_v6452"
description = "v6.4.52 SOLVED the macOS/Windows allocator wall from v6.4.51 (see the sibling note about alloc(1 GiB) failing there): give each allocator a dedicated LARGE-object path, mirroring Linux's fresh-chunk-per-request. macOS (lib/alloc_macos.cyr alloc()): for a request that won't fit the remaining 256 MiB reserve (`if (_heap_ptr + size > _heap_end)`), do a dedicated UNHINTED `syscall(9, 0, size, 3, 0x1002, 0-1, 0)` (mmap(NULL,size,RW,MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANON)) and RETURN IT DIRECTLY — bump pointer untouched, no contiguity needed. This REPLACES the old hinted 1 MiB grow loop, which could never succeed on macOS (macOS ignores mmap address hints without MAP_FIXED, so the grow returned a non-hint base and the contiguity guard `result != _heap_end` always failed). macOS overcommits anonymous mmap (proven: alloc_init already unhinted-mmaps the 256 MiB reserve, and main_aarch64_macho.cyr unhinted-mmaps its ~94 MiB heap, both self-host on ecb every release), so a 1 GiB mapping costs only touched pages. NO ESYSXLAT change needed — syscall(9) already translates 9→BSD mmap 197 (aarch64 emit.cyr) / 0x20000C5 (x86 EMACHO_SYSXLAT). Windows (lib/alloc_windows.cyr alloc()): replace the no-grow bail `if (new_ptr > _heap_end) return 0` with a dedicated `syscall(9, 0, size, 3, 0x22, 0-1, 0)` which the PE backend reroutes via EMMAP_PE (parse_expr.cyr literal-syscall(9) path -> src/backend/x86/emit.cyr:1879) to VirtualAlloc(NULL,size,MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE,PAGE_READWRITE). EAGER-COMMIT CAVEAT: EMMAP_PE hardcodes `mov r8d,0x3000` (MEM_COMMIT|MEM_RESERVE) — there is NO MEM_RESERVE-only variant in the backend — so a 1 GiB VirtualAlloc charges the full 1 GiB against the Windows commit limit (RAM+pagefile) UP FRONT (physical RAM still lazy/demand-zero). This SUCCEEDS on a normal dev box (verified on cass; cass's own ~116 MiB heap at main_win.cyr:351 commits up front every release) but a reserve-only + commit-on-touch path would need a NEW codegen path (a fault/commit-on-write seam) — deferred. alloc_reset SAFE on both: dedicated large mappings live outside the bump [_heap_base,_heap_ptr) region, are fresh kernel-zeroed, never recycled (no reuse info-leak). Then the 6 native drivers drop the .51 `if (_output_base == 0) { ...S+0x4D9D000; _output_cap=16777216 }` fallback: `_output_base = alloc(1073741824); if (_output_base == 0) { error+exit }; _output_cap = 1073741824;` — a DEFENSIVE GUARD (hard-error on true OOM, NOT a blind assign that SIGSEGVs writing to page 0). _output_cap stays the single source of truth for all 4 output caps. VERIFY: tests/tcyr/vr01_alloc_1gib.tcyr (alloc 1 GiB + touch both ends, per-arch SYS_* not raw nums) in the cross-OS vr01_ glob — must PASS on real ecb + cass + pi, not just self-host. This is a LIB+driver change so cycc x86-Linux codegen is differential-0, but cycc INCLUDES the driver so its size shifts (+48 B). See CHANGELOG [6.4.52], issue archived/2026-07-11-macos-windows-large-single-allocation-path.md."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["alloc", "macos", "windows", "large-allocation", "unhinted-mmap", "virtualalloc", "mem-commit", "eager-commit", "emmap-pe", "output_buf", "1gib-all-platforms", "alloc-reset-safe", "vr01_alloc_1gib", "v6.4.52"]
